window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['metaphysik-k3/kemp'] = {"metaphysik-k3-s01-001":["1.) Metaphysics belongs to the material part of philosophy, or rather comprehends that part under itself. Since it presupposes actual objects, it rests upon laws, that is, upon grounds of knowledge (*principia*) of, and concerning, whatever belongs to the existence of things. From it, therefore, the merely formal part of philosophy is separated off, namely the laws of thought expounded in logic, since these abstract from the objects themselves. Metaphysics is already distinguished by this from mathematics. Mathematics rests, not as philosophy does, upon laws of the knowledge of things, but upon concepts of things that have been made, through the construction of them.",["Restated the closing sentence slightly for clarity; 'gemachte Begriffe' rendered 'concepts ... that have been made'.","'Erkenntniß Gründen' rendered 'grounds of knowledge'; 'deßen, und über dasjenige' is awkward in the German and kept close ('of, and concerning')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-002":["Metaphysical modes of knowledge must therefore be throughout rational knowledge, arising *a priori* through pure concepts of reason. That is, the *principia* or grounds of knowledge are so constituted that the necessity of what is known is connected with the knowledge itself. The concepts, moreover, are directed to objects which are not only known independently of all experience, but which also can never, in any possible way, become objects of experience. For example, God, freedom, immortality. These therefore differ *e diametro* from all empirical appearances and from the principles derived from them. Metaphysics accordingly has no *principia a posteriori*, but only *a priori* principles. These are given, and are known through reason alone; they are not made.",["Divided the long second sentence into several; 'Erkenntniße' as 'modes of knowledge'.","'ZE.' read as 'z. E.' (for example); 'Erscheinungen' = appearances."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-003":["In general, then, metaphysics is the system of pure philosophy. To express its specific difference from all empirical modes of knowledge, and to separate it from pure mathematics, metaphysics is the system of pure rational modes of knowledge through concepts. Its objects are the material part of philosophy, namely physics and ethics, that is, the laws of nature and of freedom. It is therefore divided into",["'Vernunft-Erkenntniße' rendered 'rational modes of knowledge'. The passage breaks off at a division continued in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-004":["a.) Metaphysics of nature, that is, knowledge of the constitution of objects *ex principiis a priori*.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-005":["b.) Metaphysics of morals, that is, knowledge of the rules or determining grounds of our actions *ex principiis a priori*.",["'Bestimmungs Gründe' = determining grounds."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-006":["It is also called *physica generalis*, because it is known only from *principia a priori*. Knowledge of nature, or the doctrine of nature in general, is namely either doctrine of body, which is called doctrine of nature *in specie* in so far as nature is an object of outer sense, or doctrine of soul, in so far as nature is an object of inner sense, and thereby abstracts from all bodily changes.",["'Sie' in 'insofern sie ein Gegenstand' taken as 'nature' (supplied for clarity)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-007":["In this respect, however, it is entirely different from an empirical doctrine of morals or of nature.",["OCR 'Sittenoder' corrected to 'Sitten oder'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-008":["Here the rules of knowledge are determined from experience, according to the appearances which morals or nature have furnished, in order to derive *principia* from them. For example, the laws of the police are laws of morals, but pragmatic, or rather practical, determining the laws of security and of the comfort of life. Again, the doctrine of nature of the chemists: its *principia* are plainly built upon experience and known from it. So too the doctrine of nature can be known *ex principiis a posteriori*. But metaphysical knowledge is entirely different from all these kinds of knowledge. It is not concerned at all with objects as they are, since, if metaphysics were built upon that, the necessity [of its propositions] would not be seen, as it would be a matter of experience. Just as little does it take as its ground the intuitions *a priori* from which mathematics alone builds its rational modes of knowledge, namely space and time. The author's definition (§ 1), which is taken from Wolff, is mistaken in demanding *prima principia cognitionis* for metaphysics. For that can only mean the first concepts in the abstraction of them, hence in the series of concepts. On this view metaphysics would be occupied only with the concept of a thing, since this is the highest concept of reason, and would then have to descend. In the descent, however, neither beginning nor end could be determined, nor which concept is to be the first or last. Every empirical concept would then become connected with it, which is erroneous, since such a concept lies outside the limits of metaphysics. For example, *ens*, substance, composition; *corpus*, on the other hand, is already an empirical concept in space.",["OCR '\\\\ olf' read as 'Wolff'; 'hegt' read as 'liegt'.","'Noth wendigkeit' joined; the clause 'da dadurch die Nothwendigkeit der Erfahrung ... nicht eingesehen werden würde' is obscure; I supplied '[of its propositions]' and a gloss, a doubtful resolution.","'Verfasser' = Baumgarten, the author of the textbook (identification by context); 'Sitten Gesetze' for laws of morals."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-009":["It would be as if one were to divide history by periods of time: one could then, for example, extend ancient history to the first century, or down to the present day.",["'lste Saeculum' is an OCR rendering, probably '1ste'; read as 'first century'. Doubtful."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-010":["The concept of 'first laws' is therefore too indeterminate, since all pure rational modes of knowledge through concepts belong here, without one's being able to call them the first.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-011":["2.) According to the meaning of the word, metaphysics is a science of nature which limits itself to the rational part, in so far as nature can be known without experience. Meta-physics is physics going beyond the empirical knowledge of nature. Here a great field was expected, without its being possible to determine it, or to determine its boundaries. Three objects were encountered here, which lay beyond the limits of knowledge of nature, and were discovered and known solely *a priori*, or through human reason alone. These are",["'Wortverstande' rendered 'meaning of the word'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-012":["1.) God, that is, the first beginning of all things.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-013":["2.) Freedom, that is, a faculty of man, independent of all influence of nature, of acting in accordance with reason, in opposition to all sensible incentives and forces of nature.",["'Vermögen' = faculty."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-014":["3.) Immortality, that is, the object of the investigation of the understanding, namely how far the soul, as a being of its own, will survive the physical man.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-015":["All three are pure concepts of reason, which absolutely cannot be exhibited in appearance, and which can therefore only be thought. They may accordingly be entitled supersensible objects, *noumena*, that is, objects of the understanding, and may be opposed to the *phaenomena*.",["'darstellen' = exhibit; 'Gegenstände des Verstandes' = objects of the understanding."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-016":["The attempt to investigate these objects more closely was the origin of metaphysics. Chance, then, was not the ground of its origin. It is alleged that Aristotle, the founder of metaphysics, had no intention of writing a system of pure philosophical modes of knowledge lying beyond physics, and indeed did not write one, but merely gave orders that something be attached *meta physicam*, i.e. *post physicam*. This is as empty a tale as that about the origin of the term 'philosophy', according to which Pythagoras would not call himself a *sophos*, but only a *philosophos*. It lay rather in the nature of things themselves that men should endeavour to develop these supersensible objects more closely. What is striking is only that man (and this is innate in every man) took, and still takes, an interest in them. For metaphysics contributes nothing to the extension of the empirical *principia*, that is, of the science of empirical physics. Knowledge of it is quite unnecessary in respect of physics, since the *principia* of metaphysics are wholly set aside and we proceed from established appearances; the principles derived from these are sufficient to explain everything that is to be explained from them. Experience confirms, e.g., the rational propositions: in all alterations substance never perishes, but only the form of things; or: every alteration has its cause. It confirms them so fully that we simply assume them without inquiring into their ground, and are already made certain of their truth, in all circumstances, through experience. On the other hand, it is certain that all our sensible modes of knowledge are only sensibly conditioned, and therefore, like the things themselves, changeable and only contingently certain. Herein lies the ground why man, so far as he strives to know and to attain his *summum bonum*, i.e. the highest end of all his ends (the highest degree of worthiness to be happy, combined with the greatest morality), finds no satisfaction for his reason in this sensible knowledge. This object of his endeavour lies beyond nature. He cannot find all his empirical knowledge sufficient for it; he must find it through reason alone, in reason's laws. He feels it to be necessary that this should be the highest end and vocation [*Bestimmung*] for reason alone, whether he directs his inquiry, e.g., to the determination of duty and right, to the recompense of his actions in that other life, to the determination of his own nature, and so on. Herein lies the ground why metaphysics must absolutely be cultivated: otherwise the whole end of all modes of knowledge of theoretical and practical reason cannot be fulfilled. Hence it is also certain that every man occupies himself with a metaphysics of his own, and that there is properly no doubt *an metaphysica enucleanda sit*; what matters is the method by which it is treated. Hence all despisers of the metaphysical sciences punish themselves, in that they despise the science itself and yet prize their own method of thinking about supersensible truths. In short, without metaphysics no man can exist.",["Restated in shorter sentences in places; 'Nicht war also der Zufall' rendered 'Chance, then, was not'. 'Es ist dies so leere Erzählung' smoothed into 'This is as empty a tale'.","'Man giebt vor' rendered 'It is alleged'; 'Pythagoras ... nicht sophon, sondern nur einen philo-sophon' rendered with Greek transliterated and in italics.","Supplied 'sensible knowledge' (the antecedent of 'darin/dabei') and 'that other life' for 'jenes Leben' by judgment; 'dabei' is ambiguous in the German.","'Bestimmung' rendered 'vocation' in 'Zweck und Bestimmung' and given in brackets; 'Bestimmung' of duty/right/self rendered 'determination'.","'Kenntniß' rendered 'knowledge', 'Erkenntniße' 'modes of knowledge'. 'ausgemachten Erscheinungen' rendered 'established appearances'.","'zufällig-gewiß' rendered 'only contingently certain'. 'an metaphysica enucleanda sit' left in Latin ('whether metaphysics is to be unfolded'). 'findt', 'Haase' etc. are spelling variants, silently normalised."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-017":["3.) The critique of pure reason is the propaedeutic to transcendental philosophy.",["OCR '( ritic' read as 'Critic' and 'transzendentalph ilosophie' as 'transzendentalphilosophie'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-018":["A science that occupies itself with the possibility of having insight into a knowledge *a priori* is entitled the critique of pure reason. All modes of knowledge of reason that are thought *a priori*, independently of all experience, belong here, whether they concern mathematical or philosophical objects, e.g. every alteration must have its cause.",["'Man nennt nemlich' rendered 'is entitled'; 'nemlich' left untranslated as a mere particle of explanation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-019":["Knowing reason here stands as subject, in respect of its capacity, before the judgment-seat of examination, to determine how far it can concern itself with modes of knowledge of this kind. The critique of pure reason is in this regard to be regarded as a higher logic, since it puts into reason's hands rules for how it is to know objects *a priori*; and these rules are nothing but *principia a priori*. It is therefore distinguished from logic itself in that logic abstracts from all objects and states the rules of thinking in general. It is entitled critique because it does not proceed dogmatically, but engages in the investigation of errors and in the testing of our presumptuous judgments. *In specie*, therefore, that philosophy which occupies itself with the possibility of modes of knowledge *a priori* in our reason is the critique of pure reason; but the system of those modes of knowledge themselves, which contain the *elementa* of pure reason, is transcendental philosophy. It differs from critique in this, that critique investigates the possibility of knowing *a priori*, whereas transcendental philosophy has in view the objective sum-total of all those modes of knowledge which are known *a priori* and are regarded as *elementa*, or first *principia*, of our knowledge. Transcendental philosophy is also called ontology, and it is the product of the critique of pure reason.",["Divided the opening long sentence and the 'Sie heißt Critic' sentence; 'Vermögen' rendered 'capacity'. 'Richterstuhl' rendered 'judgment-seat'.","'Die ... die critic ... ist' : in the first sentence the German comma-splice was split into two sentences. 'sie' in 'sie ist also von der Logic' resolved as the critique.","'angemaaßten Urtheile' rendered 'presumptuous judgments' (i.e. judgments arrogated without warrant). 'Philososphie' silently corrected."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-020":["4.) All concepts are acquired, but not all from the senses. The capacity, on the other hand, to acquire concepts by the development of marks is innate. E.g. the hare, when pursued, seeks to outwit the dog; the dog learns by practice the hare's artifices and seeks to get the better of it. Here, of course, no concepts are to be assumed, since animals have no faculty of reason; but instinct, through the many similar cases, forms an experience which serves the dog as a guide. Concepts, indeed, presuppose already representations of objects, and are abstracted from marks; they cannot therefore be innate, but are acquired, or made. This cannot, however, happen otherwise than through the power of the soul that operates in this.",["'Merkmale' rendered 'marks' (Kant's Latin *notae*). 'Vermögen' rendered 'capacity' in the first case, 'faculty of reason' for *Vernunft Vermögen*, and 'power' for the soul.","'so hiebei operirt' rendered 'that operates in this' ('so' = 'welches')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-021":["Yet in all this there was a great problem:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-022":["whether many concepts are innate in us, or whether all concepts, *sive a priori sive a posteriori*, are acquired.",["OCR 'sive I a priori' read as 'sive a priori'; the stray 'I' is probably a misreading and has been dropped. Please check against the Academy text."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-023":["Plato assumed *ideas connatas*, Aristotle on the other hand the *principium*: *nihil est in intellectu, quod antea non fuerit in sensu*, and maintained, in accordance with the sense of this principle, that we acquire all concepts through the senses. Plato, it is true, also admitted sensible representations, but he separated this source of knowledge, as uncertain, from the pure concepts of the understanding, as the *ideae connatae*, which alone he held to be certain. Plato maintained, namely, that concepts *a priori*, or pure concepts of reason, are produced simply out of the fund of reason, and that for consciousness of them it is not necessary to call to one's aid objects of the senses, or to presuppose experience in their origin. For he was struck with astonishment by the mathematical truths, with their so fruitful consequences and irrefutable certainty, e.g. the propositions drawn from the figure of the circle in *mathesi pura*.",["Kant's/transcript's Latin kept in italics: *ideas connatas* (accusative, as written) and *ideis connatis* rendered *ideae connatae* for the English nominative; 'Bewußtseyn' rendered 'consciousness'.","'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'. 'fond' rendered 'fund'. OCR 'Vernnuft' silently corrected. 'Es setzten ihn ... in Erstaunen' smoothed to 'he was struck with astonishment'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-024":["In the circle all intersecting lines yield correct relations of lines, angles and triangles, e.g.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-025":["a : b : c = b : d : e",["Formula given as in the source; the OCR may have disturbed the letters, and the original diagram is not reproduced."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-026":["He assumed that the human understanding had not discovered these concepts and propositions through sensible perception, but that in man there is present a supersensible faculty of knowledge, that is, a *noumenon*, in which modes of knowledge could form themselves purely abstracted, without calling the senses to their aid. This faculty, and the knowledge by means of it, we may at least call supersensible. Only his hypothesis was mystical. For he laid at the foundation a pure intuition of the non-sensible, or, what is the same, a supersensible intuition, and assumed that the soul, before it came into its bodily constitution, had the capacity for intuition of the Godhead; and that, although in this life it no longer shares in it, a consciousness of those pure ideas of the understanding can nevertheless be awakened in man, and that this consciousness is the source of knowledge *a priori*.",["'so sich Erkenntniße rein abgezogen bilden konnten' is awkward, perhaps corrupt or an OCR/transcript slip (e.g. 'rein abgezogene'); rendered literally, with 'in which' supplied for 'so'.","'des nicht sinnlichen oder = eine übersinnliche Anschauung' rendered 'or, what is the same'; the '=' sign is a shorthand in the transcript.","'Verfaßung' rendered 'constitution'; 'Vermögen zur Anschauung' rendered 'capacity for intuition'. 'Gottheit' rendered 'Godhead'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-027":["Aristotle, on the other hand, assumed that by reason alone we could attain no concepts whatever. He denied the *ideae connatae* and maintained, on the contrary, that all our concepts have their source solely in sensible *receptio* [reception], and are therefore acquired through experience. He denied the *ideae a priori* and admitted only *sensualia*. Hence the celebrated dispute over the certainty of our knowledge.",["OCR \"Erd fahrung\" corrected to \"Erfahrung\"; \"Gewisheit\" rendered \"certainty\".","\"reception\" is left as Kant's Latin word, with the English supplied in square brackets.","The Latin terms *ideae connatae*, *ideae a priori*, *sensualia* are kept in italics; the transcript's \"ideas connatas\" and \"ideas a priori\" are accusative/irregular forms, normalised here."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-028":["The defenders of the *noumenon* (or of pure concepts of the understanding, that is, the view that through the pure faculty of reason we are in a position to obtain, without experience, concepts which are called innate) asserted: *phaenomenorum s. sensibilium non datur scientia* [of appearances, or of the sensible, there is no science]. (That is to say, the senses offer no material for the truth or certainty of knowledge. For all modes of knowledge *a priori* whose object is possible are entitled *phaenomena*; *noumena*, on the other hand, are modes of knowledge, or transcendent [überschwengliche] concepts, which have no object in possible intuition.) The defenders of the *phaenomena*, on the other hand, rejected the pure concepts of the understanding: there is, they said, certainty and truth only in outer things.",["The parenthesis is kept, though the definition of *phaenomena* as all knowledge *a priori* whose object is possible is odd (one expects 'all knowledge whose object can be given'); it is probably a slip by the student, and I have translated as it stands.","\"Erkenntniße\" in the plural rendered \"modes of knowledge\"; \"überschwenglich\" as \"transcendent\" (literally 'overflowing', hence beyond experience).","I supplied \"they said\" and the bracketed English of the Latin tag; the sentence is a restatement of indirect speech in the German (subjunctive \"gebe\")."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-029":["Epicurus admitted concepts only in so far as their objects could be visibly presented; hence he also flatly denied a deity.",["\"Epicur\" rendered \"Epicurus\"; \"geradehin\" as \"flatly\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-030":["The sceptics admitted certainty, and existence, neither in the case of modes of knowledge of the understanding nor in the case of empirical modes of knowledge.",["The German is awkward (\"eine Gewisheit, und die existenz\"); I have kept the sense that the sceptics granted neither certainty nor existence in either domain."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-031":["In dealing with the question *an ideae connatae existant* [whether innate ideas exist], we must distinguish between the *modus acquirendi* and the *modus habendi* [the mode of acquiring and the mode of having]. All knowledge begins with experience, and all rational knowledge also can be obtained in no other way than from experience. But it does not follow that all the knowledge we possess is derived immediately from experience. The manner in which modes of knowledge are present in us, that is, the *modus habendi*, can yield modes of knowledge which are grounded only in reason, abstracted from all experience. Something similar lies in the derivation of the *conceptus originarii* and *derivativi*. Both, like every concept, are by their nature *factitii* [made]; but it is true that the *originarii* are grounded, in respect of their origin, not upon experience but solely upon reason.",["\"Alle Erkenntniß fängt von der Erfahrung an\" echoes the opening of the *Critique*'s Introduction; I keep \"begins with experience\".","\"abstrahirt von aller Erfahrung\" is ambiguous (grounded in reason, or in reason as abstracted from experience); I take it as qualifying the knowledge as independent of experience.","The transcript's \"Conceptus originarii — derivativi —\" has dashes where words appear to be lost; I have supplied \"and\" and left the dashes out."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-032":["To define the foregoing more precisely and to elucidate it, the following is certain:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-033":["1.) In man all representations and concepts begin at the outset with objects of experience. This means no more than that our faculty of knowledge, in order to obtain modes of knowledge, even concepts of the understanding, must be awakened by objects of experience, and the receptive faculty of the senses must be set in activity.",["\"receptions Vermögen\" rendered \"receptive faculty\" (cf. Empfänglichkeit); \"Tätigkeit\" as \"activity\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-034":["2.) All concepts are acquired, and there can be no *idea connata*. For concepts presuppose a thinking; they are made, or thought, through apprehension of marks and abstraction of the universal. Thoughts therefore arise through an *operatio mentis* [operation of the mind] which must previously be undertaken.",["\"Merkmale\" rendered \"marks\"; \"das allgemeine\" as \"the universal\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-035":["3.) Notwithstanding this, there are concepts *a priori*, there are *intuitus a priori*, there are propositions and judgments *a priori*. Thus the concept of cause and of causality, that is, of the property of an object of being a cause, is a *conceptus a priori*. The representation of space is a concept of something which may not be derived from experience, nor arises from it; on the contrary, we must grasp the representation of a space before we think an object within it. This representation is grounded on an intuition *a priori*, which precedes all experience. The proposition and judgment *ex nihilo nihil fit*, or, in all alterations nothing [substantial] arises or passes away, is a judgment *a priori*. The criterion of all concepts *a priori*, on the side of their source, lies in this, that they derive their origin not from experience but from the faculty of knowledge of reason itself. The whole attention therefore rests solely on inquiring in what manner we come to be capable of knowing *a priori*; and this, together with the limits of knowledge, is taught by the Critique of Pure Reason. Moreover, in the acquisition of concepts *a priori* it makes no difference that we apply our faculty of knowing to objects of experience; for it does not follow from this that the acquired concepts must derive from experience, and not solely from the faculty of reason.",["\"Verunft\" in the German is an OCR/transcript slip for \"Vernunft\".","I restated nothing but divided no sentences beyond the original; the long final sentences are kept as they stand.","\"(substantielles)\" is put in square brackets as the transcriber's own parenthesis, rendered \"[substantial]\".","\"Caussalitet\" is the transcript's spelling; \"Critic\" rendered \"Critique\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-036":["4.) The manner of acquiring concepts *a priori* may be conceived in two ways, analogically to natural law [Natur-Recht].",["\"analogice wie im Natur Recht\" alludes to the jurists' distinction between *acquisitio originaria* and *derivativa*; hence the following headings."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-037":["a.) *Originarie*, that is, in so far as the concepts *a priori* are taken solely from the nature of the faculty of knowledge, or from the faculty of thinking. For example, the representation of space. These concepts have so little arisen through experience that they are, on the contrary, present before all experience, without our being able, however, to call them *ideae connatae*. Perhaps the concept of space in general might belong to the latter.",["\"zu letztem\" taken to mean the *ideae connatae*, the last-named term; the sense is doubtful."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-038":["If, besides the capacity to know, there is still",["The passage breaks off, continuing in the next fragment (b)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-039":["b.) something empirical mingled in, so that the concept is not pure, then it is *derivativus*.",["Continues the previous fragment."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-040":["5.) Just as it is certain that there could hardly exist any mode of knowledge for which, although it has taken its origin from experience, there should not be a corresponding object in the latter, through which the concept could be presented, so modes of knowledge *a priori* are divided into (a) sensible modes of knowledge, *vel phaenomena* [or appearances], that is, all modes of knowledge *a priori* which can be presented and applied in objects of the senses; from these we pass to (b) *noumena*, or supersensible modes of knowledge, pure modes of knowledge of the understanding, whose object cannot be given in experience or presented to the senses.",["The first clause is obscure in the German (\"davon nicht ... ein correspondirender Gegenstand ... sollte\"); I have resolved the double negative as best the argument allows, and the reading is doubtful.","\"phaenomena\" is rendered \"appearances\" in the bracket, though Kant's Latin is kept in the text.","\"Gegenstand\" rendered \"object\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-041":["6.) From the diversity of the sources from which modes of knowledge were originally derived there arose the difference between (a) Empiricism, that is, the principle of regarding each and every concept, in respect of its origin, as derived from experience. Epicurus is accused of this principle, at least so far as is evident from the works of his disciples, since we possess nothing from him himself. He assumed that no certainty can be ascribed to a concept whose object cannot be shown in experience and thereby confirmed. It does not follow from this, in itself, that he disputed all concepts *a priori*, since for very many of them the proof is found in experience. A consequence of this principle is that metaphysics must be wholly rejected, as Epicurus too is accused of doing. For metaphysics must rest absolutely upon pure concepts of reason, and can therefore never, as regards its source, derive them from experience. The error of empiricism accordingly rests on this: that because our faculty of knowledge can be set in activity only through objects of experience, it infers from this that no concept can exist or be made which did not have its origin in experience. (b) Rationalism, the principle of the possibility of representing to oneself modes of knowledge *a priori*. This principle is the first proposition of all metaphysical truths.",["\"Ur-Sprunge\" rendered \"origin\"; the sentence on Epicurus's \"Beweis in der Erfahrung\" is kept close to the German.","The definition of rationalism (\"dem princip der Möglichkeit ...\") is grammatically irregular; I have smoothed it to a nominal phrase in apposition.","I divided the long final sentence on the error of empiricism from the one before it only by the existing full stops; no further restatement."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-042":["Between these two principles, empiricism and rationalism, there stood in a certain way, in the middle, (c) Mysticism, or the presupposition of an *intellectus intuitivus* or *intuitus intellectualis* [intuitive understanding, intellectual intuition], that is, the possibility that *conceptus a priori mere intellectuales* [concepts *a priori* that are merely intellectual] rest on immediate intuition of the understanding. This mystical hypothesis therefore assumed that the understanding could operate like the senses and have pure intuitions. But the capacity of intuition cannot be ascribed to the understanding; it belongs rather to the senses alone, and so this hypothesis of Plato collapses of itself. It is said, indeed, that the understanding of God is pure intuition; but these are words without a concept, for we men at least cannot form a concept of an intuiting understanding. By them, probably, all that was meant was to bring the operation of the divine being into relation with the thinking power of man; but not even in the case of God can an analogous thinking be assumed.",["I have divided the German's final long sentence into several, as the method permits; the content is unchanged.","\"Verstände\" in \"dem Verstände\" is the dative; \"Vermögen der Anschauung\" rendered \"capacity of intuition\".","The bracketed English for *intellectus intuitivus* etc. is supplied by me."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-043":["Plato's principle is that men, by virtue of a faculty of an intuitive understanding which they once possessed, still have the power of recalling through their understanding the concepts they then had. This rests, it would seem, on an error into which he was led by the admirable clarity and astonishing fruitfulness of many mathematical propositions, in which he was a master. The error consisted in his taking *intuitus a priori puri* and *conceptus a priori puri* to be one and the same.",["\"besehenen\" is an OCR slip; I read \"besessenen\" (possessed).","Restated as two sentences; the German is a single long sentence with an inserted \"daß\"-clause."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-044":["Aristotle improved on him to the extent that he also admitted *intellectualia*, but [did so] at the same time *per conceptus*, and rejected the *ideae connatae ex intuitu puro*. Yet his principle, *nihil est in intellectu*, etc., also sufficiently shows his error. By virtue of it his *conceptus* were not intellectual but *sensitivi*, apprehended through the senses and given universal form by the understanding, and hence empirical, e.g. the concept of warmth.",["\"aber zugleich per conceptus\" is elliptical; I supplied \"[did so]\" in brackets.","\"\\ erstand\" read as \"Verstand\"; \"pp.\" rendered \"etc.\"; \"ideas connatas\" normalised to *ideae connatae*; \"Irthum\" read as \"Irrthum\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-045":["4.) Our faculty of knowledge certainly requires, in respect of supersensible modes of knowledge, a closer examination and determination of its power and limits. Already in the days of the oldest philosophy, of Parmenides, Xenocrates and Pythagoras, when concepts were still clothed in pictorial language, men thought about the development of the same concepts as now. Everyone ponders freedom of the will in action, causality, the beginning of the world, and its origin and author. These objects were regarded as such as must be known a priori, that is, through reason, and it was quite forgotten that in experience there was no object which could possibly correspond to the concepts, judgments and inferences. Then men saw the happy progress which mathematics made when, by virtue of its intuition a priori, it raised itself above the limits of experience into the field of possibility. They concluded that, since this was one kind of modes of knowledge a priori, and since mathematics had succeeded in extending its knowledge beyond sensible objects, one might venture the same in philosophy. And so, in their inquiries concerning supersensible objects, they gave themselves up to a free and unrestricted course.",["Divided the long German passage into several sentences; \"von jedermann gebrütet wird\" is rendered \"Everyone ponders\", with the clause made a separate sentence.","OCR slips corrected silently: \"I hilosophie\", \"Fortschi itte\". \"Erkenntniße\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Mathesi\" as mathematics.","\"sähe\" (for \"sah\") is rendered in the past tense."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-046":["Dogmatism guided the inquirers. That is, it was the principle according to which men believed that, in regard to the metaphysical, i.e. modes of knowledge of reason a priori, they had no need of a critique of reason in order to obtain knowledge a priori and to extend their sensible knowledge beyond the sensible. The fault of dogmatism lay, then, in this: that men did not notice that they were here passing from physical to metaphysical objects (NB, that is, from sensible to supersensible objects); that the latter can have no object in experience by which the truth of the representations, concepts and judgments obtained could be tested; that, for want of an object in intuition, the concepts would therefore have no reality; and that consequently, when the inquiry went further than concepts a priori could lead, empty judgments became possible. Mathematics could not be appealed to. For since it constructs its concepts according to intuitions a priori, and the concepts of its objects must therefore always be capable of being exhibited in experience or intuition, it always has a sure clue by which it does not overstep the limits of reason, and it is certain to discover its errors in the attempts it makes. Not so in philosophy. Here it has to do merely with modes of knowledge of reason from pure concepts, and there is nothing which lays fetters on the understanding in the progress of the inquiry.",["The transcript repeats \"also wegen Mangel des objects in der Anschauung die Begriffe keine\"; I treated the repetition as dittography and translated it once.","Restated the long opening and the \"daß\" series as separate sentences; \"Der Dogmatismus leitete die Untersucher, d. i. derjenige Grundsatz\" is slightly loose in the German, and I made the sense explicit.","\"Ansehnung\" read as \"Ansehung\"; \"Critic\" is \"critique\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-047":["Hence the result was that all the attempts of the philosophers were overthrown by scepticism. Scepticism demonstrated the uncertainty of the product of supersensible modes of knowledge, and now in its turn declared uncertain all modes of knowledge that rest on pure concepts of reason.",["Divided the sentence at the relative clause; \"products\" rendered in the singular as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-048":["Hence dogmatism is the ground of the origin of scepticism. Scepticism also went too far, since concepts a priori really cannot be disputed. Before rejecting the possibility of having such modes of knowledge of reason, it was therefore certainly necessary first to examine the faculty of the understanding, its compass, bounds and limits, and to find a standard, or criteria of truth, by which the genuineness of such pure concepts, to which no object in experience corresponds, could be tested. Only then could doubt be raised with good ground. This is now the business of criticism.",["\"Citeria\" is an OCR or scribal slip for \"Criteria\".","\"Criticißmus\" is rendered \"criticism\"; \"Ächtheit\" as \"genuineness\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-001":["Division of Metaphysics.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-002":["5.) Metaphysics, or the system of pure modes of knowledge of reason, divides into two main sections. I.) Transcendental metaphysics: that part of metaphysics which presents the elementary concepts for knowing a priori objects that can be given. This system of metaphysical knowledge is entitled ontology, and rests on an analysis of reason according to all the elementary concepts contained in it, e.g. magnitude, quality, substance, cause, effect, etc. II.) *Metaphysica propria*: this is what metaphysics is called when it is applied to objects themselves. These objects are",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence and continues in the next passage; translated as it stands.","\"Größe\" rendered \"magnitude\", as the category of quantity; \"Zergliederung\" as \"analysis\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-003":["a.) either sensible, and then",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-004":["1.) the system deals either with objects of inner sense, or of the soul. Hence the doctrine of the soul, *psychologia rationalis*;",["\"betritt\" is probably a slip (or OCR misreading) for \"betrifft\"; I translated \"deals with\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-005":["2.) or with objects of the outer senses. Hence the doctrine of body, *physica rationalis*.",["\"oder objecte\" is carried on from the preceding \"betritt\"; I supplied \"with\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-006":["b.) or objects of mere reason, that is, *ideae* or concepts of mere reason = modes of knowledge whose objects cannot be given through the objects. These are the objects of supersensible knowledge, and these are presented by",["\"ideac\" is an OCR slip for *ideae*.","\"durch die Objecte\" is doubtful; the sense suggests \"durch die Sinne\" or \"durch Erfahrung\". I translated what stands.","The passage breaks off, and the sentence continues in the next passages."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-007":["1.) *cosmologia rationalis*, or cosmology of pure reason, and",["A stray quotation mark after \"und\" was dropped as an OCR artefact."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-008":["2.) *theologia rationalis vel naturalis*, the theology of pure",["Breaks off; continued in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-009":["reason.",["The completion of the preceding sentence: \"…presents the theology of pure reason.\" Verb \"stellt … dar\" is in the earlier passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-001":["History of Metaphysics.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-002":["6.) It is striking that nature calls us to investigate modes of knowledge of reason, and that it is of especially weighty interest to man to become more closely acquainted with the objects of the supersensible; and yet all effort hitherto has borne little fruit, since it is still doubtful what, among metaphysical truths, can and should be accepted as certain. Already in the earliest times of philosophy men occupied themselves with metaphysics sooner than with physics, and directed their inquiry especially to the supersensible rather than to the sensible. We cannot, indeed, state the doctrines and theories of Parmenides, Xenocrates and Pythagoras with clearness, since no writings of their own have remained to us. Only later writers, e.g. Diogenes Laertius and others, have set out their opinions, and have not expounded them with sufficient precision. Yet it is certain from this that they directed their inquiry to the origin of the world, to the cause of the world as the supreme being, to the nature of the human soul, and to its existence after death, or the future life.",["Divided the long sentence on the sources into two sentences.","The final clause lacks a finite verb in the transcript (\"daß sie … die Untersuchung gerichtet\"); I translated \"directed\" as in the past and did not add anything further.","\"es hat … ein wichtiges Interesse\" is rendered with \"it is of … interest\" for the impersonal subject."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-003":["It is certain that men were misled by mathematics into believing they could make happy progress a priori in the investigation of such objects. They believed that, since mathematics, as one kind of modes of knowledge of reason, had succeeded in this, the same would hold in philosophy. They did not consider that mathematics in its investigations built upon intuitions which could be given through the senses, namely space and time, whereas here, where mere concepts of reason without intuition lay at the ground, a like success was not to be expected, at least not on like grounds.",["\"reußiret\" (for \"reussiert\") rendered \"had succeeded\"; the long sentence was restated as three sentences."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-004":["Plato in particular contributed pre-eminently to this error by ascribing to the pure concepts of reason a power of intuition, just as [he ascribed] to the sensible [faculties] a receptive capacity, and therefore derived modes of knowledge of reason here, just as in mathematics, through the construction of intuition a priori.",["\"den sinnlichen receptions Vermögen\" is awkward and possibly corrupt; I have supplied \"[faculties]\" and \"[he ascribed]\" to make a sense, and the reading is doubtful.","\"abieitete\" read as \"ableitete\" (OCR slip)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-005":["So long as the systems built upon this were not challenged, men could go on building. They did not, however, become certain of the truth of their presuppositions and consequences, since it was impossible to test upon objects the modes of knowledge produced by these attempts. The objects could not be given, and a comparison with them therefore did not take place. All judgments and every whole system were accepted as valid, provided only that one remained consistent and did not contradict oneself. But a conflict arose among the philosophers over the propositions asserted as consequences of their systems. One party believed it had clearly proved them to be well grounded; the other party refuted them just as clearly, and showed that the opposite could be as clearly established. This dispute concerned the transcendental propositions, or transcendent [*überschwenglich*] problems in philosophy. For example, it was proved that every space, and bodies, consist of the simple parts of matter. Others denied, just as clearly, the possibility of simple parts, because all composita consist of parts, and these again of parts. Or the proposition: the world has a beginning, for the absolute world-whole is only an idea of reason, whereas the world itself is bounded. Others denied this and asserted that there can be no last boundary of the world, and that it is only we who cannot determine the boundary. — And so on.",["Restated the long sentences as several shorter ones, and set out the examples (simple parts, beginning of the world) as separate sentences; the dashes of the original are not kept.","'hierauf' is taken to refer to the procedure just described in the preceding passage (not in this excerpt); rendered 'upon this'.","'Erkenntniße' = modes of knowledge; 'Versuche' = attempts; 'überschwenglich' = transcendent, German given in brackets."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-006":["As soon as the contradiction, and the existence of wholly conflicting propositions, were equally clear, there arose the party which doubted the certainty of both. This party took occasion from it to declare all truths of reason uncertain, and adopted as a principle that in all our modes of knowledge certainty is lacking. It even contradicted itself, conceding that the very question whether everything is uncertain is itself uncertain. This killed all progress of enquiry, since dogmatism was overthrown and scepticism laid down no *principia* from which one could set out. The interest of mankind suffered thereby, and both opposites rendered no service.",["'ander Gewisheit' read as 'an der Gewißheit' (OCR slip).","Split the original's second sentence at the colon into two sentences."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-007":["It was necessary, and before undertaking investigations upon the supersensible objects themselves one ought to have begun with this:",["The sentence breaks off; the infinitive clause continues in the next passage. The construction 'Es war nöthig, und man hätte … sollen' is loose in the transcript and is rendered as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-008":["to treat reason, as the investigating subject, like an object which must be more closely explored; to investigate its capacity to know *a priori*; and to determine its boundary and extent. Up to the closer critique of pure reason, the principles which have been dominant, and upon which men built, can be classified, according to their kind and form, as follows:",["Continues the previous passage; 'Critic' rendered 'critique'.","'Vermögen' = capacity."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-009":["(a) Aristotle, and later Locke, set up what may be called a physiology of reason, in that they regarded knowledge *a priori* as of a kind that can be acquired empirically. What we raised to a knowledge *a priori* was, on this view, drawn from experience, namely the determinations of things, made universal by generalisation. In regard to origin, therefore, they assumed all knowledge to be sensible (*nihil est* etc., says Aristotle), and believed that the more a proposition is raised aloft by abstraction, the nearer it approaches to a proposition *a priori*. Aristotle and Locke thus adopted the *systema influens physici*, that is, the origin of our representations from the senses and their influence, together with the principle that we arrive at universal modes of knowledge *a priori* only through abstraction. The contradiction is clear. Reason is to supply the knowledge, and yet it is to be acquired through the senses.",["OCR corrected: 'sinnlieh' to 'sinnlich', 'nähere' to 'nähert', 'Erkenn tniß' to 'Erkenntniß'.","The second sentence of the German is syntactically loose ('und was wir … erhoben'); I have smoothed it and restated it.","'Physiologie der Vernunft' kept as Kant's term, with 'so called' ('so genannte') rendered 'what may be called'. 'systema influens physici' left in Latin as in the transcript."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-010":["(b) Plato, and later Leibniz, seem to assume a *systema harmoniae praestabilitae* with regard to the modes of knowledge of reason. They laid at the foundation innate ideas, which were placed in us before we became acquainted with the objects themselves, and which, just because the Author has placed them in us, agree with the objects. Plato assumed, as the source from which all pure ideas of reason are acquired, that they were derived from the intuition of God. Leibniz altered this, in that he assumed certain innate dispositions of reason as existing in us, whose only use is that, relying upon them, we find the objects themselves in agreement with these ideas.",["'Erwerb Quelle' rendered 'source from which … are acquired'.","'Urheber' = Author (i.e. God as creator); 'Anschauung Gottes' = intuition of God."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-011":["Both found themselves, in establishing such hypotheses, taken by surprise through the conviction which truths so often forced upon them with such evidence.",["The sense of 'überrascht' here is somewhat doubtful; the rendering follows the most natural reading, and the clause is rather loose in the transcript."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-012":["(c) Wolff, and, in his old age, his antagonist [?], Crusius. Crusius indeed disputed such a united action of the soul upon the body as Leibniz had assumed by virtue of the *harmonia praestabilita*. He laid down, however, that the criterion of truth is to be sought only in the ideas which the Creator has placed in us, because he could not trust our reason to discover these ideas for itself. He thus assumed an inner revelation in man, and its necessity, in order to bring him to conviction. Wolff, whom he refuted, had not investigated the origin of the ideas at all, but proceeded as a dogmatist. He built his success in philosophy at random upon mathematical presuppositions, and, by applying the mathematical method, confounded the modes of knowledge *a priori* *ex puris ideis* with mathematical knowledge. He believed that he could operate with them, through the construction of concepts from intuition *a priori*, just as in mathematics. First Part of transcendental philosophy, or metaphysics, which contains ontology.",["'Antigon' is doubtful, probably an OCR or transcript slip for 'Antagonist'; translated tentatively with a query.","'aufs gerathe wohl' read as 'aufs Geratewohl' (at random).","The last sentence ('1. Theil …') is a heading of the transcript and is translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-013":["7.) It is striking to think representations and concepts *a priori* whose object itself cannot be given through possible intuition. For example, a spirit is an idea of pure reason whose object absolutely cannot be exhibited. It is indeed true that this concept, and all like it, are, theoretically taken, empty; yet the idea is possible, and is qualified to render practical service. But these ideas, which are transcendent [*überschwenglich*], and the elementary principles through which we know such representations *a priori*, of a supersensible kind, of which, however, no object can be exhibited in intuition, do not belong here either. The subject-matter of ontology, or of transcendental philosophy so far as it is here treated, consists only of those elementary principles of such concepts *a priori* in which the objects themselves can be exhibited or known. These are indeed also entitled transcendent, but they are at the same time immanent, just because the objects of the modes of knowledge are possible.",["The clause 'aber diese Ideen, so überschwengliche sind, und die elementar-principien …' is grammatically broken; I have restated it with 'these ideas, which are transcendent, and the elementary principles … do not belong here'.","'Vorwurf' = subject-matter; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'heißen' = 'is entitled'.","'qualifiziert' rendered 'qualified'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-014":["8.) *ad* Sect. 1, §§ 7–11 of the author",["'auctoris' = Baumgarten (the author of the textbook); the heading is kept in its abbreviated form."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-015":["Of an object as the supreme concept. The supreme concept, under which all the remaining elementary concepts can be subordinated, is the",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-016":["concept of an object in general, which lies at the foundation of representation. The author, on the other hand, holds the concept of possibility to be the first concept, and opposes to it at the same time the concept of nothing. But the representation",["Breaks off in mid-sentence; continued through the following fragments."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-017":["— of the *possibili impossibili*",["The Latin is left as transcribed; the case endings are odd (probably the transcript's abbreviation for 'possible and impossible')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-018":["— a — *a non-a*",["'ein' stands without a noun; the reading is doubtful (OCR or abbreviated notes), so it is rendered literally."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-019":["— a something, a nothing, evidently indicates a division in which the members must be grounded upon a higher concept; and this is the concept of an object in general, which can then be a something or a nothing. It seems striking to think an object which includes a nothing. But a nothing presupposes only a thought which cancels itself (that is, which contradicts itself), and therefore never has an existing object at its foundation.",["Joins the sentence begun in passages 015–018: 'But the representation … of a something, a nothing, evidently indicates a division.'","Restated the second sentence of the German in two shorter sentences."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-020":["The nothing is now either",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-021":["1.) a *nihilum logice tale*, or *logicum*, or *negativum*; that is, one to which no thought or representation corresponds. It is ordinarily so constituted that it involves an inner contradiction in the representation.",["'s.' in the Latin expands to *sive* ('or'). 'ordinair' rendered 'ordinarily'; 'involvirt' rendered 'involves'.","'oder ein solches' is rendered 'that is, one' since it explains the term rather than adding a third alternative."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-022":["2.) A *nihilum privativum* is something that, though it can be thought, has nothing existent corresponding to it. For example, the aether in physics is a fictitious concept, which has no reality *intuitu objecti* [in respect of an object], but which can be thought without contradiction. So likewise a positive cold, as the absolute cause of cold, contains nothing contradictory in being thought, but has no existence. To this class belong all fictions, since they are not to be found in nature *quoad objectum* [as regards the object]. An example is complete contentment, against which existence bears witness in man's constant propensity to raise himself above his condition and to transfer himself into another, which he holds to be better. Thus *nihil negativum* and *nihil privativum* differ from one another as thought and thing: in the one case or the other, [something] does not correspond to the representation. Or",["The long second sentence is divided; the example of contentment is made a separate sentence. Words in square brackets after the Latin are glosses of mine.","'eins oder das andere correspondiren nicht der Vorstellung' is awkward (plural verb, no clear subject). I render 'the one or the other' and supply '[something]'; the sense may be that in one case the thought lacks a thing, in the other the thing lacks a thought.","'nihil negativum et privativum': I repeat *nihil* before *privativum* for clarity.","The passage breaks off at 'Oder'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-023":["3.) A nothing is also a *conceptus inanis*, an empty concept, that is, a concept to which no object can correspond or (more precisely) to which no object can be given in intuition. We must guard against calling such a concept false on that account. We cannot become aware whether a real object can correspond to the empty concept; but it can nevertheless be thought, that is, it does not conflict with the possibility of the mere representation of it. Between what can be thought and that of which the object can at the same time be exhibited, that is, given, there is (in accordance with 1 and 2) a difference. Concepts of this kind occur among the supersensible ideas. For example, the concept of spirit has nothing contradictory in its representation, but whether it is possible that such an immaterial being can exist, we cannot see. In problematic propositions no contradiction is to be found; but in them we do not settle whether the concept can obtain a corresponding object in intuition, whether a case can be placed under it, and whether it can be indicated in existence and applied. Such *conceptus inanes* are not serviceable, but they are not on that account false.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'ei' as 'er', 'Widerspruc' as 'Widerspruch'.","'= dem man kein Object...' (the sign '=' introducing the more exact statement) rendered 'or (more precisely)'.","'er in der existenz angedeutet' is elliptical; I take the subject to be the concept and render 'it can be indicated in existence'.","'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Gegenstand' and 'object' both rendered 'object'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-024":["4.) A merely formal concept is the nothing in so far as the lack of everything material in intuition is found in connection with it. It is therefore only a *nihilum in sensu materiali*, but is certainly a something *in sensu formali*. For example, the concept of space in itself is, as regards its form, a concept that can be known in intuition; but *in materiali* it has no intuition so long as it is thought without thing or matter, and is therefore relatively a nothing. From this it follows that all concepts belong here which lack all empirical intuition. For empirical intuition rests essentially on sensation of the object, and consequently empirical intuition alone yields only empirical concepts.",["'Ein blos formeller Begriff ist das nichts' is a little loose; I read 'das Nichts' as the fourth kind of nothing in Kant's enumeration.","'Empfindung' = sensation; 'Anschauung' = intuition."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-025":["5.) Nothing is entitled impossible when no concept, and not even a thought, rests upon it. This impossible nothing is recognised solely through contradiction: a thought that cancels itself, a concept that falls to pieces through inner contradiction. Meanwhile there may be objects that are impossible in themselves, though we assume them to be possible because the concept of them encounters no contradiction. On the other hand, the thought of an actual or possible object cannot be impossible, in so far as it corresponds to the object.",["The dashes and fragmentary clauses ('Ein Gedanke der sich selbst aufhebt...') are kept as appositions to the preceding sentence.","'heißen' rendered 'is entitled'; 'unmöglich' is a predicate adjective of 'Nichts'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-026":["Hence it may well be accepted as correct:",["Introduces the two propositions that follow in the next passages."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-027":["that everything which contradicts itself is impossible, but not conversely, deny:",["'aber nicht umgekehrt verneinen:' is syntactically unclear; the colon seems to lead into the next passage. I translate literally and do not complete it. Possibly the text is faulty or some word has dropped out."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-028":["everything which does not contradict itself is possible, because otherwise it would have to contradict itself. Thus only impossibility, not possibility, rests upon contradiction.",["Taken with the preceding passage, the sense seems to be that the converse is to be denied or at least treated with care; the transcript is obscure here and I have not smoothed it further."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-029":["All that matters is that I should see the contradiction, and then what I held to be possible becomes impossible. Contradiction is only the means of recognising impossibility. Possibility, however, corresponds with the existence of the object. We can therefore very well think the predicates of a thing without contradiction; but from this the possibility of the thing itself does not yet follow, since the possibility of the thought does not yet involve the possibility of the object in intuition. Hence an empty concept is also possible, so long as there is no inner contradiction. On the other hand, no object is known which corresponds to it, and therefore neither the possibility nor the impossibility of the thing itself yet follows from this.",["'Umnöglichkeit' is an OCR slip for 'Unmöglichkeit', silently corrected.","'Es kommt ja nur darauf an' rendered 'All that matters is'; 'ja' is a weak particle and is not separately rendered."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-030":["6.) *ad eosd. §* [garbled] — The *principium contradictionis*, together with the *principium identitatis*, which is equally dominant, is the supreme principle in respect of all analytic judgments, that is, those judgments in which the concept of the predicate and of the subject can be known through analysis. It is not, however, sufficient for every kind of judgment without exception, for example for synthetic judgments, in which we go beyond the concept of the subject and by the predicate say more than was contained in the subject. The formula under which it is expressed is:",["'ad eosd. § phos' is garbled in the OCR (perhaps 'ad eosdem §§', referring to Baumgarten's sections); I give the Latin as far as legible and leave the rest.","'Zerghederung' is read as 'Zergliederung' (analysis).","'das gleich herrschende' taken as 'equally dominant'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-031":["*nulli subjecto competit praedicatum sibimet ipsi oppositum* [no predicate opposed to the subject itself belongs to any subject]. It is usually defined in this way:",["The bracketed English gloss is mine, supplied for the Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-032":["*quod idem simul esse et non esse potest* [as printed], that is, to no thing can a predicate, and at the same time (*eodem tempore*) the opposite of it, belong. It is easy to see that the determination of a time-relation, that is, of the existence of the thing in time, is indicated here by the word *simul*. According to this, therefore, a contradiction can hold between subject and predicate only under the same condition of time. Consequently, if two predicates are attributed to the same subject, not at the same time but one after the other, it is possible that, although they would contradict one another in the subject *eodem tempore*, each predicate taken alone could nevertheless be attributed to it *diverso tempore*. For example, 'a learned man is not learned': here the contradiction is clear, since both cannot be at once, namely to be learned and unlearned, and consequently the predicate and the subject cancel each other, and conversely. But the learned man can forget [vergeben] what he knew, and then he may well afterwards pass as unlearned. Here, then, are two contradictory determinations with a change of time. So also in the two concepts of the understanding: a man at rest and a man in motion, thought at the same time, contain a contradiction; but with regard to space and time, not at all.",["'quod idem simul esse et non esse potest' is printed without a negative ('non potest'); I follow the text as it stands and flag it as probably faulty or abbreviated.","'eodern' is an OCR slip for 'eodem'; 'cont.radictorische' read as 'contradictorische'; 'vv.' expanded as 'conversely' (*vice versa*).","'vergeben' is doubtful (perhaps 'vergessen' or 'verlieren'); I render 'forget' and flag it.","The sentence beginning 'Es kann also hiernach' is divided into two, and 'Z.E.' sentence made separate.","'Bestimmung' rendered 'determination'; 'Zeit-Verhältnis' as 'time-relation'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-033":["This determination of concepts is called the changeableness of the thing. How this comes about is to be developed below; here we draw only the conclusion that the determination of time does not belong to the formula of contradiction.",["'Veränderlichkeit' rendered 'changeableness'; 'Bestimmung der Begriffe' taken as the successive attribution of opposed predicates at different times, as in the preceding passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-034":["N.B. In addition to the above formula of the *principium contradictionis*, we must certainly also admit the formula: *cuidam subjecto competit praedicatum praedicato sui ipsius oppositum* [a predicate opposed to its own predicate belongs to a certain subject]. For the judgment has, besides subject and predicate, a connecting word as well. If this word indicates merely the copula, the above formula of the *principium contradictionis*, *nulli subjecto competit* etc., holds universally, since the two concepts directly cancel each other. If, however, it also indicates existence determined in time, two *opposita* can occur according to the second formula. For example, 'the learned Caius is unlearned' is a contradiction; but not so 'Caius, who is learned, is unlearned'; for this means that Caius exists at one time as learned, but at another time exists as unlearned. It must therefore be regarded as a fault that a concept of existence has been introduced into the concept of the *principium contradictionis*: *nihil est, quod simul* etc. Besides the negative formula *nulli* etc. just cited, the principle of analytic judgments can also be determined positively: *omni subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum* [to every subject there belongs a predicate identical with it]. It is then entitled the *principium identitatis*. For example, every learned man has knowledge [*Kenntnisse*]; to think the opposite of this is a contradiction, and *Kenntnisse* lie directly in the concept of the learned man. The proposition is therefore equivalent to: a learned man is a learned man.",["'Formul' read as 'Formel'; 'compet.it' as 'competit'; 'Ixenntniße' as 'Kenntniße' (OCR slips).","The Latin formulae are left in Latin, with English supplied in brackets for the two that are not merely quoted in abbreviation.","'Kenntniße' is kept in German to distinguish it from *Erkenntnis* (knowledge); here it means roughly 'items of learning'.","'so können 2 opposita nach der 2ten Formel statt finden' is rendered literally; 'second formula' refers to the *cuidam subjecto* formula."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-035":["In § 11 the author seeks to prove this *principium identitatis* by the *principium contradictionis*. But the proof cannot be carried out, because the *principium contradictionis* can establish the *principium identitatis* only in the same way as the latter is supposed to establish it; and so the *principium contradictionis* would prove itself by itself.",["The sentence is obscure in the German ('ebenso, wie es das principium identitatis beweisen soll, auch darthun kann'); I have restated it as a circularity charge, which seems what the argument requires. The reading is doubtful."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-036":["If one denies that the predicate belongs to the subject, then the *oppositum* would belong to it; and this would not be contradictory, but would only prove that the *idem* was not *idem*, so long as the contradiction had not been proved in and for itself. Both principles are indemonstrable. One can say: what contradicts itself is nothing; [and] this is an affirmative identical proposition. These are the two supreme formal principles of our modes of knowledge. The *principium contradictionis*, however, is more used than the *principium identitatis*. This is because in the *principium contradictionis* there lies the necessity which forces upon us the truth of that which is to be proved. The proof of truth by the impossibility of its opposite is indirect, but it is apodictic and bound up with the concept of necessity, whereas mere truth does not carry this impression with it; yet the *principium identitatis* is always understood along with it.",["The text 'ist nichtsdies ist ein bejahender' is garbled by OCR; I read 'ist nichts [;] dies ist ein bejahender identischer Satz' and supply '[and]'. Doubtful.","'widersrpäche' read as 'widerspräche'; 'hegt' as 'liegt'; 'Beweis' etc. unchanged.","'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge'.","The first sentence is difficult; 'solange, bis…' is rendered literally and the sense is uncertain."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-037":["The author defines the *impossibile* by the *principium contradictionis*. This is false, however, because reciprocity is lacking in the concepts. If it were present, the proposition 'the impossible is what contradicts itself' would have to be convertible *per contrapositionem*, and hence the proposition 'what is not impossible, that is, possible, does not contradict itself' would hold. But, as already mentioned, something may well be possible in the concept which shows itself to be impossible as soon as the contradiction has been perceived; the definition is therefore incorrect. It is only true that the *principium contradictionis* is necessary in order to know the impossibility; but the impossibility is not thereby refuted.",["'den bey den Begriffen' read as 'denn bei den Begriffen'.","'widerlegt wird dadurch die Unmöglichkeit nicht' is rendered literally; Kant's sense may rather be 'the impossibility is not thereby grounded'. Doubtful."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-038":["10.) On the author's §§ 12 and 13: *posito impossibili oritur contradictio* [if the impossible is posited, contradiction arises]. A *contradictio in adjecto* occurs when two concepts attached in one and the same concept run directly counter to each other, or when in a judgment subject and predicate directly contradict each other. The contradiction is *apparens*, as for example a visible darkness, that is, an approach of light to darkness, as Milton says of Hell. [Likewise] visible eclipses of the moon: the negations of light. The contradiction is *patens* (*explicita*), or *latens sive implicita*, according as an analysis of the concepts is required in order to recognize it, or no dissection is needed. In regard to the person judging, however, we must in both cases assume that the contradiction was hidden from him, and it must therefore be proved to him.",["The dash and 'Sichtbare Mondfinsterniße' appear to be a separate jotting; I supply '[Likewise]'.","'fällen' read as 'Fällen'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-039":["11.) On the author's § 10: There are three logical principles of all judgments, which determine the correctness and certainty of the latter. They are logical formulae of our judgments, but not metaphysical principles of objective knowledge. These are",["'letztem' rendered 'the latter'; the reference is to the judgments' certainty and correctness. The passage breaks off, with 'These are' continued by the following passages."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-040":["1.) the *principium contradictionis et identitatis*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-041":["2.) the *principium rationis logicum*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-042":["3.) the *principium exclusi intermedii inter duo contradictoria*, or *principium divisionis*, or *principium disjunctionis*. N.B. Under this are understood all analytic judgments, in that predicates are presupposed which are thought in the concept of the subject, are proved by the ground of knowledge [*Erkenntniß Grund*] of the latter, and whose necessary attribution is demonstrated. The *principium contradictionis* and the *principium identitatis* are the two supreme and universal principles to which all such judgments are subject, the latter governing the affirmative, the former the negative.",["'durch deßen Erkenntniß Grund bewiesen' is obscure; I render 'ground of knowledge' and flag it as a doubtful reading.","'dem letztem… dem erstern' resolved as identity (affirmative) and contradiction (negative)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-043":["All three principles, together with the *principium contradictionis*, come down to a single concept, since one cannot judge without presupposing them; and the *principium contradictionis* contains, so to speak, the other two. For",["The text breaks off ('Denn'); it is continued by the following passages. 'Alle 3 principia… mit dem principium contradictionis' is as in the transcript, which is slightly irregular."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-044":["a.) the *principium contradictionis* is applied in all problematic judgments. Here all that matters is that the predicate in itself does not contradict the subject, for example 'a divisible soul'. There lies in the concept of soul an *oppositum* which cannot unite the presupposition in the judgment or concept with the predicate 'divisible'. If this is so, the judgment is not thinkable at all. Every thought, on the other hand, which does not fall apart in itself accords with this principle, since so far its truth does not yet come in question. This enters",["Passage breaks off at 'Diese tritt', continued by b.)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-045":["b.) with assertoric judgments or propositions. For every proposition must be grounded by virtue of the *principium rationis* to be applied here, which, however, already presupposes that it is thinkable at all; after which, in accordance with this second principle, the predicate must be connected with the subject through a ground of proof.",["'Beweis Grund' rendered 'ground of proof'.","'wonächst' rendered 'after which'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-046":["c.) the *principium disjunctionis* can only make the judgment an apodictic judgment, and give it certainty through the disjunction of the members of the concept. The latter results from every concept being divided into two *opposita*, of which only one member can belong to the subject, and it is proved that the *oppositum* cannot belong to it, just as there can be no third beyond the two members which would at the same time have to be ascribed to it. This is therefore the principle of all logical division of concepts, because these divisions are only dichotomous.",["'Gewisheit' rendered 'certainty'; 'so ihm zugleich zugeschrieben werden müßte' resolved as relating to a hypothetical third member."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-047":["12.) All our knowledge consists of concepts and of intuition.",["'Alles unser Erkenntniß' is as printed; 'Anschauung' rendered 'intuition'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-048":["Intuition gives the concept its objective reality; that is to say, without intuition every concept falls under the suspicion of being empty. If, then, a concept has been thought, but its object cannot be given to it in intuition, we say of it that it lacks objective reality. It is not, however, necessary that the object be presented to the senses; what is required is only that its presentation be possible. Intuition gives the object to be known, and at its basis lies an immediate representation of the object. The marks which have been abstracted through intuition yield, mediately, the representation which the concept contains and through which the object is known. Through the concept the object is thought. Now if I could place no object under the concept, I can indeed think the concept, but this thinking alone yields no knowledge, since objective reality is wanting to it. Intuition must be added, so that the concept may have an object corresponding to it; only then has it objective reality, and only then can it be known. Thus, just as knowledge is impossible without intuition, so little is it acquired through intuition alone. For intuition contains only the immediate relation to the object.",["Restated as several shorter sentences; the German 'es kommt dabey jedoch nicht darauf als nothwendig an' is smoothed to 'It is not, however, necessary'.","'Erkenntniß' = knowledge; 'zu erkennen geben' rendered 'to be known'. 'Darstellung' = presentation; 'sinnlich dargestellt' = presented to the senses.","'könnte ich nun kein object ... unterlegen, so kann ich' is an irregular mood sequence in the transcript; I have rendered it as a plain conditional ('if I could place no object').","OCR 'einschheßt' read as 'einschließt'; 'noth wendig' joined; 'Erkenntniß' after 'kein' left as in the text (neuter, probably 'keine Erkenntniß')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-049":["In knowledge, finally, there is present no immediate relation to the object, but only to the intuition of the object; and it is in the relation of the concept to the intuition that knowledge is acquired. All intuition is sensible. To know anything without it would be, if that were possible, supersensible knowledge, which would also have to rest on a supersensible intuition. All supersensible concepts are of such a kind that no intuition can be given to them. Hence, even if we may otherwise have ground for thinking them, they lack the objective reality that would be directly bound up with the concept; for example, the concept of spirit.",["The German 'wäre ... übersinnliches Erkenntniß, so auch auf ... beruhen müßte' is irregular (a relative 'das' seems intended); I supply 'which would'.","'die directe mit dem Begriff verbunden wäre' rendered 'directly bound up with the concept'; 'directe' may be a transcript slip for 'direct'.","'Geist' rendered 'spirit'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-050":["13.) In order now to set up the principles (*principia*) of transcendental philosophy itself, what first matters is to develop the distinction between analytic and synthetic judgments. 1. Analytic judgments are those in which the predicate, thought as a [predicate of the] subject, can be known through the analysis of the concept. The predicate thus lies implicitly (*implicite*) in the subject, or is contained in the subject; the concept of the subject need only be analysed, that is, explicated, in order to know it. For example, 'A body is divisible' is known through the analysis of extensibility. Analytic judgments must therefore be capable of being thought and known solely in the concept of the subject, and in this they already differ essentially from synthetic judgments. To know the latter through the subject alone, without adding a concept that does not lie in it, is impossible. For example, all mathematical propositions of every kind are synthetic; even in algebra it is impossible to develop a single proposition by analysis.",["'als ein subiect gedacht' is awkward; I supply '[predicate of the]' in brackets as the sense seems to require, but the reading is doubtful.","'Zergliederung der Dehnbarkeit' is as the transcript has it, though one would expect 'des Begriffs des Körpers' (extensibility is presumably the concept analysed); left as written.","'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'; 'explicirt' = 'explicated'. OCR 'subject s' and 'entwicklen' silently corrected."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-051":["The proposition 'All bodies are heavy' will not result from analysis of the concept of body alone; I must first have come to know through experience that a body is heavy before I can ascribe this predicate to it.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-052":["The proposition '7 + 5 gives the magnitude 12' cannot be developed out of the concept of the magnitude 7 + 5 alone by any unfolding of it. The magnitude 12 arises from the other two very easily by synthesis (*per synthesin*), and the proposition is therefore synthetic. That is to say, I have carried out the operation of finding 12 with the two magnitudes through experience in such a way that to one magnitude, for example 7, I have joined and so added the other (5) by a sensible counting-on of the units.",["OCR '7 -f- 5' read as '7 + 5'. 'Zuzählung' rendered 'counting-on'. The wording 'durch die Erfahrung' is as in the transcript.","The first sentence is awkward in German ('wie durch Entwicklung ... allein'); I have smoothed it."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-053":["2. Analytic judgments may be entitled judgments of elucidation (*Erläuterungsurtheile*). Such a judgment serves to make us conscious of the manifold that lies in the concept, which was previously thought obscurely but is now developed. Synthetic judgments, on the other hand, are judgments of amplification (*Erweiterungsurtheile*). They go beyond the concept of the subject and contain a further predicate which was not thought in the concept of the subject. Now the question arises",["OCR 'Erläuter ngsurt heile' corrected to 'Erläuterungsurtheile'. 'dunckel' = obscurely.","The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-054":["3.) how, and which, of the two kinds of judgment are possible *a priori*? The possibility of modes of knowledge *a priori* is clear in analytic judgments, since here the subject need only be developed according to the concept in order to know the predicate. No experience is therefore required to know the predicate contained in it. For example, to know the divisibility of body, only the development of the concept of body is needed; its divisibility is thus found without our first having to make trials about it *a posteriori* and to look for the product investigated from experience.",["'Erkenntniße a priori' rendered 'modes of knowledge'. 'product' (*product*) is as in the transcript; sense obscure ('das aus der Erfahrung erforschte product'), rendered literally.","OCR 'findt' corrected."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-055":["Synthetic judgments, on the other hand, can be judgments *a posteriori*; they can also be judgments known *a priori*. But even *a priori*, reason must always add something that did not lie in the concept, and this cannot be grounded on experience. If the synthetic judgment is to be known *a priori*, this can happen only through an intuition *a priori* which is laid at its basis, namely by an intuition being placed under the concept that is to be extended through synthesis. This takes place either through empirical intuition of the object that is added to the concept, or through intuition *a priori*, that is, formal relation to the object and determination of the form in which the subject is affected by the object; and from this intuition the added object is taken up. From this it follows that synthetic judgments are possible only by means of the corresponding intuition in general and the concept formed from it and added, and that this intuition must take place *a priori* if the judgment is to be framed *a priori*.",["The OCR opening '-^agegen' read as 'Dagegen'.","'auch können sie Urtheile seyn, a priori erkannt' is elliptical; I supply 'known' as the grammar suggests ('[judgments] known *a priori*').","'Bestimmung' = determination; 'afficirt' = affected. 'Gegenstand' in 'der zugesetzte Gegenstand' = 'object'; the sentence is obscure and rendered closely.","'Diesgeschieht' corrected to 'Dies geschieht'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-056":["For example, 'The three angles of a triangle are equal to two right angles of 180°' is a synthetic judgment; for this proposition cannot be extracted from the analysis of a figure enclosed by three lines, but trials to find it must be made, at least in thought, and the proof thereby thought out. An intuition *a priori* differs from empirical intuition as form from matter. That is to say: if not merely the form but also the object of sensation lies at the basis of the intuition, the intuition is empirical; if, on the other hand, the matter, that is, the object of the intuition, is lacking in the intuition, and only the form is present, and the object possible, the intuition is pure. For example, space in itself is nothing palpable, nothing sensible, and is therefore merely the form that is intuited; whereas something in space that offers resistance, or a filled space, contains something that can be sensibly sensed, and thus contains a representation of sense of something existing. A synthetic judgment, therefore, whose corresponding intuition is an intuition *a priori*, has a pure intuition, without being accompanied by an object of the senses.",["'Empfindung' = sensation; 'Vorstellung des Sinnes' = 'representation of sense'. 'sinnlich empfunden' rendered 'sensibly sensed'.","'Eine Anschauung a priori ist aber verschieden...' — 'aber' left untranslated for smoothness; 'd. i. nemlich' condensed to 'That is to say'.","'sein' (for 'seyn') and 'Widerstand' as in the transcript; 'widerstand leistet' rendered 'offers resistance'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-057":["14. We may assume in advance that, first, all propositions of experience are synthetic judgments and must be known through synthesis (*per synthesin*). For in so far as a judgment is already grounded through analysis, the judgment lay in the concept of the subject, and no extension of knowledge or of the ground of knowledge was needed in order to ascribe the predicate to it. But if the judgment is grounded on experience, the predicate can be known only through the latter, and so is ascribed to the subject through synthesis.",["'Erkenntniß-Grund' rendered 'ground of knowledge'; OCR 'Erkenn tniß' and 'Erkenntniß- Grundes' silently corrected.","'1.' after 'daß' is retained as 'first'; no corresponding '2.' appears in this passage, which may continue in the next."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-058":["2) [We say] that there are propositions *a priori* which are synthetic judgments *a priori*, and which are therefore known independently of all experience. How this is possible, *vide infra*. — Upon these last rests the science which is entitled metaphysics. It is that science, or system, which sets up all the principles of all possible synthetic judgments *a priori*. Transcendental philosophy contains the highest principles of this kind. The science which determines the limits of the possibility of all synthetic modes of knowledge *a priori* (that is, not by extension but by limitation of the faculty of knowledge to the principles which lie in it) is the critique of pure reason.",["The sentence opens with 'daß' dependent on a lost main verb (presumably continuing a preceding passage); I supplied '[We say]' to carry it, and flag the gap.","Restated the long final sentence as three; 'Wissenschaft oder System' kept as 'science, or system'.","'Erkenntnißvermögen' = 'faculty of knowledge'; 'Erkenntnisse' = 'modes of knowledge'.","'Critic' rendered 'critique' per the method; 'Gränzen' = 'limits'. 'd. i.' resolved as a parenthesis."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-059":["15.) Only through a pure form of sensibility are pure intuitions possible; and only through the latter is an extension of our knowledge possible by means of synthetic judgments *a priori*. Extensions of our modes of knowledge *a priori* therefore do not take place in any other way, and hence not at all through supersensible intuitions. These propositions are deduced in the following manner. 1) What representation is in itself cannot be explained. A definition of it cannot be given, because a representation can be explained only by representing to oneself a representation once more, so that [the explanation] lacks grounds of knowledge *in sensu logico*. This action of the mind can be described as something in me which relates itself to something else. This relation of this something else in me is representation, taken subjectively. Representation refers partly to the object to which I relate myself, partly to that action of the mind by which I compare something in me with the object. In the former case we are occupied with the object in itself and its constitution, which must be wholly distinguished from the subject's mode of representation, since this involves the second, the action of the mind. The latter is entitled consciousness, or the representation of myself, in so far as I present to myself the representation of my representation. We are not, however, always conscious of a representation, though we can at any time become conscious of it. Apperception, which accompanies the represented object, is also entitled consciousness. 2) Representation, as source of knowledge, can be brought about only by two sources, namely by intuition and by concepts.",["'Erkenntniß Gründen' rendered 'grounds of knowledge'; 'es fehlt' has no clear subject, so I supplied '[the explanation]'.","'Dann beschäftigt man sich' is obscure: I read it as contrasting the first reference (to the object) with the second, and supplied '[In the former case]' in effect; the sentence is probably corrupt or elliptical.","'Diese Beziehung von diesem Etwas Andern in mir' is awkward in the German and is rendered closely.","'Quelle' occurs twice ('Erkenntniß Quelle... durch 2 Quellen'); retained, though the first may be a slip. 'durchAnschauung' OCR-joined and corrected.","'Action' rendered 'action'; 'Gemüth' = 'mind'. 'Object' left as 'object'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-060":["Both contain in themselves a relation to an object, with this difference only, that in concepts the object is merely thought, in intuition it is given.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-061":["Intuition is, that is to say, an immediate representation of an object; in other words, it contains only one object. For were several present at once, they could be represented only in common through the mark, that is, thought through concepts. This object can therefore be only individual: for example, one sun (for several suns would fall under the concept of a self-luminous body).",["'Merkmal' = 'mark'. 'Z.E.' = 'for example'. 'läge' (singular) rendered with the plural subject as 'would fall'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-062":["A concept is a mediate intuition, or presentation, of an object by means of the mark common to several objects, as ground of knowledge. It therefore always has at its basis a representation which is common to several objects, and the knowing through this [representation], as mark, belongs merely to the thinking of the object. The two kinds of representation are therefore distinguished in this: in intuition the object can be represented in so far as it is given, whereas through the concept the object is representable in so far as it can be thought mediately as given. The concept has reality, therefore, only in so far as an object can be subordinated to it. 3) All human intuition is sensible. Concepts, however, belong solely to the thinking understanding, and sensibility can be mingled with them only in so far as they rest upon intuition; otherwise they cannot in and for themselves have any sensibility through which they might be perceived.",["'mittelbare Anschauung' is Kant's (or the transcriber's) wording, strange for a concept; kept literally, possibly a transcript slip.","'realitaet' rendered 'reality'; 'untergelegt' = 'subordinated' (literally 'laid beneath').","OCR 'kerne' corrected to 'keine'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-063":["Representing is sensible in so far as the object is represented by the subject as appearance.",["Erscheinung = appearance."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-064":["Representing is, on the other hand, intellectual in so far as the object is considered through the representation as it is in itself.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-065":["This is to be understood as follows. The capacity of the mind to receive a representation through being affected by objects is sensibility. If, then, an effect is produced by the object upon the receptivity (that is, upon the capacity of sensibility just noted), sensation of the object arises; and if this brings about a relation to the object, or representation, intuition of the object arises, which, without further more precise determination, is entitled appearance.",["'Vermögen' rendered 'capacity'; 'Bestimmung' = 'determination'. 'receptivitaet' = 'receptivity'.","'bewirkt diese eine Beziehung' taken with 'Empfindung' as subject."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-066":["NB. Appearance can contain truth, and is therefore distinguished from illusion (*Schein*); that is, from the error arising from confusing the thing itself with the mere appearance, or from holding to be true what comes from a subjective representation which one takes to be certainly objective.",["*Schein* given in German as the sense turns on the contrast. 'd. i.' read as introducing the definition of *Schein*; the sentence is loose and I did not divide it."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-067":["It is therefore clear that all intuitions represent the object in appearance; that is, the intuition is taken, on the side of the subject, as the manner yields in which it has been affected by the object. If the object is now considered merely as phenomenon, that is, if the representation is merely sensible, we look only to what is merely subjective in our representation of the object, and this is the manner in which we are affected by the object; in that manner the object also appears to us. It is clear, however, that from the manner in which the object appears, and hence in which the subject is affected by it, we cannot conclude that the object in itself is also so. It is rather certain that in itself it is not so, since sensibility can take account only of the form of the object in appearance. On the other hand, there is no intellectual intuition. The understanding can only think. It does not have the source of sensibility or receptivity, but only the ability to represent through concepts, or spontaneity, whereby it knows; and this class of representations, which relate to objects in themselves, are entitled *noumena*. Accordingly, therefore, there is",["'als die Art ergiebt, wie es...' is irregular; smoothed to 'the manner yields in which it has been affected', and the reading may be corrupt.","The passage breaks off at 'Hiernach ist also'; the numbered point 4) follows in the next passage. Rendered as it stands.","'phaenomen' and 'noumena' kept in Latin form; 'Fähigkeit' = 'ability', 'spontaneitaet' = 'spontaneity'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-068":["4) The form of the subject for being affected in a certain way by the object, the subjective in the representation whereby we are entitled to name the object appearance, and the form of sensibility for being affected by the object, is phenomenon; for the manner in which the subject is affected rests upon the form of this capacity (receptivity), and through it intuits the object as matter. That this [form] may differ is evident from the differences in the instruments of the outer senses. For example, the fly has an eye which is a polyhedron (many-sided), man a quite different eye. Man seems to see singly, and yet perceives the object doubly. A jaundiced person sees differently from a healthy eye.",["The first sentence is tangled (apparently two statements joined by 'und'); I kept the construction rather than invent a connection, and flag it as possibly corrupt.","'Vermögen' = 'capacity'; 'Materie' = 'matter'. 'Z.E.' = 'for example'.","'Daß diese verschieden seyn kann': 'diese' taken as the form of sensibility, supplied '[form]'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-069":["5) If we are to have pure intuition *a priori*, the particular form of sensibility, which we assume in the appearance of the object, must underlie it. This form must, however, be *a priori*, and so cannot be determined through the appearance itself. But it is also certain that in our inner sense there lies a particular form of sensibility which gives the condition under which the objects of appearance can be known by us. This is the pure form of sensibility, which precedes all appearances of things, and so is present before objects are known through intuition. It is necessary in order to know objects.",["OCR 'borm', 'b orm' corrected to 'Form'; 'dei' corrected to 'der'.","'Sie soll aber a priori seyn' — 'Sie' resolved as the form of sensibility, not the intuition; a judgment of the argument.","'Erscheinungen der Dinge' rendered 'appearances of things'; split into shorter sentences."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-070":["6) Extensions of our modes of knowledge *a priori* are possible only through synthetic judgments *a priori*. The possibility of synthetic judgments *a posteriori* raises no difficulty at all. All synthetic judgments of this kind rest on experience, experiences rest on appearances, and hence on intuition. A concept that is to be extended can therefore be extended only through an appearance connected with it. For example, the concept of body is extended through the intuition, and the experience of weight abstracted from it. But how can anything more be added to a concept *a priori*, without any experience, than was contained in it? To this question, as to the possibility, there is no other answer than that it must come about by virtue of the pure form of sensibility, and that synthetic judgments *a priori* can be determined only through this form of sensible intuition. The actuality of all the propositions of mathematics shows that such judgments are possible. For example, *cordarum maxima est diameter* [the diameter is the greatest of chords]. The judgment cannot be got by analysis from the concept of the diameter, but only through the addition of a new appearance. Something that was not in the concept must therefore be added *per synthesin*, and this depends not upon experience but upon the form which is given to intuition.",["Restated the sentence beginning 'und hierauf ist keine andre Antwort' more plainly; 'über die Möglichkeit' is rendered 'as to the possibility'.","'Escheinung' (OCR) silently corrected to 'Erscheinung'; 'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge'.","The bracketed gloss on *cordarum maxima est diameter* is my own supplied translation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-071":["Synthetic propositions *a priori*, however, cannot be related to objects in any way other than as these present themselves in appearance. Otherwise they would be intellectual, and there would be no pure intuition. They are therefore possible only on the condition that the proposition *a priori* contains nothing other than the object *a priori*, so far as it is appearance. This can come about only in the following manner. Synthetic judgments are to extend our concept, and so must contain more than was thought in it; [to this end] the concept is brought into connection with pure intuition, and can be known by virtue of the pure form of sensibility.",["The final sentence is irregular in the German (the main clause is lacking after the 'daß' clause); I smoothed it into two sentences and supplied '[to this end]'.","'Object' retained as 'object' for Gegenstand/Object alike."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-072":["Of these forms of pure sensible intuition there are only two, namely:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-073":["a) space, as the form of all outer intuitions;",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-074":["b) time, as the form of all inner intuition. These forms of pure intuition accordingly require no object itself, but can be represented *a priori*, and are capable of determining synthetic judgments *a priori*.",["'a piioii' (OCR) read as *a priori*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-075":["That they are present in us is taught by consciousness; and they are known as pre-existing before all intuition of sensible objects, and hence as present necessarily and universally. Now if synthetic judgments *a priori* are to be effected, this is not possible without intuition *a priori* (since no synthetic judgment is possible without intuition). But intuition presupposes an object, and so the faculty of sensibility lies at the foundation. This sensibility, however, lies at the foundation *a priori*; and hence it can be determined beforehand through pure intuition how objects will appear, before they have been presented. This is possible through the derived principles of the forms of pure intuition. For example, the various properties of the [...]; the principle: every part of space is again a space, etc. If the ground of the possibility of all synthetic intuitions *a priori* is to be sought only in the inner form of our sensibility, then the latter can be related to an object only in the manner in which the object affects the subject, that is, in so far as it appears as *phaenomenon*. From this it follows that, since we can extend our modes of knowledge only through synthetic judgments, and these require intuitions, all extension of knowledge has at its foundation *phaenomena*, which must be known through the form of sensible intuition. Further, we cannot extend our knowledge *a priori* in so far as we consider things in themselves; for since intuitions belong to this, they would have to be intellectual intuitions, and there are none of that kind. Consequently, in the case of supersensible concepts no extension of knowledge takes place.",["The sentence 'Z.E. Die verschiedenen Eigenschaften des Das princip …' is garbled in the OCR (apparently a broken-off example followed by a restarted one); I translate what can be made out and mark the lacuna with [...].","'a jwiori' read as *a priori*; 'intellectuclle' as 'intellectuelle'; 'findt' as 'findet'. 'Erkenntnißes' rendered 'knowledge'.","The long sentence on the inner form of sensibility is kept as one, since its structure is clear; 'sämtlicher synthetischer Anschauungen' is taken as written, though 'Urtheile' may be intended."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-076":["16) We can therefore have intuitions only by virtue of the form of our sensibility, and objects will appear in accordance with this form, whether empirically or *a priori*. The character of objects in appearance rests, then, solely upon the subjective form of intuition, by which it determines the mode of intuition in the mind, and upon the difference in the relation in which objects are intuited by us and affect the mind. Thus even objectively the circular line appears different, even as an oblong or a straight line, according to the difference of the direction in which we see it. The appearance of the object accordingly has nothing further in common with the objective character of the thing itself, and our knowledge is not capable of extending beyond the limits of appearance (that is, perception and its place). We are rather able only to transfer the appearance, as it is determined through the pure form of sensibility, back again to the objects themselves, and so to apply our perception once more.",["'wodurch sie die Art derselben im Gemüth bestimmt' is ambiguous; 'sie' is taken to refer to the form of intuition, and I rendered it 'it'.","'oblongum' translated 'oblong'; the last clause ('auf die Objecte selbst wieder zu übertragen') is obscure in the German and rendered closely.","'Verhältnisses' rendered 'relation'; the sentence beginning 'Es hat also' may be a student's compression of Kant's argument."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-077":["17) Space and time are now the only pure forms of sensible intuition, namely space as the form of all outer sensible intuitions, and time as the form of all inner sensible intuitions. Hence they concern only the subjective mode of representing things *a priori*; that is, they are the subjective condition for being able to know objects through sensible intuition as appearance presents them. For all things in sensible intuition are placed in space and time in being represented, and through this it becomes possible for us to represent or intuit objects in appearance. They are therefore the form of sensibility, present in us before all experience of the object as the subjective condition of intuition. Consequently they cannot be things in themselves, or coexist with the objects. For if they were, we should have to know space and time, like objects, in and for themselves, and so through the understanding. Knowledge would then be only *a posteriori*, and extension of it possible only therein, and could never come about *a priori*. We should never be able to judge *a priori* about representations, or to see the necessity of the character of things in knowledge. Hence they are in and for themselves nothing objective.",["Restated the long sentence beginning 'Sie können daher nicht Dinge an sich selbst seyn' as several sentences.","'so wie die Gegenstände an und für sich erkennen' is taken to mean that space and time, like objects, would be known as things in themselves.","'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-078":["I 1. Both, space and time, are the forms of sensible intuitions or, what is the same, they are formal sensible intuitions; that is, they are forms of each individual subject, according to which the given object can be represented only under this form and under no other. They can also be only intuitions, and never concepts. For what is essentially required is the immediate representation of objects, and indeed of a single object, and no mark of a concept can ever be laid at the foundation of it.",["The opening 'I 1.' is probably an OCR remnant of a paragraph number and is kept as printed; the stray comma in ',dazu' was silently corrected.","The dash at the end of the passage is omitted."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-079":["All spaces and all times are only parts of one single space and one time. There is only one space and one time. Parts of one and the same space and time can therefore well be thought alongside one another and, in the case of time, after one another; but these parts cannot be subordinated to a universal space or time to be assumed. Space and time are therefore each a representation of a *res singularis* and not *communis* [of a single thing and not of a general one]. When it is said that the world has a beginning and an end, this can mean only that the object of the world has a time in which it was not, begins to be, and will be no more; the time itself, however, always remains one representation, unalterable in itself.",["'Theü' (OCR) read as 'Theile'; 'Escheinung'-type slips silently corrected.","'respective nach einander' rendered 'in the case of time, after one another' by judgment of the argument.","'re singulär i et non communi' is garbled in the OCR; read as *re singulari* / *non communi*, and the bracketed gloss is mine. 'Wirklichkeit' etc. not at issue here."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-080":["2. Space and time are pure sensible intuitions *a priori*, and are therefore independent of all experience. For in order to have an experience of that which is in space, we can think this experience only on the supposition that we have previously thought a space. All things can be thought as annihilated, and yet space remains. Space and time therefore do not depend upon the things in space and in time, as the *coexistentia rerum* [coexistence of things], as Wolff believes; they are intuitions which subsist for themselves, once we have abstracted from all things. Space accordingly contains essentially in itself only the subjective form of outer intuitions, just as time contains the subjective form of inner intuitions. We could not, however, accept them as something formal if we held it necessary first to abstract space and time from the things or from matter itself, in order to regard them as something objective, which would then have to be thought as existing outside us, as something coexisting with the things. They would in that case be concepts *a posteriori*, whereas they are intuitions *a priori*. Hence, in order to think them, we need not first assume things to which they are supposed to give determination [Bestimmung].",["The final sentence of the German is irregular (the \"Man könnte sie ... nicht annehmen, wenn ...\" construction runs on); I divided it into three sentences and resolved \"so außer uns ... gedacht werden müßte\" as referring to space and time as objective.","\"Wolf\" is rendered in the modern spelling \"Wolff\"; \"coexistentia rerum\" kept in Latin with English supplied in square brackets.","\"Dinge\" is rendered \"things\"; \"Bestimmung\" is given in brackets because the sense (space and time determining things) is a choice.","\"Anschauung ... für sich bestehende\": the sense of \"subsisting for themselves\" is kept literally; the German is somewhat loose here, as is usual in student transcripts."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-081":["3. The properties of space and of time are of such a kind that they must be derived not from concepts but immediately from intuition. Wolff, for instance, derives space and its properties from objective concepts. He says that it is the *ordo simultaneorum quatenus extra se positae existunt simul* [order of things simultaneous, in so far as they exist together as set outside one another]. But on this view it cannot be explained why space has three dimensions, namely length, breadth and height. Nor can these be derived from experience; on the contrary, we must already have space with its three dimensions in thought before we can represent a body in experience. They therefore already lie, as a condition of representation, in inner intuition. Consciousness is compelled to accept them, and they accordingly exist *a priori* as necessary. Were they not grounded upon this higher intuition and pre-existent in it, they could not, being abstracted from things, be necessarily ascribed to things. Through this the order of things becomes distinct, which Wolff, in his definition of space, determines only obscurely, when he holds that space is a confused representation of the order of things in so far as they are outside one another.",["Long sentence \"Sie liegen also ... präexistirten\" divided into several; the clause \"und von Dingen abstrahirt ... wenn sie nicht ... präexistirten\" is obscure and my rendering is a judgment of the argument's requirement.","\"Anschauung\" in \"innern Anschauung\" is rendered literally as \"inner intuition\"; the sense of \"diese höhere Anschauung\" is unclear and may be a transcript slip.","The Latin definition is given as in the text (\"quatenus extra se positae\"); the grammar is irregular (feminine \"positae\"), and I have not emended it.","OCR: \"deutlieh\" read as \"deutlich\"; a stray full stop before \"Länge\" omitted."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-082":["On the other hand, there is no doubt that concepts too can be drawn from the pure intuition of space, and thereby derived.",["\"leidt\" (for \"leidet\") read as \"leidet\"; \"keinen Zweifel leiden\" rendered \"there is no doubt\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-083":["4. From the fact that space and time are pure intuitions *a priori*, and not concepts abstracted from objects, it follows that all propositions of geometry and arithmetic, that is, those which concern numbers and figures, are synthetic judgments, and indeed *a priori*, and therefore necessary and apodeictically certain. This would not be so if they could not be derived from space and time as pure intuitions. For example, it can be found without trial that a straight line is perpendicular to a given point.",["The German \"oder, die Zahlen und Figuren betreffen\" is rendered \"that is, those which concern numbers and figures\", reading \"oder\" as explanatory.","The final example is as transcribed; \"perpendiculär auf einen gegebenen Punkt\" is odd (a line is perpendicular to a line), but I translate it as it stands. The transcript may be defective.","\"Arithmetic\" and \"apodictisch\" are rendered \"arithmetic\" and \"apodeictically\", following Kemp Smith's usage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-084":["5. Space and time are indeed objective in respect of objects, that is, they represent the objects; but only as they are in appearance, not as they are in themselves.",["OCR hyphenation \"Gegen -stände\" silently repaired.","\"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"an sich\" = \"in themselves\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-085":["If we represent the thing in itself, we regard it as a whole consisting of many parts, and consider all the parts in themselves and in their relation to one another. This is done through the understanding, which thereby forms the concept of the whole, under which the parts are comprehended. Now if space and time rested upon concepts, we should absolutely have to represent to ourselves parts of space and of time first (and be able to represent this), before we could think space and time as a whole. But the parts of space and time, namely particular spaces and times, are contained as coordinate in space and time, and not under it. No space and no time can be thought without at the same time thinking a far greater space or time; and thereby the representation raises itself to a single object, and this can be grounded only in intuition. Indeed, if space and time were properties of things in themselves, they would have to be attributes of God. For space is unbounded, and the duration of time is likewise without limits. Space and time are unique; both are necessary in respect of the existence of all things. They are all-embracing in regard to all objects, which they all contain in themselves. They are eternal. Hence, since the reality of things rests in God as its ground, both could be ascribed only to God; and the hypothesis of Spinozism did in fact consist in this, that space and time were taken for things in themselves and for attributes of God.",["Divided the German's long sentences and some run-on clauses (\"Der Raum und Zeit sind einig; ...\") into shorter ones; \"einig\" is rendered \"unique\" (i.e. one, *einig* for *einzig*), a judgment of the sense.","\"unter ihm\" (singular) is rendered \"under it\", referring to space and time together as a single whole.","\"einen weit großem\" read as \"weit größeren\"; \"Einzelnen Object\" = \"single object\".","\"Eigenschaften\" rendered \"attributes\" when said of God and \"properties\" elsewhere; \"wircklich\" modernised silently."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-086":["19. If we now pass to man's knowledge of the things themselves, the human capacity for knowing things proves to be very limited. Only the understanding of God is entitled intuition. This kind of understanding is inexplicable to us men; yet the term is meant to indicate that God has the power of knowing things as they are in themselves, which is wholly lacking in man. Man can know only through concepts, that is, through marks which he abstracts from objects sensed in intuition. But by this procedure of abstraction he must set aside very many features of the things, in order to gain insight into even a little of them. The marks so formed, and the thinking of things through them, therefore yield only a limited knowledge. It can never go further than sensibility furnishes the material for it, and consequently man is capable of knowing things only in the way in which they appear to him, and nothing of the things themselves as they are in themselves. Just as sensibility, then, is the capacity for intuition, so the understanding of man is the capacity for concepts.\n\nConcepts can also be thought *a priori*, if they contain nothing but the concept of *synthesis*, that is, of the combination of the manifold in the representation so as to constitute a mode of knowledge, and if this *synthesis* has unity, that is, consciousness of myself in the combination of the manifold in my representation. It is a matter of indifference whether this combination involves negative or affirmative parts of the representation. Under these conditions there arises a concept which is thought *a priori* of the object; for the combination of the manifold in the representation, and the consciousness of it, or the unity, are not empirical concepts but *a priori*. For example, that a body is composed I cannot know from experience; I cannot see the composition. Here there is a mere *actus* of thinking, although at first sight the concept of the composite seems to be formed *a posteriori*. The understanding alone has combined the manifold in the representation, and the concept arose through the consciousness of the combination. On this rests the pure concept of the understanding, or concept *a priori*. The subject, or the understanding, obtains the consciousness itself of the combination of the manifold through the pure intuition of space and time.\n\nPure concepts of the understanding are therefore entitled those which contain synthetic unity, or the consciousness of the combination of the manifold in the representation; or [one may say] concepts of the unity of the manifold in the synthetic representation. The pure concept of the understanding is predicated as category; this is accordingly the consciousness of the synthetic unity of the given manifold in the representation. The category in and for itself leaves undecided and undetermined whether the concept, that is, the category itself, has objective reality. In this respect it is like a representation which contains nothing contradictory and so can be thought, but may be empty. Whether it rests upon sensible synthetic unity [— empirical unity —], or has such unity, thus remains undetermined in its case. Yet it is only at the start that the pure concept of the understanding is so taken that it is uncertain whether or not it has reality. Afterwards, through the condition of space and time, there is added the fact that I thereby become aware that it signifies something objective.",["OCR slips silently corrected: Bingen/Binge = Dingen/Dinge, Ber = Der, Benken(s) = Denken(s), Bas = Das; 'abstractions Verfahren' rendered as 'procedure of abstraction'.","'gedacht 6sist': the '6s' is a stray page or line mark; read as 'gedacht ist' and rendered 'is thought'.","The passage is a very long paragraph; I restated it as several sentences and two paragraphs, as the method permits. The sense is unchanged.","Merkmale = 'marks'. 'Nennt man Anschauung' = 'is entitled intuition'; Kant evidently means the divine understanding is called intuitive. 'Praedicirt man Categorie' = 'is predicated as category' (a transcript irregularity).","'ob sie auf sinnlicher synthetischer Einheit — empirischer Einheit — beruhe, oder habe': the construction is irregular and possibly corrupt (the dashes may be an insertion or a gloss). I translate it as it stands and bracket the dashes.","'Anschein ... Begriff von dem Zusammengesetzten': 'first appearance' rendered 'at first sight'. 'Nur Anfangs' is read as 'only at the start' (contrasted with 'nachher')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-087":["For example, the category of magnitude, as a many of the same kind which together make up one: this cannot be comprehended without space and time. Here, moreover, what matters is not the empirical actuality of bodies but only the possibility, according to our understanding, of finding what magnitude is; and this possibility, as has been said, is possible through pure intuition *a priori*.",["'Z. E.' (zum Exempel) rendered 'For example'. 'Begreifen' rendered 'comprehended'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-088":["It is the same with the concept of cause, so far as it consists in the ground of the existence of something else according to a constant rule. But the category that B is the effect of A, and the necessity of the cause [for] being an effect, that a *causatum* has a *causa*, is likewise as little to be comprehended unless one takes pure intuition of space and time to one's aid.",["'die Nothwendigkeit der Ursach, eine Wirkung zu seyn' is obscure and perhaps garbled in the transcript (perhaps 'eine Wirkung zu haben'). I translate it literally and supply '[for]'.","'ist aber so wenig zu begreifen' has no clear antecedent for 'so wenig'; I read it as 'as little as the category of magnitude' and render 'likewise as little'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-089":["18) On the presupposition, now, that we are in a position to represent objects only in appearance, and not as they are in themselves, Herr Kant builds",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; it is continued in the following passages. The numbering '18)' after '19.' is as in the source."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-090":["1.) the theory of the ideality of space and time, and rejects",["Continues the preceding sentence and breaks off; translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-091":["2.) the theory of the opposing reality of space and time.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-092":["The theory of ideality accordingly assumes that space and time are nothing other than something subjective, or concern merely the form of sensible intuition. It rests, then, on the principle that space and time are not real determinations of things in themselves but mere forms, given as conditions lying solely in the subject for the purpose of intuition. The possibility of having such sensible forms is made clear, for example, by the fact that in the concept of God we may ascribe to him, not the predicate of thinking, because of the limitedness of that concept, but a capacity for the intuition of things in themselves, which [capacity] would rest upon this principle.",["'oder = blos die Form ... angehe': the '=' is in the source; I render it as 'or'. The subjunctives (angehe, erhelle, beruhe) are indirect speech and are rendered with 'would' where needed.","Last clause restated: 'als welches auf diesem princip beruhe' refers, I judge, to the Anschauungs-Vermögen, not to God; hence the bracketed '[capacity]'.","Bestimmungen = 'determinations'; Vermögen = 'capacity'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-093":["The defenders of the theory of the reality of space and time assume, on the contrary, that space and time are a character of things in themselves, and that we have space and time not *a priori* but from experience. Herr Kant refutes this by the existence of modes of knowledge *a priori* of space and time, and by the point that it would be impossible to know their character, for example the three dimensions of space, synthetically *a priori*, or to judge about them *a priori*.",["'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge' (plural). 'Beschaffenheit' rendered 'character'; 'Abmeßungen' rendered 'dimensions'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-094":["But Herr Kant determines",["The sentence is cut off and is continued in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-095":["3. the theory of the ideality of space and time as something merely subjective, more precisely in this respect: that through it we should become capable not merely of knowing our own subject but really of knowing objects. For once we represent objects to ourselves as *phaenomena*, all the predicates which we have represented and intuited by virtue of the subjective form really belong to the objects, though only as appearance, and so far the objects have reality for us. Since it is accordingly impossible to represent the object in itself, *noumena* could concern only objects of non-sensible intuition.",["Restated as three sentences; the German is a single sentence in indirect speech with subjunctives (würden, erkennten, kämen, hätten), rendered partly in the indicative for clarity.","'darin genauer' is taken to govern the 'daß' clause and to depend on 'bestimmt' in the preceding passage. 'Erscheinung' = 'appearance'; 'phaenomena' and 'noumena' kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-096":["Herr Kant grounds his theory of the ideality of space and time more closely through the properties of both.",["'Eigenschaften' rendered 'properties'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-097":["Suppose it were necessary that space and time belong to the things themselves as properties, and that we do not intuit the things as appearance through the subjective form of our senses. Then we should have to know space and time of the things through the understanding. The form of the understanding consists in the formation of concepts, but essentially in this, that it thinks a manifold (that is, parts) and combines these into a unity (that is, into a whole); in other words, that it thinks things through a concept. The object, then, essentially has parts. Now what is a part of something else must also admit of being regarded as subsisting for itself, and can be entitled a part only in relation to the whole. But a single space can be thought only as a part, that is, as a section of a larger space, and this larger space again as a part of one still larger, and so on to infinity. Hence infinity must also be predicated of space and time. Space therefore cannot have a limit, as objects have; it has no parts under it which could be combined into a unity; and so it cannot be thought as a whole either. It has, then, no determination whatever that represents anything objective; it is therefore no object, and cannot be thought through concepts. From this it follows that it is only something single, and hence that the representation of it rests on intuition. It follows further that this intuition must be not empirical but *a priori*, since space can be thought prior to the object in space. The representation therefore lies in the subject alone. Now, since space and time are thought *a priori*, it is necessary to place all objects of sensible intuition in space and time. But these objects could not be ordered unless sensibility were provided with a form co-ordinated to this end, and this form must be sought in space and time. Hence they are the only sensible forms of our intuition, and are merely subjective. From this it follows again that, since we intuit the objects themselves only in a certain form, this form lies in our subject and not in the object, and that we can therefore know the objects only as they appear to us.",["Restated the opening long sentence as several; 'Ist es nämlich nöthig…' is read as a supposition (the opponent's view), which the argument requires.","'deßen I orm' is an OCR slip for 'dessen Form'; 'Iheil' for 'Theil'; 'großem' (twice) read as 'größern'; 'Es hegt' as 'Es liegt'.","'Er' (of Raum und Zeit, strictly singular) is rendered 'space' / 'it' by judgment; the German passes between singular and plural.","'Verstand' = understanding; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Bestimmung' = determination; 'Erscheinung' = appearance.","'ordinirten' in 'dazu ordinirten Form' rendered 'co-ordinated to this end'; slightly doubtful.","'zu legen' ('to place'): 'in Raum und Zeit zu legen' kept as 'place'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-098":["*Consequentiae.* From the concept of space and time there now follow some synthetic propositions *a priori*.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-099":["1.) Two times are not simultaneous but successive, just as two spaces are not successive but simultaneous.",["'zw ey' is an OCR break for 'zwey'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-100":["2.) In time all objects are thought and represented by me as they are simultaneous or successive; in space, however, the objects are represented as they are outside me. Consequently what exists in me and not outside me cannot be intuited in space.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-101":["3.) The representation of time is, that is to say, in me, and time therefore belongs to inner sense; the representation of space, on the other hand, is outside me, and belongs to outer sense. The determinations of representations in respect of time therefore all take place in me, but in respect of space all outside me. When Newton, then, says in a scholium to the *principia philosophiae naturalis* that time is everywhere and space at all times, this cannot be understood to mean that time has a place. Time has no place to which I can assign something in time, and what I think in time is not in space at all. But it can quite certainly be taken to mean that the concept of time comprehends all representations, whereas space comprehends only those representations in so far as their objects are outside me.",["'Die Vorstellung vom Raum ... ist außer mir' is rendered literally; the sense is that its objects are represented as outside me, as the last sentence of the passage indicates.","Newton's phrase is paraphrased in the German, not quoted; kept as given.","'Es kann aber ganz sicher heißen' rendered 'it can quite certainly be taken to mean'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-102":["4.) All things outside me I can think, in so far as I take account of their relation to other things, only in space. That is, determinations of the relation of things outside me can be specified only on the presupposition of their existence in space.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-103":["Hence when Wolff thinks things in space and places space in the *ordo simultaneorum*, space is known through a concept of the understanding, through the relation of things. So likewise when he determines time through the *ordo successivorum, quatenus sunt invicem connexa* (*quae post invicem essent*). But one must already have thought space and time in advance before one thinks things as simultaneous or successive.",["'ordine simultaneorum' / 'ordinem succeßivorum' appear in the transcript in irregular case forms; I have given the nominative as the sense of the technical terms, keeping the Latin of the quotation otherwise as it stands.","The Latin quotation is left untranslated as a technical formula (roughly 'order of successive things, in so far as they are connected with one another').","Wolf spelled 'Wolff' in English."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-001":["Remark",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-002":["In the *Critique of Pure Reason* it has been concluded, from the exposition of the concept of sensibility (*perceptivitaet*) as the capacity to receive representations according to the manner in which the subject is affected, and of the concept of the understanding (capacity to think, *spontaneitaet*) as the capacity to know in accordance with these representations, that the mind appears to itself, or that the soul can be an object of appearance to itself. It is in itself correct that the subject can be affected by itself, and so through inner sense can receive representations of the soul and know from them, after the mind has first been affected by objects. In order, however, to avoid ambiguities on this point, Herr Kant proposes instead the following definition of sensibility: the merely subjective in the representation of things, in so far as it contains the formal ground for presenting the objects to ourselves in accordance with it; or also, the sensible form of our intuition. For nothing objective can itself lie in it. By the form lying in it, it determines the appearance; that is, it determines in advance the manner in which the object will appear. It lies at the foundation of all kinds of sensible intuition, and is thus present *a priori* in order to determine these intuitions in space and time; but it has no connection at all with the object itself, still less is it a property of the object itself, but merely makes known its appearance.",["'Critic der reinen Vernunft' rendered *Critique of Pure Reason* as the title.","The first sentence is tangled ('zu erhalten ... geschloßen worden'); restated in one sentence with 'it has been concluded' governing. 'zu erhalten' ('to receive') is taken as completing 'Vermögens, Vorstellungen ... zu erhalten'.","'Sie' in 'Sie bestimmt...' is taken to refer to sensibility (Sinnlichkeit) as defined.","'perceptivitaet' and 'spontaneitaet' left in the transcript's spelling; 'Vermögen' = capacity; 'Gemüth' = mind."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-003":["Here is the place to indicate the distinction between a representation that is *sensitiva* and one that is *sensibilis*. With regard to the objects that are represented, representations are either:",["'sensitiv' / 'sensibili' kept in Latin as technical terms; 'sensibili' is an ablative-dative form in the German context (von der sensibili), rendered as *sensibilis*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-004":["*sensitivae*, that is, sensible representations, which rest on the form of sensibility alone, without the existence of matter concurring in them. Thus space and time are forms of sensibility without any object of the senses concurring and being connected with them.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-005":["These, however, can at the same time be combined with matter, or with the object of the senses, and then the *sensitiva* become *sensibilia*, or [things] capable of sensation (*empfindbar*), that is, which at the same time contain reality. Thus there can be in space and time something that is an object of the senses, and this is what is capable of sensation, which gives reality; the *sensibile* is therefore equivalent to the matter of sensibility. Thus all empirical modes of knowledge contain not merely the *sensitivum* in respect of form [*rationem*], but at the same time something capable of sensation; whereas in sensible knowledge in general nothing empirical, and consequently nothing capable of sensation, is contained.",["'das sensitive rationem der Form' is awkward (a Latin accusative in a German sentence); rendered 'the *sensitivum* in respect of form', with *rationem* bracketed. Possibly corrupt.","'Erkenntniße' = modes of knowledge; 'sinnliche Erkenntniß' = sensible knowledge.","'[things]' is supplied for the neuter plural 'empfindbaren'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-006":["In opposition to sensible knowledge, however, there are intellectual representations, which are thought through the understanding alone. Here too the form of thinking and the matter or reality are similar; the concept of the understanding is [related to them] as the product.",["The last sentence is obscure and possibly corrupt ('ähnlich der Verstandes Begriff als das product'); my rendering is conjectural, and the bracketed words are supplied. The German may have lost a verb or a negation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-007":["ad § 19. Everything that has so far been said about the possibility of obtaining modes of knowledge a priori through the subjective form of sensible intuition belongs to the transcendental aesthetic, that is, the doctrine of the senses [*Sinnen Lehre*]. Transcendental logic, that is, the doctrine of the understanding, on the other hand, furnishes a second source of principles whereby pure modes of knowledge a priori may possibly be obtained. This logic differs from general pure logic in that the latter is occupied with the mere form of the employment of our understanding, whereas the former is directed to the determination of the pure knowledge of objects through the mere understanding.",["Restated as three sentences; 'diese ... jene' resolved as general pure logic (the latter) and transcendental logic (the former).","*Sinnen Lehre* rendered literally as 'doctrine of the senses', as Kant's gloss on 'Ästhetic'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-008":["A pure concept of the understanding is a pure knowledge of the object through the mere understanding, and the complete *oppositum* of the empirical concept, inasmuch as it is thought wholly pure a priori. Consequently it may also be entitled a concept of pure thinking, in order to distinguish it from sensibility.",["OCR 'V erstand' silently corrected to 'Verstand'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-009":["The concept a priori of the synthetic unity of the manifold in intuition is the category. The combination of the manifold, and the consciousness of this combination, constitute what is constitutive in the category.",["'Categorie' is left without an article in the German; I supplied 'the'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-010":["The consciousness of the unity of the manifold according to concepts is a logical function.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-011":["All objects (whether they occur in appearance or through concepts) can indeed be perceived, but their composition never. This the understanding must add to the representation, and it is therefore solely an act [*Actus*] of the understanding, namely the composition, whereby the composite is so represented that it becomes one. This Herr Kant entitles synthesis; for example, the parts that together make up a room.",["Restated; 'um sich das zusammengesetzte dahin vorzustellen damit es eins werde' is awkward, and I read it as 'so represented that it becomes one'.","'Vorkommen' (capitalised in the OCR) read as 'vorkommen'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-012":["The consciousness of the synthetic unity of the manifold in intuition in general, or (which is the same) the consciousness of the concept that contains the synthetic unity, is the category.",["Kant's '=' in the transcript is rendered 'is the same' in the parenthesis and 'is' in the predicate."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-013":["The pure concepts of the understanding were so entitled by Aristotle. The Scholastics called them *praedicamenta*, and they were equal in number [to ours]; there are, that is, twelve in all, and they contain the *elementa* of modes of knowledge a priori through the understanding. Besides these there are also *praedicabilia*, which are in part composed of the former, e.g. cause combined with magnitude, and in part the *praedicamenta* combined with the concepts of space and time. These also can be enumerated, and presumably quite completely. Herr Kant has not enumerated them.",["'der Zahl nach gleich' is elliptical; the bracketed 'to ours' is my supplied reading (the sense may rather be 'equal in number to Aristotle's', which would conflict with the twelve; doubtful).","'aus erstem' read as 'from the former' (the praedicamenta)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-014":["Taken together, the categories and *praedicabilia* constitute all the elementary principles required for knowing the extent of all modes of knowledge a priori.",["OCR 'Unfang' corrected to 'Umfang' (extent)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-015":["20. It is very remarkable that all possible concepts of the understanding can be brought under classes, and can be so derived from the faculty of the understanding that the understanding is thereby exhausted as regards its extent, just as the principles of all sensible modes of knowledge result from the form of space and time. The understanding is the faculty of concepts. Now in order to bring anything under a concept, a judgment is in each case necessary; consequently all actions of the understanding come down to judging. For thinking is a discursive knowledge, that is, knowledge through certain marks of things. These marks constitute the predicate, which is ascribed to the representation of the thing as subject; and it is in this combination that the judgment lies. Now just as logic contains functions of the understanding, that is, rules of thinking in general, without regard to an object, so transcendental logic, which is that general logic applied to objective knowledge a priori, contains these functions of thinking, or forms of judgments. Hence here also the four logical functions can be taken as foundation, and all possible elementary principles of pure thinking can be reduced to quantity, quality, relation and modality. Accordingly the table of the pure concepts of the understanding can be traced back to the table of the four logical functions, thus:",["Divided and smoothed in several places; 'um nun etwas uns unter einen Begriff zu bringen' read with the stray 'uns' omitted as a slip.","OCR corrections: '\\ erstandes' to 'Verstandes', 'Dei' to 'Der', 'mithm' to 'mithin'.","'Merkmale' rendered 'marks'; 'Urtheil' 'judgment'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-016":["1) In respect of quantity our logical judgments are *judicia*: a *singularia*, singular; b *particularia*, particular. Since several objects are here always presupposed, and it is yet undecided whether the several may not be a totality, whereas the two must be distinguished, it would be better to entitle these *pluralia*.",["The sentence is tangled in the German; I restated it in two sentences, taking 'dies aber davon unterschieden seyn muß' to mean that plurality must be distinct from totality."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-017":["c *universalia* — universal",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-018":["II) In respect of quality: a *affirmativa*, b *negativa*, c *infinita*; that is, affirmative judgments in respect of the copula, or negative in respect of the predicate, e.g. *anima est non mortalis*. III) In respect of relation (relations)",["OCR 'nonmortalis' read as 'non mortalis'.","The German text is itself compressed ('affirmative ... oder negative'), and I kept it close."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-019":["a categorical, in the relation of predicate to subject; b hypothetical, in the relation of consequence to ground; c disjunctive, in the relation of a member of the division to the whole sphere of the concept. IV) In respect of modality (relations)",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-020":["a problematic b assertoric c apodeictic. These contain the relations of the predicate to the subject according to the difference ad a) of logical possibility, ad b) of logical actuality, ad c) of logical necessity. That is to say, if the judgment contains a predicate which in respect of the subject is thought only under the condition of possibility, it is thought merely problematically; it becomes a proposition, or assertoric judgment, when the predicate is actually ascribed to the subject; and if this proposition is combined with necessity, the judgment becomes apodeictic.",["Restated the final sentence for smoothness; OCR 'V erschiedenheit' and 'Noth Wendigkeit' silently corrected."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-021":["On these rest just as many categories, with this difference only, that the latter are directed to objects, so that here everything is predicated of objects which in the case of the logical functions was said of concepts. Thus: I) In respect of quantity",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-022":["1 a Unity",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-023":["2 b Plurality",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-024":["3 c Totality II) In respect of quality",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-025":["4 a Reality",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-026":["5 b Negation",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-027":["6 c Limitation; that is, in respect of quality the category rests on the presupposition either of something which contains the concept of being, e.g. light, or of the concept of non-being, e.g. rest, or of the concept of being combined at the same time with non-being as limitation. III) In respect of relation, where two correlative concepts always concur",["'Vielheit' rendered 'plurality', 'Allheit' 'totality'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-028":["7 a. Substance — accident [*accidens*]",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-029":["8 b. Cause — effect",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-030":["9 c. *Agens* — *patiens*, or = community, or = *influxus mutuus*, or = *commercium*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-031":["NB ad 9 c. The relation of community differs from that of consequence to ground in that here there is reciprocally cause and effect; that is, there is something in the *causatum* which is ground of the *causa*, and something in the *causa* which is ground of the *causatum*, both at once. IV) In respect of modality",["'caußato' read as *causatum*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-032":["10 a. Possibility — (impossibility)",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-033":["*potentialitas*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-034":["11 b. Actuality *actualitas*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-035":["(Existence) (Non-existence)",["'Daseyn' and 'Nichtseyn' rendered 'existence' and 'non-existence'; the scheme in the original is a tabular layout, kept as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-036":["12 c. *Necessitas* (contingency). Here the concept of actuality may not be put on a level with the concept of existence; for the existence of a thing comprehends in itself the possibility, the actuality and the necessity of an object, existence being predicated of all three, whereas of *actualitas* actuality alone is predicated.",["OCR 'aetualitas' read as 'actualitas'; sentence slightly smoothed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-037":["Comparison of the four logical functions with the twelve categories ad A. 1 Quantity *singularia* *particularia* *universalia* Unity Plurality Totality",["Tabular layout lost in OCR; kept as a run of terms."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-038":["2 Quality *affirmativa* *negativa* *infinita*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-040":["categorical, hypothetical, disjunctive",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-042":["problematic assertoric apodeictic Possibility, Actuality, Necessity",["OCR 'N othwendigkeit' corrected."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-044":["I Remarks",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-045":["1) These twelve categories can be reduced to two classes, each of which again comprehends two classes under it, and",["Breaks off at 'und'; continued in the next passages."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-046":["A.) the first six categories, those of quantity and quality, may be entitled the mathematical class.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-047":["B.) the last six categories, those of relation and modality, from 7 to 12, may be entitled the dynamical class.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-048":["2) Aristotle brought out only ten categories; but a classification according to the table of the logical functions was wholly lacking. They were therefore found by chance and are defective, and even include the forms of space and time among them, although these belong to a separate class.",["'formen' left lower-case in the German and rendered 'forms'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-049":["3) It is always a remarkable phenomenon [*Erscheinung*] that we are in a position to discover the functions of the understanding completely, and that there are exactly as many pure concepts of the understanding *a priori* as there are logical functions; that the former correspond to the latter; and that we are thereby in a position to determine all the elements of pure thinking in regard to all objects, that is, prior to all possible experience. This holds, however, as is self-evident, only within the limits of transcendental philosophy. That is to say, the modes of pure rational knowledge whose elements are here set out are immanent, that is, have objective reality; and consequently that class of rational knowledge which has no object of possible experience is here excluded.",["*Erscheinung* here means 'phenomenon' in the ordinary sense of a remarkable fact, not 'appearance' in the technical sense; I have so rendered it and marked the German.","Restated the long sentence as two; the second begins at 'This holds, however…'. 'Vernunft-Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of pure rational knowledge' (plural of *Erkenntnis*)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-050":["4) From the *praedicamenta*, that is, from the categories, as the Scholastics call them, there arise the *praedicabilia*, that is, those pure concepts of the understanding which are either compounded of two or more categories, or arise from the combination of a *praedicamentum* with a form of sensibility, space or time.",["*praedicamenta* and *praedicabilia* kept in Latin as technical terms. 'Form der Sinnlichkeit von Raum und Zeit' rendered 'a form of sensibility, space or time'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-051":["For example, the concept of duration rests, in respect of quality, on reality or existence; and duration also has the magnitude of a [mode of] existence [*Daseyn*], in regard to existence in time. For in this respect the duration of a grain of sand is equal to the duration of a mountain of sand, and this magnitude is different from material quantity. Thus a *praedicamentum* concurs with the form of sensibility. The concept of motion presupposes existence or quality, and is thought with a change of existence under opposed determinations of time and space; hence as existence in successive form, one after another, in time and space.",["'Dasejms' read as *Daseyns* (OCR). The bracketed 'mode of' is my supplied gloss; the German has only 'Größe eines Daseyns'.","'Mit entgegengesetzter Bestimmung von Zeit und Raum' is obscure in the transcript; rendered literally, 'determinations' being plural for sense."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-052":["5) In each function the categories are so ordered that the third is compounded of the two preceding. For example, community presupposes two substances, together with cause and effect, and combines them into a third, namely reciprocity of action [*Wechsel*]: cause and effect. So reality, combined with negation, gives limitation.",["'Gemeinschaft der Begriffe' is probably a slip in the transcript for *Gemeinschaft der Substanzen*; translated as 'community'. 'Wechsel' is doubtful here; I give 'reciprocity of action' with the German bracketed, as the argument requires."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-053":["So totality [*Allheit*] is a plurality which is at the same time unity.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-054":["So necessity is a possibility from which actuality can be inferred.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-055":["6) In accordance with the classification of the categories premised above, ontology or transcendental philosophy is now also to be treated. By setting the categories over against one another, the development of these elements of our concepts of the understanding *a priori* is facilitated; by their union the arbitrary, which is bound up with Baumgarten's method, is avoided; and these principles *a priori* are better grasped as a whole. Accordingly the six categories of the mathematical class will also be treated, and among these there follows first:",["Divided the sentence in two. 'heßer' read as 'besser' (OCR). 'prämittirten' read as *praemittirten*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-056":["The category of magnitude, or the class of quantity.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-057":["21. Quantity [*Quantität*], magnitude [*Größe*], is a pure concept of the understanding, the development of which rests on the following:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-058":["1) In every magnitude the concept of a manifold is contained which, considered as combined, presents itself as one. A manifold of like kind, combined, constitutes quantity as such. In the concept of magnitude there lies, therefore, the representation of one and many.",["'Ein Vieles' rendered 'a manifold' / 'many' as the context suits; 'Vorstellung' = representation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-059":["2) One and many can be thought only as correlates, and can be thought only in opposition [*in opposito*]; neither of the two can be defined, but they can only be thought through each other. Hence the author's definition, S4 § 74, runs round in a circle: he thinks a one on the presupposition of a many.",["'S4 § 74' (Baumgarten, *Metaphysica*, Sect. 4, § 74) kept as printed. 'definitio autoris' rendered 'the author's definition'; 'in Circulo' as 'in a circle'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-060":["3) Unity can be thought both formally and materially [*formaliter*, *materialiter*].",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-061":["Formal unity rests on the combination of the manifold under a principle. Material unity is the unity of the object itself. Formally, therefore, only one unity of a thing is possible (*in singulari*), for every thing has only one concept under which the manifold is brought by the understanding. Materially, however, there are unities of one and the same object, that is, as many as can be called one in each thing. Plurality in the object is therefore always material. The manifold in the representation of the object, considered in itself, is formal plurality.",["'giebt s Einheiten' read as 'giebt es Einheiten' (OCR)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-062":["Thus a play has a plurality of objects, but unity in the representation according to the laws of time, place, and so on; for without these it would serve no purpose in combining them.",["'Zweck verbinden' is slightly elliptical; I have supplied 'in combining them'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-063":["4) The Aristotelian school regards formal unity as transcendental, and, as the author also does in Sect. 4, §§ 6 and 7 [sic], assumes in the metaphysical sense the principle: *quodlibet ens est transcendentaliter unum, verum, bonum*. They predicated of the object: (a) *unum*, that is, that one thing is not many things; (b) *verum*, that is, that to every thing there really belongs what is proper to it; or, certain predicates which belong to the concept of the thing belong really to the thing, so that the predicates of the thing could be ascribed to it according to actuality, not merely according to possibility, e.g. every A has angles. This is called the proposition that every thing has truth in itself, or that in every thing there is truth: every thing has in itself nothing which does not agree with itself.",["'Sect. 4, 6 u. 7' is doubtful: possibly '§§ 6 and 7' or 'Sect. 4, § 6 f.'; I mark it [sic] rather than resolve it.","'Einheit b, verum' restated with (a), (b) enumeration; 'praedicirten' taken as 'they predicated'. 'Wirklichkeit'/'Möglichkeit' sentence is awkward in the German and was smoothed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-064":["(c) *bonum*, that is, every thing has in itself everything needed to be what it is; or, every thing is transcendentally perfect.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-065":["It is clear that here every thing has been considered only in relation to its own essence, and so far these criteria of the thing are correct, since every thing is, after all, what it is. But in the metaphysical sense, that is, considered in the relation of the thing to what is outside it, and hence to all other possible things, no perfection can be ascribed to any thing, with the exclusion of the *ens realissimum*; rather every thing has a lack of reality, hence negative perfection, or is imperfect.",["'Vollkommenheit' = perfection. 'Mangel an realität, mithin negative Vollkommenheit' kept literally; the transcript is somewhat odd here."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-066":["In the metaphysical sense [*in sensu metaphysico*] these criteria are therefore unserviceable; but as logical precepts in the consideration of an object it is important that one must see from its determination whether it has unity, truth or perfection, e.g. the formal unity of a book.",["'unnutzbar' rendered 'unserviceable'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-067":["5) Transcendental unity, on the other hand, is in every thing in so far as a manifold must be thought as combined, in order to make, according to one principle, a concept of a thing. From this formal unity arises; but formally there cannot be unities of one and the same thing. Formal unity belongs essentially to the understanding, as the faculty [*Vermögen*] of bringing the manifold of the representation under a concept.",["'das \\ ermögen' read as 'das Vermögen' (OCR)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-068":["The author in § 74 entitles the unity of the object *unitas categorica*, and in this he is right. Considered objectively, unity is a thing which is not many, which in regard to what it contains is opposed to diversity and to the many; it is therefore a material unity. Formally, however, it is hypothetical. A many, thought according to form, can be a unity, even though the objects, materially, have a plurality.",["Divided the second sentence's relative clauses; 'nennt' rendered 'entitles' per the method."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-069":["[6]) Combination of a plurality belongs to magnitude, and this is not sufficient for the concept of the *quantum*, because on this basis an aggregate of heterogeneous parts would be admitted; the concept of the *compositum*, on the other hand, is determined thereby.",["The numeral reads '0)' in the OCR; probably '6)' (or a continuing numeral) — doubtful, so bracketed. 'B<‘g> iff' read as 'Begriff'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-070":["*Compositum est unum ex conjunctione plurium*, for example a garment, however diverse in kind its parts may be.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-071":["If the combination is to involve the concept of magnitude, then the concept of homogeneity lies at its ground, and the concept of magnitude itself is determined by the word *quantum*: *est unum ex pluribus homogeneis conjunctum*. It therefore essentially contains",["The passage breaks off at the colon-less 'enthält mithin wesentlich'; the enumeration follows in the next passages."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-072":["1) *multitudinem*: so that what is regarded as a unity cannot be called a *quantum*.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-073":["2) *homogeneitatem*, that is, things of one and the same kind (*genus*); in this the *compositum* differs from the *quantum*, and the many would then be able to be a variety [*varietaet*]. *Omne quantum continet multitudinem, sed non omnis multitudo est quantum*, but only when the parts are of like kind.",["'inde differt compositum vom quanto' mixes Latin and German; kept close to the original. 'gleichartig' = 'of like kind'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-074":["That determination of a thing by which one knows a thing as a *quantum* is quantity or magnitude.",["'erkennt' rendered 'knows'; 'Sache' and 'Ding' both rendered 'thing'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-075":["NB. Quantity [*Quantitaet*], then, is nothing more than the determination of how great something is; so something can be thought as a *quantum* without letting itself be determined as to quantity, or being thereby determined.",["\"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"groß\" rendered \"great\" to keep the link with \"Größe\" (magnitude).","The second clause is restated slightly for smoothness: \"sich ... bestimmen zu laßen, oder dadurch bestimmt zu seyn\" is kept as two alternatives."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-076":["Formally, a *quantum* is a manifold in the representation which is homogeneous [*gleichartig*], and the determination of the manifold as *quantum* is quantity [*Quantitaet*].",["\"so gleichartig ist\" read as a relative clause (\"so\" = \"which\"), modifying \"Mannigfaltiges\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-077":["From this it follows:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-078":["1) There are indeed many *quanta*, but not many quantities, since *quantitas*, as determination, can be only one. *Quantitas*, like *qualitas*, is formed in a barbarous fashion from *quantum* and *qualis*. One might just as well say *quaeditas*, in order to express through all three the familiar question: *quae, qualis, quanta?* *Quidditas*, if one is so to speak, would differ from quality as the determination of the genus differs from that of the specific difference. For example, *quidditas* is the essence of the genus; but whether a thing is hard or soft belongs to quality, and so has regard to the species comprehended under the genus.",["Restated as several sentences. \"von quantum qualis gemacht\" rendered \"formed ... from *quantum* and *qualis*\"; the Latin is kept in italics.","\"wenn man so sagen soll\" is rendered \"if one is so to speak\" in the sense \"if one is to use such a word\"; the phrase is somewhat compressed in the transcript.","\"das generis weßen\" is probably an error or shorthand for \"the essence of the genus\"; translated so."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-079":["*Quaeditas* would express relation; at least it signifies this in the interrogative *quae?*, where it is meant to indicate the different relation of the concept: categorical, hypothetical or disjunctive.",["OCR \"ausdriicken\" corrected to \"ausdrücken\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-080":["2) *Qualitas* differs from quantity in this, that, and in so far as, the latter, in the case of the same object, indicates something heterogeneous [*ungleichartiges*] which is found in it in respect of other determinations. Hence *qualitas* is that determination of a thing in accordance with which what is specifically different is found under the same genus and can be distinguished from it. This is the heterogeneous, in opposition to what is not specifically different, that is, the homogeneous.",["The sentence is obscure: \"die letztere\" (quantity) as indicating something heterogeneous is awkward, and the transcript may have confused \"letztere\" with \"erstere\" (quality). Translated as it stands.","\"findt\" read as \"findet\"; \"in opposito\" rendered \"in opposition to\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-081":["3) By comparing a thing with itself and its parts we can indeed recognise that it is a *quantum*; but without comparing a thing with other things we can never determine what magnitude it actually has, or how great it is. For example, the earth has 5400 miles; the mile is [illegible fraction] of a degree; a degree is [illegible fraction] of the greatest meridian. All this can also be applied to a pea, and it is vain to seek the magnitude of the earth without comparison with a measure. Hence the definition of Wolff and of the author [Baumgarten], § 69, p. 3, *quantitas est interna entis determinatio, quae dari quidem sine alio assumto autem intelligi nequit*, can never count as a definition, however correct the proposition is and however capable of being proved; it is not a concept equivalent to magnitude, nor can it be arrived at in this manner. It is, however,",["The OCR \"JL eines Grades\" and \"~ des größten Meridians\" are garbled fractions; I have not supplied them.","\"Wolf\" given as Wolff; \"Autoris\" taken to be Baumgarten, whose Metaphysica is the textbook, supplied in brackets by inference; \"§ 69 S. 3\" left as printed (doubtful).","\"nie als Definition gelten\" and the following clause are loosely constructed; I have smoothed them and divided the sentence. \"_ wie groß\" read as \"oder wie groß\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-082":["4) striking: the concept of magnitude, or the determination of a thing that it has magnitude, is given; but magnitude as magnitude, that is, how great the object is, cannot possibly be known from the thing itself. Indeed, more still. The concept of magnitude is peculiar to the understanding, since the understanding is occupied with the combination of the homogeneous manifold. In mathematics magnitude is applied with the help of a pure intuition in sensibility, that is, through the form of space and time, in determining any figure or number. But in philosophy it cannot be determined from the concept alone whether the category of magnitude has objective reality; that is, it cannot be known that a many together make up one.",["The \"4)\" continues the \"Es ist indeß\" of the previous passage; the sentence is therefore left in this fragmentary form.","OCR corrections: \"d. 1.\" to \"d. i.\"; \"lorm\" to \"Form\"; \"B.-griff\" to \"Begriff\"; \"gerade zu eigen\" taken as \"geradezu eigen\" (peculiarly proper to).","\"Vieles zusammen Eins ausmache\" rendered \"a many together make up one\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-083":["It is absolutely necessary to call in the aid of intuition *a priori*, as in mathematics. All the categories, like that of magnitude, are of such a kind that it cannot be proved through their concept itself how far it is possible that the concept should also have objective reality. This proof must be sought in the sensible intuition of space and time. Through this alone does extensive magnitude too obtain reality.",["OCR \"Biesen\" corrected to \"Diesen\"; \"Anschauung a priori\" kept with \"*a priori*\" in italics.","\"mit derjenigen von der Größe von der Art\" smoothed to \"like that of magnitude, are of such a kind\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-084":["*Extensio* differs from magnitude of time; or it is a manifold outside one another, as, for example, present time is opposed to future time.",["The sense of the last clause is obscure in the transcript (the example seems to illustrate succession rather than extension); translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-085":["5) If one pays particular attention to magnitude in the case of numbers, one can determine a *quantum* through *aliquoties facta positio ejusdem* [the positing of the same thing made a number of times].",["The bracketed English gloss is supplied by me."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-086":["*Successive unum uni addendo multitudinem cognoscimus* [we know a multitude by successively adding one to one], that is, by counting we know a multitude; with every number, then, a multitude is present. If this goes on to infinity there arises a *multitudo omni numero major* [a multitude greater than every number]. This one may well call an infinite multitude, but not an infinite number. For just because of its infinity it cannot be expressed in numbers, and the expression therefore involves a *contradictio in adjecto*. Such an infinite multitude is, however, also a *multitudo omni cognitione major* [a multitude greater than all knowledge], for we can have a concept only of a determinate magnitude.",["\"unendliehe\" corrected to \"unendliche\". English glosses of the Latin are supplied in brackets."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-087":["*Numerus* is, in fact, a *multitudo numerando (unum uni addendo) cognita* [a multitude known by numbering, by adding one to one]. To count on to infinity is impossible; then something ceases to lie within the limits of a number as soon as it can be counted no further. &",["\"in's\" modernised; the final \"&\" is left as printed (probably an abbreviation for \"etc.\")."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-088":["6) In order, then, to know a magnitude, it is necessary that the concept of measure be connected with it at the same time. For that something is a *quantum* expresses merely something which determines the quality of the thing; on the other hand, without measure it is impossible to represent to oneself what *quantum* has been produced by the composition.",["\"qualitaet\" rendered \"quality\" as printed, though one might expect \"quantity\"; left as it stands and flagged as doubtful."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-089":["The category of magnitude cannot be known with certainty to have objective reality also, unless an object is presented to it through which meaning is given to it.",["\"dahin\" (to this effect) is absorbed in the English; the sentence is restated for smoothness."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-090":["Hence: *mensura* is the *unum, quod numerando quantitatem reddit cognoscibilem* [the unit which, by numbering, renders quantity knowable]. Just as little would one see that there are three specifically different ways of measuring a magnitude, if one did not have an example in space, in its length, height and thickness.",["English gloss of the Latin supplied in brackets."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-091":["Time, on the other hand, has only one dimension [*dimensio*], and this we represent to ourselves as length, and depict figuratively by the sign of a line. The line itself presents all its points simultaneously, but the sign of the line expresses the successive sequence, or the changes, in time.",["\"Abmeßung\" rendered \"dimension\", with Kant's Latin parallel."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-092":["7) A magnitude is now either",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-093":["a) a *quantum assignabile*, which is also called *quantum dabile*. This is the magnitude which can be presented intuitively in its entirety (in its totality); every measure must be so constituted, and hence such a magnitude must also be capable of being determined in its relation by means of the measure; or",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-094":["b) a *quantum omni assignatione majus* [a quantum greater than every assignment], or",["English gloss supplied in brackets."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-095":["c) [*quantum omni assignatione*] *minus*, that is, an infinitely great or infinitely small magnitude; hence they also signify *quantum infinitum* or *infinito-parvum*, and so, since both are respectively greater and respectively smaller than every magnitude that can be specified, neither is a *quantum assignabile*, and neither can be given.",["The words in square brackets are supplied from the parallel in (b); the source has only \"minus\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-096":["8) The ground why a magnitude is not finite is entitled a negation; the negation on account of which a *quantum* is not the maximum may be called either limitation [*Einschränkung*] or boundary [*Begränzung*]. Only an object of reason is called restricted [*beschränkt*], but an object of intuition bounded [*begränzt*]; likewise also unrestricted and unbounded, according as it is an object of mere reason or of the senses.",["The German pairs Schranke/Einschränkung/beschränkt versus Gränze/Begränzung/begränzt; I render \"restricted/limitation\" and \"bounded/boundary\" to keep the distinction Kant draws.","\"Gegenstand der Vernunft\" kept as \"object of reason\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-097":["Restrictions [*Schranken*], then, differ from boundaries [*Gränzen*]. A being of the understanding (*noumenon*) has only restrictions (*limites*), that is, the negation is thought merely in accordance with the understanding; hence there is here limitation [*Einschränkung*]. A *quantum noumenon* is therefore a magnitude thought through the understanding, and is restricted in so far as it is not the maximum; and the negation on account of which it is restricted is entitled restriction, *limes*. For example, the boundaries or restrictions of the human understanding and will. —",["\"Verstandes Wesen\" rendered \"being of the understanding\". In the example Kant uses \"Gränzen oder Schranken\" interchangeably; kept as printed.","OCR \"menschlichenVerstandes\" separated."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-098":["A *quantum phaenomenon*, on the other hand, is bounded when the determination of its magnitude, or the negation, belongs at the same time to its *intuitus*, or when the ground of the restriction at the same time contains something positive in space.",["\"oder = wenn\" read as \"oder wenn\"; the \"=\" is taken as an OCR mark or an equivalence sign. \"phaenomenon\" kept in Latin italics."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-099":["The positive element by which the limits [*Schranken*] of intuition are determined is entitled the boundary [*Gränze*]. The point in space has in itself no negation, nor is it itself bounded; it is merely positive, and indicates the place to which something is limited.",["*Schranken* rendered 'limits' and *Gränze* 'boundary', to keep the contrast with *Einschränkung* ('limitation') in the next passage.","'is entitled' for *heißt*; 'wohin etwas sich einschränkt' rendered 'to which something is limited' (reflexive smoothed)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-100":["The line obtains its boundary through the point, the surface through the line, and the body through the surface. These are also the three dimensions of space, if we think it completely, by itself, as a part of a larger space; and then the solid content, the surface and the length each have again their own space. Bounding [*Begränzung*] and boundary are therefore always something positive in the appearance, but limitation [*Einschränckung*] is not.",["'Fläche' rendered 'surface'. 'Körperlicher Inhalt' rendered 'solid content'; the sentence is obscure and may be a faulty transcription.","'wenn man ihn vollständig als einen Theil eines großem Raumes' — OCR 'großem' read as *größeren*; I take the sense as 'larger'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-101":["9) *Quantum est vel continuum vel discretum.* A *quantum continuum* is a continuous magnitude, all of whose parts are again *quanta*, however small or however large they may be.",["'Größe' rendered 'magnitude'; *Quantum* kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-102":["It cannot, therefore, contain simple parts. Composition, on the other hand, is so constituted *quod sunt partes per se indeterminatae*, that is, in themselves; and from the magnitude itself it cannot be determined how many parts we are to assume, but so far as our knowledge goes it remains arbitrary.",["The Latin clause is kept as written; its grammar is loose (*quod* for *ut*?), and the sentence may be corrupt. 'Composition' (*Composition*) is the transcript's word; the sense seems to concern division into parts.","'nach unserer Erkenntniß' rendered 'so far as our knowledge goes'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-103":["To the *quanta continua* belong, then, space and time. For every part of space and time can be thought only as again a space or a time, and the division proceeds as far as the point and the moment; but these two are not parts of space and of time.",["Reading 'als wie wieder' as 'as again'. 'nur beyde letztere' rendered 'but these two'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-104":["A point is only the positive, the boundary of a space; and the moment is precisely the same in respect of time.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-105":["From this it follows that space and time do not consist of simple parts. Were the opposite correct, this circumstance would prove that space and time were something objective in the juxtaposition [*neben einander*] and succession [*nach einander*] of things; but since no simple part can be met with, it proves that space and time lie only in the merely subjective [element], the form of intuition.",["The sentence was restated slightly for smoothness; 'im neben einander und nach einander' is awkward and 'of things' is supplied in the translation.","'[element]' supplied."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-106":["The point in space is indivisible, and, as has been said, it is also not a part of space. Hence a space consisting of nothing but points would be a space made up of nothing but boundaries, and so would be a nothing. The point is merely a position [*Stelle*] in space; but up to that point space, like time up to the moment, is divisible, and it is therefore impossible to assume simple parts in space and time.",["'bis dahin' rendered 'up to that point', i.e. up to the point (or moment) as limit of division."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-107":["A point cannot be thought prior to the representation of space, from which it follows that space cannot consist of points. Space fills matter as *extensum*; but *extensum* is *id, in quo est spatium*. The concepts are therefore correlated [*correlata*] with matter; space itself makes matter extended. Matter, then, can consist of no other parts than space itself has, and it is therefore impossible that matter should be able to consist of simple parts.",["'Der Raum füllt die Materie aus als extensum' is obscure and perhaps faultily transcribed (one expects matter fills space); translated as it stands. 'Die Begriffe' presumably refers to space and extension.","'extensum, extensum. aber' read as a repetition of the term followed by *aber*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-108":["A *quantum discretum*, on the other hand, is a quantum consisting of units, that is, of a determinate multitude of parts which I think as units. This is the concept of number; accordingly a magnitude expressed by numbers is a *quantum discretum*. For example, a quantum of gulden: here no further division is possible without the unit, the gulden, ceasing altogether; the parts therefore do not again contain *quanta*. It would be otherwise if this were thought of a quantum of silver in general, in *Schotten* [?], ounces and so forth; the latter can be divided into *quanta* and is therefore a *quantum continuum*.",["'Schotten' is doubtful (possibly *Lothen*, 'half-ounces', misread by OCR); left as it stands. The clause 'So wie dies … gedacht seyn würde' was recast.","'Gulden' left untranslated (a coin)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-109":["The *quantum discretum* can be regarded as *per se discretum*, because it consists of simple parts, that is, because its units cannot again be considered as *quanta*; they must, however, be expressed by a number in order to represent the quantity, that is, in order to know how great the quantum is.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-110":["10.) Quantum 1",["Fragmentary heading; the trailing '1' is probably an OCR remnant of the bracket marking the pair *Quantum / Quantitas*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-111":["*Quantitas* is either rational or irrational. The rational is *quantum computabile*, that is, the magnitude which can be calculated or expressed in numbers; the irrational, on the other hand, is the *quantum finitum*, but *incommensurabile*, that is, one which cannot be calculated, or whose relation to the measure cannot be expressed by numbers. It can only approximate to the determination which the measure gives; for example, the relation of the root of 1 to the root of 2, which obtains between the diagonal and the two sides, is immeasurable.",["Garbled OCR: 'Quantitas j lst Vel rationale>' read as '*Quantitas* is either rational …'. 'Wurzel j1 zu |/2' read as √1 to √2; 'Diagonal Lime' as *Diagonal-Linie*. Strictly the ratio is that of the diagonal to the side, i.e. √2 to 1.","'Verhältniß' rendered 'relation'; 'Bestimmung' 'determination'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-112":["These latter magnitudes in space and time are given to intuition only between the boundaries of space and time. &",["The final '&' (etc.) is kept as in the transcript. The sentence is terse; 'der Anschauung' taken as dative with 'gegeben'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-113":["11.) A *quantum maximum*, like a *quantum minimum*, cannot be thought of a *ens phaenomenon*, and a representation of it can be ascribed only to an *ens noumenon*, God. For a *quantum maximum* would be allness [*Allheit*], the magnitude thought without limitation. Such a thing is as little thinkable for the human understanding as that magnitude whose still smaller measure would be, as it were, a nothing. For example, an allness of space and of time, which would be no part of a still larger space and time, is not capable of any subjective intuition. The phenomenon can be thought only as a plurality [*Vielheit*], and under this concept an allness.",["*ente phaenomeno*, *ente noumeno* are ablatives; given in nominative in the translation.","'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Verstand' = understanding. 'Subjectiven Anschauung' retained literally."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-114":["For phenomena there is therefore no *quantum absolute maximum*, but only ever a relative maximum, for example the *chorda maxima in circulo*; and just as little is there a *quantum absolute minimum*. For all parts, even the smallest that can be represented, again have *quanta*; hence here too the representation is directed only to a relation.",["'minimuni' in the OCR read as *minimum*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-115":["On the concept of the relation of things, according to which alone we can know their magnitude, rests the concept of *magnitudo*, *parvitas*, which is different from that of the *quantum*. These involve the determination of the *quantum* or of quantity, according to which something is called great (*magnum*) or small (*parvum*).",["'Verhältniß' = relation; 'Bestimmung' = determination. 'Begriff von Magnitudo, parvitas' rendered as one pair."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-116":["If we suppose that some things in the universe were to become ever smaller while all the rest remained unchanged, we represent a relation in which the smaller things stand to the larger. For example, if our earth, which is called a *globus terraquaeus*, were so flooded by water that only the size of a pea remained, the change would be representable, through comparison, also in respect of magnitude. But if we suppose that the whole universe has changed, no further comparison is possible, and so no change of magnitude is thinkable; the smallest terrestrial globe would be equal in size to the largest, with regard to our subjective representation, because we could not compare it with a third object.",["'großem' (OCR) read as *größern*. The example is restated slightly for smoothness; 'Gegenstände' rendered 'object'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-117":["The concept of magnitude would therefore remain in itself the same, and so is absolute; but the *magnitudo* or *parvitas* can never be known absolutely, but only through relation.",["*Begriff von Größe* is kept distinct from *magnitudo/parvitas*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-118":["From the fact, then, that we are unable to determine the magnitude of things in and by themselves, it follows that we cannot represent them to ourselves in and by themselves either, but only as they appear to us. Space, and the things which occupy space, are therefore determined solely in accordance with the laws of sensibility, to which they are put in relation. Space and time are accordingly also not thinkable through the understanding, and hence through determinate concepts of the understanding, but are merely the subjective form under which things appear to us. They belong not [only?] to intuition through sensibility and not to the understanding as a concept of the understanding.",["The last sentence is faulty or elliptical in the transcript ('nicht für die Anschauung … und nicht für den Verstand'); translated as it stands, with a doubtful gloss in brackets. 'und daher bestimmte Verstandes Begriffen' smoothed.","'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'erscheinen' = appear."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-001":["Category of Quality",["Heading only; the next section begins here."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-002":["22.) Quality, even when applied to objects, relates, like the corresponding logical function of quality, to judgments that contain the condition of the quality of a thing that is to be determined. Space and time contain in themselves no quality of a thing; that is, they have in themselves no transcendental matter, nor an object in general. They are only the formal condition of our intuition. A something, on the other hand, which lies in the concept of being [*Seyn*], existence [*Daseyn*, *Existenz*], belongs to the quality of the thing, which accordingly presupposes something material. Quality, as a function, has a threefold object, that is, the representation of a thing .... . Being involves",["The \"....\" after \"representation of a thing\" stands in the source; the first member of the threefold division (reality) is not present, and nothing is supplied. The passage breaks off at \"Seyn involvirt\".","Seyn, Daseyn and existenz are rendered \"being\", \"existence\" and \"*Existenz*\" in one run; the transcript's distinctions among them are not clear here.","I joined \"d. i.\" clauses with semicolons and divided the long final sentence; no content was added."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-003":["2.) Negation, that is, the representation of a thing as something that contains a non-being [*Nichtseyn*].",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-004":["3.) Limitation, that is, the representation of a thing whose being is affected by its non-being, and whose concept accordingly contains being and non-being combined. For example: light is reality; darkness is negation; shadow is limitation, for it is a darkness that is bounded by light. So also with knowledge, ignorance, and restricted knowing [*eingeschränktes Wißen*].",["*Kenntniße* is rendered \"knowledge\" and *Wißen* \"knowing\" to keep the two apart; the German does not distinguish sharply."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-005":["It is a positive something, in opposition to negation [*in opposito mit Negatio*]. Something, or combined.",["The sentence is fragmentary and probably garbled; \"Etwas oder Verbunden\" is given literally, and no sense has been supplied. The antecedent of \"It\" is unclear."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-006":["1.) The reality of a thing is, according to the meaning of the word, the thinghood [*Sachheit*] of the thing, and accordingly something positive in itself.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-007":["2.) To give the concept of reality meaning, an object of sensibility must be placed under it, so that it can be substantiated through it. The category of magnitude corresponded to the concept of space and time as a something in intuition, in order to determine them as to quantity; and so too in the representation quantity [*Quantitaet*] is necessary, but only as that which comprehends sensation. The real is something sensible in intuition, and an object of sensation is therefore required in order to represent to oneself the magnitude of the real. It is not so with quantity, where pure intuition was present, that is, without an object in space or in time. Thus, for example, a thought has quantity as intuition in time, but a clear head [*ein heller Kopf*] has reality, on account of the subjective sensations of its representations.",["The syntax of the first long sentence is irregular; I divided it and smoothed it, keeping the sense. The clause \"so ist auch in der Vorstellung Quantitaet nothwendig\" may be a slip for *Qualitaet* or *Realitaet*; I follow the text as printed.","*Belegt* is rendered \"substantiated\" (literally \"documented, covered\")."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-008":["3.) The real has a degree of sensation, or [a degree] in the sensible element of the sensuous representation. Sensation is opposed to the formal element in intuition, and the sensible then signifies the material in the transcendental sense. Hence here the formal intuition in space and time is opposed to the real as the sensible. Sensation thus here comes to be added to mere intuition; but an intuition with sensation is perception [*Wahrnehmung*]. This, however, requires an object of perception. The perception of an object is consciousness of the object through sensation. In kind, therefore, this intuition of the real differs entirely from the pure.",["OCR \"Empfindüng\" and \"Wahrnehnehmung\" silently corrected.","*Das empfindbare* rendered \"the sensible\" (that which can be sensed)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-009":["This degree of sensation is entitled intensive magnitude, in order to distinguish it from the extensive magnitude of quantity. It is said: everything sensible has intensive magnitude, or a degree of sensation. That is to say, it can be represented that sensation may rise from zero (= nought), which has no sensation at all, or may decrease again from a certain amount to 0. By this, however, is understood a magnitude in which the parts are not first known in order to determine the magnitude. Rather it must be known as a unity, and the parts must be drawn out of the unity. Thus, for example, a line, which has to be put together, differs from a light that is dying out. In the latter there is only one unity of sensation, but in each successive state a different degree of it.",["I divided the sentence beginning \"Es wird aber…\" and resolved *wobey* as \"in which\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-010":["The author [Baumgarten] entitles the degree of sensation the magnitude of quality. This designation does express that something is thought as posited in the thing, but the degree is better determined as magnitude of unity. That is, the representation of an object, in so far as I think its magnitude (*quantitatem*) as unity, gives the magnitude its degree. Here, then, magnitude is given not as multiplicity but as unity, and is thus exactly distinguished from extensive magnitude (*quantitas extensiva*, and therefore different in its consequences from *quantitas intensiva*). Hence, for example, a drop of boiling water is indeed less than a kettleful, but both are equally hot. The unity which is here perceived in the sensation of the object thus shows that it rests on the equality of the ground, and the unity of the ground makes the homogeneous manifold quite dispensable in considering reality. So an angle is equally large however far the lines or sides may extend. What matters is the equality of the inclination, and a difference in this is only a difference of intensive magnitude, since this reduces simply to the existence of the movement in large and small angles. An enlivening thought, which however gradually reverses its impression, has unity, but one which is different at each step of the decrease.",["\"Der Autor\" is taken to be Baumgarten (the textbook); the transcript does not name him here.","The sentences \"Es macht die Gleichheit der Inclination…\" and \"Ein belebender Gedanke…\" are syntactically broken and probably corrupt; I give the most natural sense. *Verkehrt* (\"reverses\") may be a mishearing or miscopying for something like *verliert*.","The OCR gap in \"GrößederEinheit,d.i.\" was silently repaired."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-011":["4.) It now follows from this that the real, since it has its ground in sensation, and so in the object of the senses, cannot have place in the merely intellectual. Consequently the degree of the real cannot be thought either as a maximum or as a minimum. On the other hand, it is certain that the variation of the degree of the intensive magnitude of real quality must be infinite, even though it may be imperceptible. Hence between a determinate degree A and 0 (zero) there must be found an infinite number of qualities of the real, in a degree that is, though imperceptible. For example, knowing, representations, and even the consciousness of man have many degrees, without its being possible to determine the smallest.",["OCR \"ün blos\" read as \"im blos\" (in the merely); \"müßcn\" as \"müßen\". The clause \"in einem, wenngleich unmerklichen Grade\" is rendered loosely."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-012":["5.) The word reality is used in a twofold sense:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-013":["1.) Adjectivally, and then it signifies only the form of the object, and is accordingly applied formally [*formaliter*]; it can then be used only in the singular [*in singulari*]. For example: representations and concepts have objective reality. Magnitude is here reality, and belongs to the form of the concept, in so far as it has an object,",["\"Größe ist hier Realitaet\" is obscure; given as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-014":["2.) or substantively, and then reality relates to the material of the object and is usable only in the plural [*in plurali*], because the realities of the thing are considered in themselves.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-015":["The word unity is likewise used in different ways in the German, Greek or Latin language.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-016":["Namely adjectivally: one says, for example, unity of the play, of the delivery, truth of the proposition, perfection of the thing. It signifies the point of view which one lays at the basis in regard to the object. Substantively, on the other hand, or as the unities of the things themselves, that is, *unitates quantitative spectatae*, [unities] are named only in the plural. So *quantitas* is usable only formally and in the singular, but *quanta* in the plural; for these concern the quality of the object itself, but the former only the form of an object in general.",["The printed \"der Vollkommenheit des Dinges\" (genitive) is read as parallel to the other instances: \"perfection of the thing\". \"[unities]\" is supplied. *Quantitative* may be an OCR/transcript slip for *quantitative*/*quantitatis*; left as printed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-017":["6.) All realities are also entitled perfections, that is, each single perfection in the object itself (so that this expression also differs from the perfection of things as a generic concept in general). Objectively, finally, reality consists in thinghood or something positive; and what is positive is perfect. An object must have something positive, and can have much that is positive, or perfections. An *ens mere negativum*, that is, one that had nothing positive at all, is a plain contradiction, for even the being of the thing already involves something positive; otherwise, if this too were negative, it would be no thing, the object being here considered only materially. For formally [*formaliter*] an *ens mere negativum* can at least be thought without contradiction. Every thing must therefore have reality; and an *ens realissimum*, which is also called *ens perfectissimum*, *summum*, is that all of whose determinations [*determinationes*] are realities, and in which, therefore, there is nothing negative.",["I divided the long sentences; \"mithin\" rendered \"so that\" / \"accordingly\" by the argument. The clause \"sonst wäre es, wenn auch dies negativ wäre, kein Ding\" is slightly irregular and smoothed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-018":["*In sensu transcendentali.* Metaphysics therefore sees that what is here laid at the basis is merely the concept of not-being, and so an analytic knowledge of the thing; yet in such a way that the thing still remains thinkable and is therefore not subject to the *principium contradictionis*. *Realiter opposita*, however, are *contrario opposita*, and they must be investigated in accordance with synthetic principles of ground and consequence, *sive rationis ponentis non tollentis*.",["Kant's \"Grund zur Folge\" rendered \"ground and consequence\"; the sentence is divided at \"Realiter opposita\" for clarity.","The passage opens mid-argument; \"In sensu transcendentali\" is left as a Latin phrase standing alone.","\"Metaphysic\" rendered \"Metaphysics\"; \"zum Grunde gelegt\" = \"laid at the basis\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-019":["The Wolffian philosophy, because reality constitutes the material element of a thing, entitled negation the formal element of things. Negation, even in the things themselves, would constitute nothing more than the formal element, whether it arose from a *contrarie* or a *contradictorie oppositum*, if the opposed realities (not, that is, in a merely logical sense) could be reduced to a negation. For example, the Wolffians say that sin consists merely in the omission of the good, or the absence of the good, and is therefore formally the not-good; they thus regard the *contrarie oppositum* of the latter as negation. It therefore involves the idea of the *malum metaphysicum*. [Since] in objects of appearance [*Erscheinung*] realities and negations are found mixed, [there is] a negation, *quatenus ens non est ens realissimum*.",["The last sentence is fragmentary in the German (\"Da ... eine Negation\"); I have translated what stands and supplied only \"[there is]\" in brackets. The text may be corrupt or the transcriber's shorthand.","\"nannte\" rendered \"entitled\"; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"realia et negativa\" rendered \"realities and negations\".","\"Es involvirt\": antecedent unclear (the Wolffian view of sin, or negation); I render \"It\" as the Wolffian doctrine's consequence, leaving it open.","\"mali metaphysici\" is Latin genitive; given in nominative in the translation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-001":["Categories of the dynamical class",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-002":["23.) There appears in the logical relation of the categories of this class to those of the mathematical class a difference. The categories of relation and modality bring with them nothing but correlates, which are so placed side by side that, *posito uno ponitur et alter*. For example, substances are related to accidents and *vice versa*, cause to effect, and likewise interaction [*Wechselwirkung*] directly to one another. In the case of the categories of quantity and quality, however, they were single, subordinated to one another, and could not be placed side by side. For example, unity bears no relation to plurality; rather, unity lies in plurality, and the two together again constitute totality. Just as little are reality and negation compatible side by side, whereas, on the other hand, the modalities in their correlates cancel one another: possible—not possible, actual—not actual, necessary—contingent, *contrarie opposita*.",["Kant's \"est alter\" in the OCR corrected silently to \"et alter\" (sense requires it); original may read \"est\" — doubtful.","Stray line numbers (5, 10) omitted.","\"Unter -schied\" and \"auf heben\" repaired as OCR breaks.","\"Verhältniß\" = relation; \"Wechselwirkung\" = interaction (Kemp Smith: \"community\" in the Critique, but \"interaction\" retained here for the lecture context)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-003":["The categories of relation in particular have as their content the knowledge of the things themselves in their relation to the object, whereas the categories of modality concern only the knowledge of the concept of the thing in relation to the whole faculty of knowledge.",["\"Erkenntniß Vermögen\" = faculty of knowledge.","\"Gegenstande\" = object; the sense of \"in ihrem Verhältniß zum Gegenstande\" is compressed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-004":["24.) In the categories of relation in particular, then, the realities are considered in relation to one another as relations, that is, the relations of things with regard to their existence, since a reality of the thing always involves the concept of being, and indeed in accordance with the threefold modes of existence in the series of logical judgments, namely assertoric, hypothetical, disjunctive; [it] is grounded on the relation of the antecedent to the relation",["The passage breaks off or is corrupt at the end (\"gründet sich auf Relation des antecedens zum Verhältniß\"): subject unclear and the final word may be a misreading; translated as it stands.","Between this and the next passage a short passage (s06-005) is not supplied."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-006":["In this they therefore differ from the categories of [qua]lity above, where this [i.e. quality] was indeed compared in various ways without regard to the relation of several things.",["The OCR \"Categorien der lität\" is broken; I supply \"[qua]lity\" in brackets, judging from the contrast with relation.","\"diese ... verglichen wurde\": the singular verb with \"diese\" is irregular; I take the subject to be quality."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-007":["25.) The first category is the relation of substance to accident.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-008":["This relation is called *subsistentia* with regard to the substance, and *inhaerentia* with regard to the accident. *Inhaerentia* is an *interna determinatio substantiae*, that is, a determination [*Bestimmung*] which belongs to the substance by inhering; hence substance is one and the same with that *quod ei inhaeret*. For example, the man (substance) thinks, *est id quod homini tamquam substantiae ejus inhaerendo competit*. The expression 'dependence', which, because no better is available, is used for the *caussata* in respect of the *ratio causae* in the relation of causality, must not be confused with inherence. The latter also depends on the substance, but as a necessary inner determination of the substance; causality, on the other hand, does not necessarily involve dependence in itself. For example, thought in man is an essential determination; but the warmth of the stone from the sunlight does not inhere in the latter, nor is it necessarily connected with the stone, but exists as something distinct from the substance. The sound does not inhere in the speaker, nor in the hearer, *substantialiter*, but subsists in a manner of its own. So it is also in the case of interaction.",["\"substantive\" read as *substantiae*; \"caussatis ratione causa\" kept in Latin as transcribed (likely *causatis ratione causae*).","\"dependirt auch von der Substanz\" in the original sentence is compressed; the sentence \"The latter...\" is restated for clarity.","\"Z.E.\" rendered \"For example\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-009":["There are properly three categories of relation:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-010":["1.) the relation of inherence to subsistence",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-011":["2.) the relation of *causalitas* to dependence",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-012":["3.) community [*Gemeinschaft*], that is, that two substances have in themselves at the same time the determination of cause and effect [*causae et caussati*].",["\"der Bestimmung der causae und caußati\" is irregular; \"Gemeinschaft\" rendered \"community\" as in Kemp Smith's Critique."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-013":["Hence in relation one lays at the basis the consideration of a thing, partly as subject, and in so far as it cannot be a predicate of another, partly as ground of another thing, partly in so far as it is reciprocally determined with another thing.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-014":["The concept of substance requires a special elucidation [*Erörterung*], since there lies in it a source of knowledge for many metaphysical truths.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-015":["Substance is that which can exist without being a predicate of another thing (*quod potest esse, etiam si non existit tanquam determinatio alterius*).",["The Latin is as transcribed (\"eße\", \"tan quam\"); the OCR/original Latin is slightly odd (\"etiam si non existit\"), and is left as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-016":["Accident, on the other hand, is that which cannot exist without being a predicate of another thing. Hence",["Breaks off at \"Daher\", leading into the numbered points that follow."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-017":["1.) Substance and accident are each something real, since their concept contains a being, and the existence of the thing, whether it be substance or accident, is necessary to it. A thing, therefore, whose concept contains a not-being (negation) can be neither substance nor accident, precisely because reality and existence are lacking to it.",["\"daßen\" read as \"dessen\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-018":["For example: body is real",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-019":["motion is real",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-020":["rest, on the other hand, is negation, and therefore cannot be an accident of a body. The existence of the substance is therefore called subsistence, and the existence of its accident inherence; that is, the accident belongs to the substance as a positive, real predicate in such a way that it inheres in it.",["\"accidentis\" is Latin genitive in a German context; rendered \"its accident\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-001":["Scholium.",["OCR spacing \"S c h o 1 i o n\" repaired."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-002":["The Scholastics divided the accident, or *inhaerens*, into 1.) *praedicabile*, by which they understood the accident of the essence; accordingly the *accidens praedicabile* was equal to the contingent. For the essence of a thing is that which necessarily belongs to the concept of a thing; and since the accident was the opposite in this respect, it was equal to the non-essential, *extraessentiale*.",["\"Zufälligen\" = the contingent; \"Wesen\" = essence."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-003":["2.) *praedicamentale*, by which they understood the accident of substance; it was accordingly something real and positive inhering in the substance. The two are distinguished also in that the *accidens praedicabile* lies only in the concept [and was there], whereas the *praedicamentale* exists and is predicated of a real thing. For example, the figure of a square [or place, *Plazzes*] was an *accidens praedicabile*; it contains no existence, but rests only on the mere form or mode of representation [*Vorstellungs Art*] of the *Plaz*, to which, in respect of the matter, it was wholly indifferent.",["\"Plazzes\" is doubtful (perhaps \"Platzes\", a square or a place; possibly misreading of \"Platzes\"/\"Plazes\"); I render \"square\" with the alternative bracketed, and leave it as *Plaz* in the last clause.","\"und da war\" is obscure (perhaps \"and was there\" or a transcriber's slip); bracketed and left unresolved.","\"esetwas\" read as \"es etwas\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-004":["The weight of a body, on the other hand, is an *accidens praedicamentale*, for it exists by inhering in the body. So also warmth and cold are *res existentes inhaerendo* as accidents of it; the body itself, however, exists in their regard by subsisting [*subsistendo*].",["\"accidentien\" rendered \"accidents\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-005":["2.) Substance, thought with the omission of all inhering accidents (that is, of their determinations), is entitled the *substantiale*. This remainder is a mere concept which has no determination. It is a something which can only be thought, or represented; for the *substantiale* cannot be known. Nothing can be known unless one has predicates of the object, through which one knows something of it, since all modes of knowledge come about only through judgments. Here, however, only the subject *absque praedicato* remains, and consequently no relation between the two. What remains, then, is only a representation of a something of which one does not know what it is. For example, if from a body one removes all accidents, such as divisibility, impenetrability, extensibility, there remains a figure, that is, a shape in space, which however does not exist; for a space, such as this figure is, cannot exist for itself (*subsistendo*), and since space is thought only through bodies as that which inheres [*inhaerens*], nothing substantial that exists remains.",["Restated the opening as separate sentences; 'heißt' rendered 'is entitled'.","'Bestimmung' rendered 'determination'; 'Erkenntniße' 'modes of knowledge'.","'so eine Gestalt im Raum ist' read as 'that is, a shape in space'; 'dergleichen sie doch ist' is obscure and I took it as 'such as this figure is' (i.e. itself a space-determination). Doubtful.","'Gedacht, oder vorstellbar' rendered 'thought, or represented'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-006":["Thus thinking, willing, and the feeling of pleasure and displeasure are predicates of the human soul. If one omits these and thinks the soul without these inhering [*inhaerentia*], there remains a something of which one has no concept, a thought without a thinking subject; and this is the *substantiale*. It is also called the *substratum* of all accidents. To know objects in this, as already said, is impossible for the human understanding, and one makes an idle complaint about its limitation, by virtue of which the understanding is supposed to be able to know only through effects, and not objects in themselves and in their substance. It lies in the quality of the faculty of understanding that we can know only through predicates, which here, however, are left out; and hence all knowledge without the connection of the accidents with the substance is impossible. [breaks off]",["Text ends '{bricht ab)' in OCR; rendered as editorial '[breaks off]'.","'Subjecte' (plural form in source) taken as singular 'subject'.","The clause 'vermöge dessen der Verstand ... erkennen können' is irregular (singular subject, plural verb); I smoothed it.","'Verstandes-Vermögen' rendered 'faculty of understanding'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-007":["*Phaenomena substantiata*, an expression of Leibniz (ad autorem p. 193), means nothing more than substance considered as phenomenon, or reality as determination in space and time. All substances are known and considered by us in so far as they can be determined in space and time; we cannot know their predicates in themselves, but only in so far as they stand in relation to the form of our sensibility. Hence *substantiae noumenon* cannot be known, because the object corresponding to the concept is lacking in intuition. Hence, since the *substantialia* do not exist in themselves, we cannot know substances in themselves, but only through their inhering accidents. For example, through the representation 'I' nothing can be known of the subject without ascribing a predicate to it. It serves only as a designation of the representation of a being that makes itself its own object. [This is known] only through observation of myself, when I direct my attention to inner sense, which can be presented as phenomenon just as outer sense can. If, therefore, one knows substances by their determinations in space and time, and takes these determinations for the thing itself, one confuses, through this illusion, the concept of substances with that of the *phaenomena substantiata*. [breaks off]",["'Durch Beobachtung meiner selbst nur...' is an incomplete sentence; I supplied '[This is known]' in brackets.","'phaenomenis substantiatis' in the source is ungrammatical; silently normalised to *phaenomena substantiata*.","'Erscheinung' not used; 'phaenomenon' left in Latin/Greek form as in source.","'ad autorem' refers to Baumgarten's text (the author), page 193 as given.","'vorstellig machen' rendered 'presented'. 'bricht ab' rendered as '[breaks off]'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-008":["Since the world is an aggregate of many substances connected with one another, that is, a real whole, as soon as it is considered in relation to the senses in space and time, there can, as *mundus phaenomenon*, be only one world, on account of the unity of unbounded space. In respect of form, then, the world is a *totum substantiale quod non est pars alterius* . . .",["'es der Welten' is odd; I read it as 'of worlds there can be only one'.","Ellipsis dots are in the source."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-009":[". . . another world than the present one cannot be assumed as possible *actu* . . . If, now, one thinks the world as noumenon, it is nothing further than an absolute whole of substances; but one is then also unable to determine *a priori* what properties or determinations it has. If, however, one thinks the world as phenomenon, and consequently things in space and time in their real relations, in which they must stand to one another, the following four principles can be laid down, under which the determinations of the world must be thought: *In mundo non datur* 1. *abyssus*, 2. *saltus*, 3. *casus* (blind chance), [4.] *fatum* (blind necessity).",["The source numbers only three items, joining 'Casus' and 'fatum'; since four principles are announced, I supplied '[4.]' in brackets.","'Phänomenon' rendered 'phenomenon' in keeping with the Latin-derived term; 'Determinationen' rendered 'determinations'.","'als ihren reellen Verhältnissen' smoothed to 'in their real relations'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-010":["This was also the opinion of Wolff and Leibniz. One sees well enough that this idea, thought through the mere understanding, may hold as correct in itself, and in so far it can be thought that *compositum substantiale consistit ex simplicibus*. As phenomenon, however, there is here an extended substance, something permanent in space, which cannot be thought without parts and without division to infinity. The monads, however, cannot in space constitute parts of the body, but must be mere points, since otherwise they would be parts of a space . . . [breaks off]",["'Dies' presumably refers to the preceding principles (context not given); left as 'This'.","The final clause is doubtful: 'mere points' and 'parts of a space' are as the text stands; the argument is cut off.","'gedenken' read as 'denken'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-011":["The substances in the world must have a reciprocal influence upon one another, that is, must stand in *nexu reali*, which can take place only through interaction with one another. This *nexus realis per commercium* could not be assumed possible among things if one thinks them through the understanding as existing in themselves. Each substance would then exist for itself, without any relation or connection with the others. Hence a *totum reale* of necessary substances cannot be thought at all. For then none would depend upon another in respect of its existence; each exists for itself, because each has in itself the necessarily sufficient ground of its existence. Many necessary substances would therefore have no connection among themselves; each could be only a world, and the primary cause of a world, but with another world and the things in it they could stand in not the slightest connection; for example, many gods. All such substances would be unconditioned and determined through themselves, but each isolated by its absolute necessity. Since, then, their connection with one another cannot be assumed directly, and without prejudice to their necessity, we can think it in no other way than by deriving their existence from a universal, common primal source, which is the universal power for the universal effect of all things. Through this, however, the latter become dependent upon it and contingent in themselves; they are connected with one another through this universal cause, and a reciprocal connection and community among them arises through the common cause, since one act of a single being was needed to produce them all; and in this manner the *nexus realis* arises.",["Long sentences restated as several; the last two German sentences were partly smoothed ('werden sie ... verbunden' construction irregular).","'Wechselwirkung' rendered 'interaction'; 'Gemeinschaft' 'community'.","'letztere' taken to refer to the substances; 'Kraft' rendered 'power'.","'Dies' in the clause 'welche die allgemeine Kraft zu der allgemeinen Wirkung' taken as the primal source."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-012":["Newton calls space the *organon* of the divine omnipresence. This idea, however, is incorrect, since space in itself is nothing, and cannot be thought, through the connection of things, as something really existing in itself. If, on the other hand, one thinks space as *symbolum*, that is, as standing in the place of all relations and of interaction itself, one thinks under it the sum total of all phenomena, and indeed as *compraesentia*, that is, as present to one another and acting reciprocally upon one another, and the being which contains them as symbol.",["'wiikend' is an OCR slip for 'wirkend'; silently corrected.","The final clause ('und das Wesen, so sie enthält, als Symbol') is elliptical and possibly corrupt; translated as it stands.","'Inbegriff' rendered 'sum total'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-013":["Physical influence (*influxus physicus*) is a) *originarius*, that is, substances are already in *commercio* through their mere existence, without any ground being assumed; or b) *derivativus* (*rationalis*). All physical influence presupposes a *derivativum*, for only this is the true one. It does not go without saying that substances are in *commercio*; for substances are precisely that which exists solely for itself, without depending on another. In the *mundus phaenomenon* (that which is in space and time) it is space itself that connects the substances, and thereby they are in *commercio*. But how are substances in *mundo noumeno* in *commercio*? — The harmony of substances is supposed to consist in this, that their state agrees with one another, that is, according to universal laws. The world is considered either as a *totum ideale*, and in that case there is a *harmonia absque commercio*; or the world is a *totum reale*, and in that case there is a *harmonia substantiarum in commercio*. This latter system is entitled the *systema influxus physici*. The *systema harmoniae substantiarum absque commercio*, on the other hand, is the *systema influxus hyperphysici*; that is, the world derives from an *extramundane* cause [*causa extramundana*], that is, from God.",["The initial 'I' in the OCR is a stray mark and is omitted.","Latin technical terms are kept in italics. 'Bei dem mundo phaenomeno' is left in the form given, although the dative would be *mundo phaenomeno*.","I read 'Dieses letztere Systema' as the system of harmony *in commercio*; the next sentence contrasts the system without it. Two sentences are lightly restated in English order."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-014":["This latter [system] can now be: 1. the *systema adsistentiae*; 2. the *systema harmoniae praestabilitae*. Leibniz wished thereby to explain not the *commercium* of substances in general but only the *commercium* between soul and body, since these are a pair of such heterogeneous substances. But cause and effect can be really [*realiter*] different; I can therefore assume that something depends on something quite dissimilar, for example, the movement of the body on the representation of the soul, as effect depends on cause. This, however, cannot be explained further; we accept such propositions because through them experience becomes possible. The *systema influxus physici* in turn admits of a twofold way of representing it to oneself as possible: 1. that of an original *commercium* [*commercii originarii*], when substances are in *commercio* through the very fact that they exist; 2. that of a derivative one [*derivativi*], when something further must be added in order to effect this *commercium*. The *commercium originarium* is a *qualitas occulta*; it is assumed as the ground of its own explanation. There remains, then, nothing but the *systema influxus physici*, and indeed in the *commercium derivativum*, where I assume that all substances exist through one causality, whereby they are all in *commercio*. This idea has something sublime about it. If I assume all substances to be absolutely necessary, they cannot stand in the slightest community. But if I assume substances to exist in community, then I assume that they all exist through one causality, for only so can their community be explained. — Space itself is the form of the divine omnipresence; that is, the omnipresence of God is expressed in the form of a phenomenon, and through this omnipresence of God all substances are in harmony. But here our reason can see nothing further.",["'Leibnitz' is rendered 'Leibniz'. 'Gemeinschaft' is rendered 'community' and 'commercium' is left in Latin, as in the Kantian usage.","The final OCR mark '_' is omitted. 'dies letztere' is taken to refer to the system of harmony without *commercium* (the extramundane cause), as in the preceding passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-015":["Those who take space to be a thing in itself, or a property of things in themselves, are compelled to be Spinozists; that is, they take the world to be an aggregate of the determinations of a single necessary substance, and so assume only One substance. Space, as something necessary, would then also be an attribute of God, and all things exist in space, and therefore in God.",["'einigen' is taken in the sense 'single, sole'. 'Bestimmungen' is rendered 'determinations'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-016":["...it displeases. If the ground of the pleasing or displeasing is to be sought merely in sensation through the sense, or in sense-sensation (not sensible, because the imagination, which does not suit here, would also belong to that), then it is not the object that pleases but a state that pleases me, by virtue of the sensation aroused in me through the sense. This character, which is referred merely subjectively, of the influence of the object, expressing the state of the subject aroused through inner or outer sense, is, however, that of which one must say: it gratifies, it pains.",["The passage begins in mid-sentence; the opening 'es misfällt' is translated as it stands.","Choice of terms: Vergnügen = 'gratification', Gefallen = 'pleasing', Empfindung = 'sensation'. 'mere subjective' is Kant's mixed Latin-German 'mere', here rendered 'merely'.","'Sinnen=Empfindung (nicht sinnliche ...)' is rendered literally as 'sense-sensation (not sensible ...)'; the sense is a little obscure."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-017":["Hence gratification and pain arise merely through the immediate sense-sensation of the subject. The characteristic mark of this lies in the interest in the existence of the object, or in its non-existence; and gratification is therefore the satisfaction and interest in the existence of the thing. What gratifies us is also agreeable to us, and in its existence we always, without exception, take an interest. For this interest, and with it the gratification, occurs only to the degree, and in so far as, the existence of the object pleases us or (properly speaking) is agreeable to us, because it depends on this to preserve for us for a longer time the subjective state into which we have been put. It is the same, *in casu contrario*, with pain. But there is also a state of pleasing and not pleasing which depends not on the immediate influence of sense-sensation but on reflection upon the object, and arises from it. Here there is present neither gratification, nor pain, nor an interest in the existence of the object and in the preservation of our subjective state; on the contrary, so far as the existence of the object is concerned, there is indifference. It is a matter of indifference whether the state of my sensation is to be gratification or pain; from that side there appears no preponderant, decisive influence. From this arises what is entitled pleasing [*Gefallen*], that is, that satisfaction in the object through the mere intuition of it, with complete indifference (without regard) to its existence. — Either pure or sensible intuition may lie at the foundation. For example: a beautifully built house pleases me in respect of its form; it is possible that its existence pains me on quite other grounds, which are furnished by the understanding, which may contingently co-operate with the gratification and pain.",["The long sentence beginning 'Was uns vergnügt' is restated in two sentences, and the clause 'indem dieses nur in soweit...' is recast with 'For'.","Terms: Wohlgefallen = 'satisfaction', Gefallen = 'pleasing', Gleichgültigkeit = 'indifference', Intereße = 'interest', Verstand = 'understanding'. In the last sentence 'der' is taken to refer to the understanding."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-018":["That which pleases through mere intuition is beautiful; that which in intuition leaves me indifferent as to whether it can please or displease is not-beautiful; that which in intuition displeases is ugly. On this pleasing the concept of taste now rests. For much can please in the representation through the senses, with which the understanding in its operation can combine, and gratification then arises; but in so far as the power of judgment combines with the feeling of pleasure and displeasure, and accompanies and determines the former [i.e. the feeling] in the intuition of an object of the senses, or in general its impression from the same, taste thence arises, which accordingly rests on aesthetic power of judgment: aesthetic (from [OCR illegible: 'atulla'], to taste *per organa sensuum*, sense as the source of sensible representations) representation determined according to the laws of sensibility. Aesthetic power of judgment is therefore that power of judgment which is directed to satisfaction or dissatisfaction in objects, in so far as these objects are [objects] of my sensible intuition, just as the logical power of judgment judges the object not as such in intuition but through reason alone.",["The sentence beginning 'Nemlich vieles' is long and irregular; I have kept its order but resolved 'sie' as the power of judgment and 'das erstere' as the feeling (doubtful: it could be the pleasure alone).","The parenthesis is garbled: 'atulla' is an OCR corruption (presumably a Greek word for sensation or perceiving) and the brackets are unbalanced in the source; I have reproduced what can be made out and not conjectured the Greek.","'UAtheilskraft' and 'Vernunftallein' are corrected silently. 'Urteilskraft' = 'power of judgment'; 'Vorstellung' = 'representation'. The words '[objects]' and '[i.e. the feeling]' are supplied."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-019":["All objects of sense-intuition or of the imagination are also objects of aesthetic power of judgment, and this is wholly identical with the concept of taste. It is therefore occupied with satisfaction in the beautiful, which it should determine, as to whether it is beautiful or not beautiful, and consequently should build its operation on a principle of the beautiful.",["'die ist' is taken to refer to aesthetic power of judgment. The final clause is restated slightly; the German is somewhat irregular ('das sie ... bestimmen ... sollte')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-020":["If, now, the matter of the object, that is, that which affects the mind through immediate sensation of it, were the object of judging, it would be one and the same as gratification and pain. These sensations of the senses, which would here be aesthetic sensations, cannot, however, being merely subjective, be determined and judged according to rules. The satisfaction or dissatisfaction in the object, therefore, in which the aesthetic power of judgment considers the feeling of pleasure or displeasure, concerns merely the form of the object, which is subject to sensible or pure intuition, and of which it determines only whether it is beautiful or not beautiful.",["'sie' in the first sentence is taken to refer to the judging (*Beurteilung*), and in the last to the aesthetic power of judgment. 'Gemüth' = 'mind'; 'Materie' = 'matter'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-021":["A peculiar phenomenon shows itself in the judging of the beautiful. We demand, and presuppose, that what we find beautiful as an object of taste will be found beautiful, as we find it, by everyone else who has taste. It is equally peculiar that the person judging cannot decide at all whether something is beautiful otherwise than by his own judgment, unless he were willing to ape another's judgment without judging for himself. From this one may well also assume that whatever fails to win approval in the judgment of all other persons equally cultivated may in the end not be beautiful either. There thus arises a strange antinomy, which for the present cannot be clearly developed from its ground. We assume that the other must find something beautiful, and at the same time we fear his displeasure, because we have no grounds by which to compel him to approval.",["Restated one long sentence (from 'und ebenso besonders') as several sentences; 'woher man denn auch wohl annehmen kann' is rendered 'From this one may well also assume'.","'Phaenomen' kept as 'phenomenon'; 'Antinomie' as 'antinomy'.","OCR 'mtiße' corrected silently to 'müsse'; 'nach dem Urtheil... den Beifall nicht erhält' is rendered as 'fails to win approval in the judgment of'.","'zur Zeit ihrem Grunde nach nicht deutlich zu entwickeln' is rendered literally; sense is that the ground of the antinomy cannot as yet be clearly worked out."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-022":["For what is to please the other absolutely must be capable of being known by him *a priori*. There must therefore be a rule prescribed to him, in virtue of which he is determined to approval or disapproval. Hence the necessity and universal validity of the beautiful can be established only if a *principle* of the beautiful exists. But a demonstration of the beautiful is impossible. All that is done is to abstract, from what is sensible, in accordance with laws of the understanding, without being able to state these laws, thus found, as universally valid, just because of the subjective element in feeling. — Even the Greeks had models, for example their Doryphoros by Polyclitus, which they called the rule of a beautifully formed young man in the art of sculpture. The judging of the beautiful, like the ground and source of taste in general, which the soul takes so much as its foundation, accordingly remains an object of the most difficult investigation.",["'princip' (lower-case in the text) is rendered 'principle' and italicised for emphasis on the technical term only; no source emphasis is recoverable.","'umnöglich' is an OCR slip for 'unmöglich'. 'Doriphoron' is rendered 'Doryphoros'; 'Polyclet' as 'Polyclitus'.","'es wird nur allein vom Sinnlichen ... abstrahirt' is elliptical; I supplied 'All that is done is to' to give a subject. The clause 'die die Seele so sehr zum Grunde legt' is obscure and is rendered literally.","'Seele' is rendered 'soul', not 'mind' (Gemüt), since the German word here is Seele."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-023":["In aesthetic judging, that alone is beautiful which pleases without any interest in the existence of the object itself, merely in the intuition of it, and indeed in its form; and it pleases because here a free play of the imagination, in agreement with the conformity to law of the understanding, is at work.",["'Einbildungskraft' is rendered 'imagination'; 'Gesetzmäßigkeit' as 'conformity to law'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-024":["From this it follows:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-025":["(a) that whatever is to be beautiful must have at least a resemblance to concepts, or to the laws of the understanding. Yet consciousness of the rules, or of these laws of taste, is by no means necessary; only their existence in the subject is required, so far as they can serve to support and direct the imagination. Hence these concepts of the understanding need not even be present in its determination;",["'seiner/ihre Bestimmung' is ambiguous: 'ihrer' refers either to the imagination or to the judgment; I have rendered 'its determination' (that of the imagination), which is a judgment call.","The passage runs on into the following clauses b) to d), which depend on the same 'daß'/'hieraus folgt'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-026":["(b) that the imagination occupies itself only with the form of the object, and not with its bodily existence; consequently it is not thrown back within the limits of the defects of that existence, but retains in itself an unbounded flight in forming the form for itself. [It must, however, be so,] in order that it may not follow its own laws alone and run to extravagance over the immeasurability of the object, but may merely create for the understanding a manifold in the object [formed] into a whole.",["The German sentence is broken ('daß aber, damit sie nicht ... sondern nur ... schaffe'); I smoothed it and supplied '[It must, however, be so,]' and '[formed]' in brackets to complete the sense. The text is probably corrupt or incomplete.","'Gegen-Standes' (OCR hyphenation) is read as 'Gegenstandes'; 'Ungemeßenheit' as 'immeasurability'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-027":["(c) the understanding in turn each time recalls it to order and holds it within limits, whereupon",["The sentence continues in (d); 'c. )' with spacing is OCR for 'c)'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-028":["(d) both powers support each other reciprocally, carry on a free play, and so occupy themselves with satisfaction. An object of this kind is found to be of good taste.",["'Kräfte' rendered 'powers'; 'geschmackvoll' rendered 'of good taste' (literally 'tasteful')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-029":["That which is approved is further distinguished from what gratifies and pleases. This is the good, that is, what pleases in the representation of reason.",["'Vorstellung der Vernunft' is rendered literally, 'the representation of reason'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-030":["Remark. One can compare the threefold kinds of the faculties of the soul with the three operations of the understanding. On this comparison the faculty of knowledge sets up the concepts; [the faculty] in which pleasure and displeasure are, through the power of judgment, subsumed under the concepts; and the faculty of desire is determined through reason, and so concludes how far pleasure or displeasure takes place in the representation.",["The syntax is irregular ('das, worin die Lust und Unlust ... subsumirt wird'); I supplied '[the faculty]' and smoothed it. The middle member (feeling of pleasure and displeasure) is not named in the German.","'Erkenntniß Vermögen' rendered 'faculty of knowledge'; 'Urtheilskraft' as 'power of judgment'; 'Begehrungs Vermögen' as 'faculty of desire'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-001":["§ 68. Faculty of Desire",["The OCR '§•' is read as '§'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-002":["All representations are related to the object as object of knowledge; but they can also be regarded as actions, and then the ground of the production of their object lies in the representation. With this there corresponds the capacity of the soul to become, through the representation of an object, itself the cause of the actuality of the object. This is the faculty of desire, which may equally validly be defined as the causality of the representation with regard to its object. That action of the soul in general by which the representation seeks to attain the actuality of its object is, in general, desire and appetite [Begierde und Begehren], which are sensible and intellectual. The feeling of pleasure and displeasure is expressed by *voluptas et taedium*. Both, however, are merely sensible; it can be determined more generally, after Herr Kant, by *complacentia et displicentia*,",["OCR 'Vei mögen' corrected to 'Vermögen'; the stray '=' before 'und dies' is dropped as an OCR/mark artefact.","'Begierde und Begehren' both given with the German since English has no clean distinction; 'Wirklichkeit' rendered 'actuality'.","The sentence breaks off at 'displicentia,' and continues in the next passage; 'Wohlgefallen und Mißfallen' follows there."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-003":["pleasure and displeasure [Wohlgefallen und Mißfallen], and the concept of desire can be founded upon this, namely as *complacentia respective actualitatis objecti*, that is, the",["Continues the previous passage ('...durch complacentia et displicentia, | Wohlgefallen und Mißfallen nach Herrn Kant bestimmen'); the translation preserves the break, and the sentence continues in the next passage.","The word order of the previous passage's 'nach Herrn Kant bestimmen' is carried in the previous translation, so it is not repeated here."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-004":["representation of the object which is bound up with pleasure in its actuality, and which is the ground of its production.",["Completes the sentence begun in the preceding passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-005":["Pleasure in the intuition of an object therefore differs from desire for it in this: that desire concerns that relation of the representation to the object in so far as the representation can be the cause of the object's actuality.",["The stray apostrophe before 'dasjenige' is an OCR mark and is dropped. 'dieses' is taken to refer to 'Begehren', the nearer antecedent that the argument requires."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-006":["It seems that this concept is contradicted by the fact that we very often desire something whose actuality it is impossible to bring about. There are, namely, two kinds of desire. The first is an *appetitio practica*, that is, the representation of the possibility of making the thing actual; it is therefore a desire in which the representation is so qualified that the object can become actual. The second is an *appetitio minus practica*, which is called wish, *optare*: a desire combined with the consciousness that it is not in our power to bring the object about. Yet both kinds of desire agree in this, that they are directed to a representation of the object, by virtue of which the desire posits in itself the ground of the possibility of being able to produce the object. The only difference is that in the first case the determining ground [Bestimmungsgrund] is sufficient, while in the second the causality is insufficient.\n\nThis latter is called *conatus*, or wishing in respect of desire. It degenerates into longing [Sehnsucht] when the wishing passes into a violent agitation of the mind, to the point of exhaustion, in an effort to make actual representations that are often quite unnatural or physically impossible. To this belongs, for example, \"*o mihi praeteritos referat si Jupiter annos*\" [sic: the text has *prateritos*]. The cause of this characteristic of human nature, whereby we often cherish wishes, and seek to realise them, whose possible causality we do not recognise in ourselves as sufficient, is as follows. If man, instead of venturing attempts, were first to test whether his powers sufficed with certainty to attain his end, he would leave much undone and would seek to spare himself the trouble. As it is, nature itself calls upon him to direct his endeavour to making trial of his powers. He is content with the possibility of being able to attain the end, although for the present he feels himself insufficient and it is quite uncertain whether he can succeed. And under great exertion there often develop in a man powers that were previously unknown to him, which now convince him more and more of the possibility, so that he has no cause to distrust himself any longer and will have powers sufficient to attain his end.\n\nIf, however, the wishes are contrary to nature, there ensues a useless sinking back, which shows itself in weeping, as weakness, in the female sex, and in merely vain attempts in the male.\n\nDesire in respect of such objects as we are conscious of having sufficient powers to produce is an act [*actus*] of choice [Willkühr]. The faculty corresponding to this desire is choice, *arbitrium*, which therefore involves not only determination to causality but also sufficient power to make the object actual. Where, however, there is the faculty of choice in respect of producing the object, there must without exception also be present the faculty of refraining from the production, or of omitting it. *Arbitrium*, or choice, is therefore in the strict sense a *facultas appetendi vel aversandi objectum*, *i. e.* *omittendo vel committendo*.",["Restated the long middle sentences as several shorter ones; the paragraph break before 'If, however' and before 'Desire in respect of such objects' is mine, for clarity.","Willkühr rendered 'choice' (Kemp Smith has no fixed rendering; Latin *arbitrium* is kept beside it). 'Bestimmungsgrund' given as 'determining ground'.","'Es scheint diesem Begriff der Zweifel zu widerstreiten' is syntactically odd (probably 'der Zweifel' is a transcription slip); I translated it as 'It seems that this concept is contradicted by the fact that…'. Antecedent of 'this concept' (Begierde) is in the preceding passage, not given.","'Dies letztere' taken to refer to the *appetitio minus practica*. 'in Ansehung der Begierde' kept literally.","The sentence 'Die Ursache … liegt darin' is followed by a counterfactual ('Wollte aber…'); the 'aber' suggests something has dropped out. Rendered as it stands, with 'is as follows'.","Quotation is Ovid/Virgil-style tag 'o mihi praeteritos referat si Jupiter annos' (Aeneid VIII.560); OCR/ms. reading 'prateritos' flagged. 'Sehnsucht' kept in brackets.","'schlechthin begleitet seyn' rendered 'without exception also be present'; 'aucli' silently corrected to 'auch', 'W illkühr' to 'Willkühr', 'Bewußtsejm' to 'Bewußtseyn'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-007":["In respect of the determining ground, the *arbitrium* has in itself a twofold aspect. For there lie in man *elateres animi*, or determining grounds, sources of the possibility of producing what is represented, *causae determinantes sive impulsivae*. These lie either in the understanding, as the law of the action, or in sensibility, namely in the feeling of pleasure and displeasure. They are accordingly either *sensitivae causae sive elateres* or *intellectuales causae sive elateres*; the former are called *stimuli*, the latter *motiva*. Hence arises the division of the concept into *arbitrium intellectuale sive superius*, the higher or rational choice, *i. e.* *facultas appetendi per motiva*, or *voluntas sive arbitrium ex causa impulsiva intellectuali*, and *arbitrium sensitivum*, *facultas appetendi per stimulos*.",["'Arbiti ium' silently corrected to 'Arbitrium'.","Split the sentence beginning 'Es liegen nemlich…' at 'und diese liegen'. Sinnlichkeit = sensibility, Verstand = understanding.","'Einteilung des Begriffes' kept literal; the concept divided is *arbitrium* (Willkühr)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-008":["Rational choice is in turn either *purum* or *affectum*, according as no *stimuli* at all concur in the choice as co-operating causes, or such sensible impulses at the same time affect the mind and determine it to action,",["The passage ends with a comma in the source; it breaks off and is rendered as it stands. 'vernünftige Willkühr' = rational choice; 'Gemüt' = mind."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-009":["In appearance [*In der Erscheinung*], however, human choice can never be assumed to be determined without being affected by *stimuli*, and just as little can it be assumed that it could be determined through *stimuli* alone.",["'Erscheinung' = appearance, as the Kantian sense (the human being as phenomenon) requires."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-010":["Animals have an *arbitrium mere sensitivum*, for example in choosing their food. They are therefore determined solely *per stimulos*, and an *arbitrium sensitivum* may accordingly also be entitled *brutum*. Human *arbitrium*, on the other hand, is never *purum* but always *affectum*. The co-operating *stimuli* can, however, never determine it, but only affect it sensibly, and the concurrence of the understanding remains necessary to determine it. But it can never be said that *stimuli* have not affected it. In giving alms, for example, *stimuli* would already be present if a man gives the alms at the cost of his own comfort, from which he thereby withdraws; or from love of honour, so as not to be harshly scolded in the eyes of his fellow men; or from pity for the ragged beggar; or because of the beggar's entreaties, which touch the giver's weakness; or also from the reward to be expected from God.\n\nOften the *stimuli* are so hidden that one must examine oneself closely whether it is merely love for obedience to the law that brings me to the action, and whether I therefore act from *arbitrium purum*. But it is equally true that it is never *stimuli* alone that determine me to action. A representation of the law of duty, even if not a clear one, always concurs; and this must be assumed if only because otherwise one would make man like the beast or the devil, were one to suppose that he could do everything from self-interest, and so forth. Otherwise it is quite possible that *stimuli* may incite to good as well as to evil.",["Divided the long final sentence into two paragraphs/sentences. 'ihn' (the arbitrium, neuter in sense but masculine pronoun in the German) rendered 'it'; the pronoun may refer to 'der Mensch' in places, which I resolved by sense.","'der er sich entzieht' is obscure; rendered 'from which he thereby withdraws' [his comfort]. 'genauselbst' read as 'genau selbst'. 'geschölten' read 'gescholten'.","'Liebe zur Befolgung des Gesetzes' kept literal ('love for obedience to the law'); possible OCR slip. 'brutum' kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-011":["On this rests the concept of freedom, namely the capacity of man to determine himself to action through motives, independently of the sensible impulses that affect him. Choice is therefore free only so far as it is not determined *per stimulos*; in human choice this comes about through the overcoming of the *stimuli*. Hence free choice, or freedom, is the independence of choice from determination *per stimulos*.\n\nThus in human choice the *stimuli* are not, and must not be, excluded; for otherwise it would be an *arbitrium purum*, a pure self-subsistent being, which can determine itself only in conformity with laws and not against them. Man has *facultates inferiores*, which act for him as sensible impulses and certainly become *causae impulsivae*, but can only affect him, never determine him, either to good or to evil. His choice is therefore *partim sensualis, partim intellectualis*. Of these, the *stimuli* that operate upon it are indeed to be regarded as a ground of the action, but only as an insufficient ground, whereas the motives contain that determining ground which makes the action necessary.",["Divided the passage into two paragraphs; 'Mithin' rendered 'Hence'. 'stimulorem' silently read 'stimulorum'. 'Vermögen' = capacity.","'das reine selbständige Wesen, so sich nur den Gesetzen gemäß…' rendered literally; the sense is that a pure will cannot determine itself contrary to law.","'Bestimmungsgrund' = determining ground."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-012":["§69. All objectively necessary rules and determinations of choice, in so far as they are subjectively contingent, are entitled imperatives.",["'Bestimmungen' rendered 'determinations'; 'heißen' = 'are entitled'. The imperatives are kept in the plural form 'imperative' → 'imperatives'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-013":["(a) They are objective in general because they express what must be done.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-014":["(b) Every imperative is objectively necessary, that is, it contains, in respect of the action, a determining ground which makes the action necessary according to a rule; but at the same time it is",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is rendered as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-015":["(c) Every imperative is subjectively contingent; that is, in its determination it is bound up with the possibility that the subject may deviate from the rule and do the opposite. Hence, in a being so constituted as to satisfy both conditions of the imperative, and which acts according to imperatives, there arises constraint [*Nöthigung*] to the action. For such a being is subject to commands in which there is present both an objectively necessary rule and a possibility that the subject may deviate from the rule. Consequently an imperative is, for such a being, an objective rule in so far as it is necessitating with respect to the subject; and both conditions are combined in it, if it is to act according to imperatives. Only of such a being, too, can an *ought* [*Sollen*] be predicated, that is, a necessity of acting according to a rule the transgression of which remains subjectively possible. Hence neither a God nor an animal can act according to imperatives. God is incapable of any deviation from the law; he is determined only through the law, that is, through himself, and in him there is no constraint and no ought. He has therefore no duties either, since duties rest upon an ought or constraint. He has only rights against human beings, just as they can have only duties towards him, never rights. Animals, on the other hand, do not act according to rules, since for want of understanding they do not know any such; they have only sensible impulses. They cannot, therefore, follow imperatives, and so determine themselves to action by them, that is, let themselves be constrained.",["Restated the long sentence beginning 'Daher entspringt…' as four shorter sentences; 'mithin' rendered 'Consequently'.","'nach beyden Bedingungen des imperativs eingerichteten Wesen': the two conditions are the objectively necessary rule and the subjective possibility of deviating (as the text itself states); 'satisfy' is my gloss for 'eingerichtet'.","OCR 'lüi ihn' read as 'für ihn'; 'neceßitirend' rendered 'necessitating'; 'Sollen' kept with German in brackets/italics.","'Noth wendigkeit', 'handlen', 'imperativen befolgen' silently corrected (the last to 'imperative befolgen')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-016":["Imperatives, then, have actions for their end, and are therefore entitled practical imperatives, that is, practical rules which show what ought to be done. The opposite [*Oppositum*] is physical rules, according to which something ought to happen. Practical imperatives, however, may be either technically practical imperatives, that is, they may command the means hypothetically for the attainment of an end, and are therefore rules which demand only conditional observance. To these belong all mechanical rules for an art or skill, in which, in order to attain the end, one follows the rules of that art. [To these belong also] rules of prudence, in respect of the actions of other men towards one's own end; the latter are, however, entitled pragmatic. In both cases it depends in the first place upon the will of the agent whether he wills the end. If he has no interest in it, no one can compel him to follow the rule; so that both kinds command only conditionally, for the sake of the end, [or else they are] moral-practical rules.",["'Oppositum' kept in Latin and italicised.","The sentence 'Regeln der Klugheit…' has no verb; I supplied '[To these belong also]' in brackets. 'welche letztere' read as referring to the rules of prudence.","The final clause 'oder aber als moralisch practische Regeln' is irregular, since the alternative to the conditional commands is left dangling; bracketed '[or else they are]' is a smoothing and the passage may be corrupt or the sentence incomplete.","OCR 'piagmatisch' read as 'pragmatisch'; 'zuförderst' as 'in the first place'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-017":["Remark. One might very well also assume a disjunctive imperative. It would consist in this, that the subject's choice among several determinations commanded disjunctively would indeed be permitted, but that transgression and contravention against every condition of the law would be unlawful. For example, in a civil war it would be unlawful to remain neutral; one or other of the parties one would have to attack.",["'innern Kriege' rendered 'civil war'.","The syntax of the German ('Bestimmungen… disjunctive gebiethenden') is loose; I have smoothed it, and 'Bedingung' in 'alle Bedingung des Gesetzes' is rendered 'every condition of the law'.","'z.E.' expanded to 'For example'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-018":["Moral-practical rules are by their nature categorical, since they command absolutely, immediately through reason, and without any regard to the existence of other grounds of determination or motives. They are unconditioned and immediate grounds of determination of actions. Any other motive assumed, other than that which reason gives itself, would be a condition, and the command would be only hypothetical. For example, if a witness were willing to speak the truth only because he fears worldly or divine punishment, or because his friend has an advantage in it, these and other grounds could easily be evaded, and the duty of truthfulness would no longer hold at all, if it depended merely upon conditional circumstances, the opposite of which is equally possible. Reason alone is the legislator, and through the subject it is also to follow the law. If the subject is to be able to do this, in spite of the inclination of sensible impulses, then it must be capable of doing so; it must be possible for him to carry out what reason commands. The categorical imperative therefore presupposes in man a subjective possibility of following the law as well as of transgressing it, or, what is the same thing, an independence *a determinationibus per stimulos* [of determinations through stimuli], and consequently freedom. Man must therefore be free if he is to follow absolute commands solely and simply because reason commands them.",["Divided the long sentences; 'Bestimmungs- oder Bewegungs-Gründe' rendered 'grounds of determination or motives'.","The witness example is loosely constructed ('weil sein Freund aber dabei Vortheil hat'); I took 'aber' as continuing the list of motives and smoothed it.","'eludirt' rendered 'evaded'; 'Neigung sinnlicher Antriebe' as 'inclination of sensible impulses'.","The Latin phrase kept in italics with the English in square brackets."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-019":["For if this were not the case, all the actions of man would have to be determined merely according to laws of nature, or according to natural necessity, and all imputability would therefore fall away. For freedom is contradistinguished from natural necessity. Actions which occur according to laws of freedom must be performed in such a way that they are not determined by the preceding state of the world. Actions according to natural necessity, on the contrary, cannot be regarded otherwise than as necessarily predetermined in the series of effects and causes. But if they are so, they are determined in the past time; and actions, regarded as determined in the past time, are not in my power, so that I might also omit the action. The opposite cannot therefore happen, since they are determined in the past time according to unalterable laws of causality, and so appear merely as effects of which the cause was present in the past time, and which are consequently not in my power, because past time as such is not in my power.",["OCR 'bestimmtwerden, undmithinwürde' silently corrected to 'bestimmt werden, und mithin würde'.","'Zurechnungsfähigkeit' rendered 'imputability'; 'in contrario' rendered 'on the contrary'.","Divided the final long sentence and smoothed the clause 'die Handlung auch zu unterlaßen' as a consecutive clause ('so that I might also omit the action')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-020":["An *arbitrium*, then, which was determined by laws of nature as predetermining causes, would be an *arbitrium servum*, and the opposite of the predetermined effect could not possibly be done by it. This is, however, contrary to the nature of a command of reason, which permits and presupposes possibility of fulfilment, not necessity, and consequently also possibility of the opposite. The determination to obedience lies, therefore, in the free choice [*Willkühr*] of the subject. Hence free choice is accompanied by a will which draws its own, and indeed sufficient, (intellectual) grounds of determination not from states and conditions of past time, but rather performs the action through self-determination (spontaneity), and without being necessitated by any cause of past time.",["*arbitrium* and *arbitrium servum* kept in Latin; 'Willkühr' rendered 'choice' (free choice) with the German given.","OCR 'fiir's', 'Wiilkühr', 'spontaneit-aet' silently corrected.","'Bestimmung der Befolgung' rendered 'determination to obedience'; the sense (whether to obey) is judged from the context."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-021":["It becomes clear from this that the system or principle hitherto called determinism must properly be called predeterminism. For since it is absolutely necessary that every action be determined by a ground, even that which a Deity performs, determinism expresses nothing of what is here in question, namely the principle according to which every action, even that of a free being, is thought as determined by its grounds of determination in past time, and so as not given in the power of the agent. This ought, according to Herr Kant, to be called predeterminism.",["*determinismus* and *praedeterminismus* rendered 'determinism' and 'predeterminism'.","'Gottheit' rendered 'Deity'; the last sentence ('Dies sollte man…') is reported opinion of the lecturer as the transcript has it."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-022":["Now the striking phenomenon in man is that freedom is found united in him with natural necessity. Both have place in him, so that one can say that everything in man happens according to laws of natural necessity, and also that everything happens according to the principle of freedom.",["'Phaenomen' rendered 'phenomenon' (not 'appearance') as the word is used loosely here; a stray hyphen after 'Natur Nothwendigkeit' was dropped."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-023":["As a being of nature (*phaenomenon*), every action of man can be explained as determined by the necessity of the laws of nature. This is also often done in criminal cases. For example, in the case of the criminal we take account of his upbringing, his external circumstances, his inclinations, or other motives that are merely subjective, in order to derive from them the *determination* to the crime. As an intelligence (rational being) [man] must, however, be thought as determined not by pure reason alone [*or as noumenon*]. Man is self-determining, independent of all laws of nature; he takes from himself the ground for refraining from an action which he can or ought to perform, something he would not be capable of if he were determined according to laws of nature. We are, however, compelled to assume in his selfhood a union of these two beings present in him, which seem wholly contradictory. By virtue of this union he is one and the same person, who is affected by the lower powers but determines himself through the independence of reason; and so he appears as ordered under reason and under nature not successively but at one and the same time. In respect of his actions, therefore, freedom can also be named (*nominal definition*):",["The sentence 'Als intelligenz ... (oder als noumenon)' has no finite verb in the German and the clause 'die jedoch nicht als pure ... gedacht werden muß' is irregular; I supplied '[man]' and '[is] thought' and kept the parenthesis in brackets. The negation is doubtful: the sense may require 'determined by reason itself' without 'not'; the transcript may be corrupt or the OCR may have misread it.","'jede neue Handlung' is rendered 'every action'; 'neue' may be an OCR or transcript slip (perhaps 'jede' alone or 'menschliche'), so the reading is doubtful.","'Bewegungs Gründe' = motives; 'Bestimmung' rendered 'determination' (kept the Latin *determination* in italics where the text has it).","OCR corrected silently: 'V ernunft Wesen' to 'Vernunft Wesen', 'j selbst' to 'selbst', and similar. 'Selbst heit' read as 'Selbstheit' (selfhood).","The passage ends with a colon; the definition continues in the next passage (s08-024)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-024":["the capacity of man for imputation [*Zurechnungsfähigkeit*].",["Continues the previous passage: 'freedom can also be named ... the imputability (accountability) of man'. 'Zurechnungsfähigkeit' could also be rendered 'imputability' or 'accountability'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-025":["Considered solely as an intelligence, man is a subject to whom actions can be imputed. In him, to be sure, a being of nature is at the same time united, which subjects him to the laws of nature. Yet, so far as the possibility of acting is left to him, and the determination depends on the intelligence, what has to be derived is only the extent to which man can be entitled the cause of his actions and the actions can be imputed to him. It is equally certain that, if he were guided merely by laws of nature, it would be impossible to impute any action to him, since the ground of the action would then never lie in his power but would be determined in the preceding time.",["'Bios' is an OCR slip for 'Blos' (merely, solely).","The middle sentence ('welches ihn ... daher ist es nur abzuleiten') is irregular and probably a defective transcript; I restated it in separate sentences, and the sense of 'daher ist es nur abzuleiten' is conjectural.","'genannt werden' rendered 'is entitled' per the method."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-026":["As noumenon, then, man has the power of determining himself to action in spite of the necessity of his nature. From this it follows that freedom is thinkable without contradiction of the concept, and is therefore logically possible.",["'Vermögen' rendered 'power'; 'Natur Nothwendigkeit' = necessity of his nature (or natural necessity)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-027":["We cannot, however, know things in themselves, what and how they are in themselves, but only in appearance. Consequently we also know man only as he presents himself to us in the form of sensibility, as phenomenon, and so know his actions, but not the grounds that determine them. It is therefore impossible to prove the real possibility of the absolute self-determination of man, or how man can determine himself freely and yet at the same time be subject to the laws of nature. For that, it would necessarily be required that freedom be an object of possible intuition. It is so far from being this that all the actions of man appear rather as determined, in respect of their ground, according to the law of causality, or in the preceding time; and that man therefore has it not in his power how he is to act, so that he must be subject to the necessity of nature.\n\nBut precisely because man presents himself in this way in accordance with the form of sensibility, a possible inference can be drawn to the supersensible, as something existing which might be reckoned among the determining grounds of his actions. Perhaps a supersensible source may be sought in him, where he no longer appears as an object of the senses, although we cannot determine *a priori* how he is there. This source would be the will [*Willkühr*] of man as noumenon, that is, the independent, but to us unknown, ground of all appearances in the sensible man. In this respect he may be presupposed as the self-subsistent, independent ground of all his actions; we may assume of him that he now begins, through the ground of determination present in him, a series of actions, and may regard him as if he were not in time. In this respect freedom could be defined as the power of man to begin a series of consequences of himself.",["Divided into two paragraphs at 'Allein'; the long sentences on the impossibility of proof and on the supersensible source are restated in shorter sentences.","'daß der Mensch also es nicht in seiner Gewalt habe ...' depends loosely on 'erscheinen'; I kept it as a coordinate clause.","'Willkühr' is left as 'will [*Willkühr*]'; Kant's later distinction from *Wille* is not yet at work here.","'läßt sich ein möglicher Schluß ... machen' is slightly irregular; 'die Art' in 'in der Art nach der Form' is smoothed to 'in this way'.","'absolutenSelbst Bestimmung' is an OCR run-together, read 'absolute Selbstbestimmung'; 'Noth Wendigkeit' read as one word."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-028":["In this respect it is quite correct to say of man that his actions are subject to determinism. For since he cannot act without being determined to the action by a ground, the only presupposition here is that he determines himself to the action; he is not predetermined to it by an antecedently determining time, that is, by the law of causality. This confusion of concepts made the concept of freedom the *vera crux philosophorum*. How are we to reconcile the following? Man is subject to the necessity of the law of nature, and so far is already predetermined to his actions, as effects, by an antecedently determining time as cause; and yet man is supposed to be capable of acting freely. As a natural being, to be sure, he stands solely under predeterminism, and if the choice (*Willkühr*) of man could be assumed to be determined merely in the preceding time, no freedom at all would be possible. But if we may presuppose a noumenon in man, and may accordingly assume that his actions flow from independent determinations of the powers of this being within him, then the concept of freedom is thinkable and possible without contradiction. Its reality, it is true, is impossible to prove, since it is a pure concept of reason *a priori*, which cannot be established through moral actions. These are performed by virtue of the determination of reason, and are the only means by which freedom reveals itself in appearance.\n\nSuppose, for example, that a witness, in giving his testimony, represents to himself the importance of his duty to speak the truth and to regulate his actions lawfully accordingly, without any other incidental motives, and that he acts without the concurrence of any natural cause. This would prove nothing further in his case than the existence of the consciousness of the moral law, which commands him, setting aside all natural causes, to follow the law. From this example it follows at the same time that there is present in us a consciousness of a moral law by which we are constrained to action, in that it commands us absolutely. By virtue of the existence of this law we can infer the freedom of our actions: that we are bound, or constrained, to act in accordance with the law at every sacrifice of the sensible.\n\nQuite incorrect, however, is the notion of certain philosophers (for example Rehberg) in this matter, that we can become directly and immediately conscious of absolute spontaneity, or of the working of the freedom of the law, in our actions. That is, they hold that we can know that the law alone determines us, and that no determining ground found in the senses is bound up with it. This is impossible. We can indeed become aware that sensible impulses concur in the determination to an action of duty. But from the fact that in a particular case (*in casu speciali*) we are not conscious of their presence, we can by no means conclude that they are not present in us at all and active. For how is one to become aware of their non-existence? Immediately this is in itself impossible, since there is no mark [by which to do so]; and that it does not take place mediately is evident from the following. The action of duty rests on constraint, so far as sensible affection concurs; without constraint no free choice is thinkable. How then can it be known whether the determination to the action has its ground in the sensible impulses, or in spontaneity alone, or in both? Take, for example, the case of someone who is given the choice between two things which are in themselves quite alike. It is false to assert that he can choose here only by a free determination of his reason, without the concurrence of any natural causes. One may suppose that he has certainly examined both things beforehand, often and repeatedly, whether from distrust or because he expects an advantage in one; finally, after fruitless effort, impatience overcomes him and he takes one. Is not the latter a determining ground which flows into his reason, so that reason may at last decide what it calls upon him to do? So too it is undecided whether the witness determines himself to the deposition of the truth more from a feeling of his moral conscience or from rules of prudence.\n\nWe cannot, then, become aware of freedom, nor of the fact that it commands the determining grounds of our moral actions; we must infer its existence only from the consciousness of the laws of reason. It follows that we know the laws of morals *a priori*, indeed, but as dogmatically practical laws, in distinction from the dogmatically theoretical propositions *a priori*, which we can have only of objects of possible experience. These moral laws are also what properly belongs to man as noumenon and constitutes the character of the latter, [the noumenon] presenting itself as the subjective principle of legislation in man.\n\nThis being within us, unknown to us, is the most sublime thought, and one very fruitful in consequences for our moral existence. We find that all these laws are laid down in us plainly and clearly; that it is so little remarkable to see them obeyed that one must rather wonder that they are so often not obeyed; that every common human understanding decides at once, on their basis, upon the rightness of actions; and that we ourselves alone are to blame for every case of transgression. Now if, in examining this being that rules within us, we find that it commands our whole sensible nature, and so must itself be something supersensible, while its proper nature and constitution remain unknown to us, this circumstance fills us with deep admiration. We do not know whether there is in us a creative being which represents these laws to us as effects, or whether we ourselves possess this creative power. In short, we cannot explain what works in us. Its effects, however, are astonishing in comparison with sensible nature. Against its commands nothing can be done. It is not outweighed by torment and bodily suffering, nor even by death. *Phalaris licet compert*, says Juvenal, and other poets ascribe to it the most sublime attributes. But it is precisely this deep admiration for the sublime attributes of a being present in us and unknown to us that constitutes its excellence, and that ought to be put to quite especial use in religion and instruction.",["Long sentences restated as several; the paragraph divisions are mine, to mark the argument's stages.","Willkühr rendered 'choice' with the German given once; elsewhere 'free choice'. Bestimmung/Bestimmungsgrund rendered 'determination'/'determining ground'.","'since there is no mark [by which to do so]': the bracketed words are supplied; Kant's elliptical 'da es kein Merckmal giebt'.","'the latter... [the noumenon]': 'als welches' is ambiguous; I take it to refer to the noumenon (or its character), and supplied the bracket by judgment.","'Gesetzes Freiheit' rendered 'freedom of the law' literally; the sense (freedom's working through the law) is obscure and may be a transcriber's slip.","The Latin tag 'Phalaris licet compert' is corrupt or garbled (cf. Juvenal, Sat. 8, on Phalaris's bull); I left it as printed.","'in dem Sinne vorkommender Bestimmungs Grund' rendered 'a determining ground found in the senses' (Sinne, not Sinn); 'Sinne' vs. 'Sinn' is a doubtful reading of the OCR.","Silently corrected OCR slips: 'Ereiheit' to Freiheit, 'PTrsache' to Ursache, 'Bewußtse}rn' to Bewußtseyn, 'VVillkühr', 'rnoralische', 'ündt' to findet, etc."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-029":["§70. Reflection upon the sensibility (*Sinnlichkeit*) of man shows a remarkable difference in respect of the faculty of choice (*arbitrium*) and especially of the morality of man. The difference lies between the representation (*Vorstellung*) of a sensibility which includes merely sensible feeling and that of a sensibility which presents intellectual feeling; and from this arises the diversity in the determining grounds of our moral actions.",["'Vor-Stellung' (hyphenated in the source) rendered 'representation'.","Restated as two sentences; the clause 'wodurch...' is attached to the difference in kind of sensibility, which I take to be the sense.","'arbitrio' is given in the ablative/dative form in the source; rendered 'faculty of choice (*arbitrium*)'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-030":["Sensibility affects either our sensible feeling alone, or at the same time with it our faculty of desire (*Begehrungsvermögen*). In the latter case it becomes at the same time a determining ground of our actions; in the former it does not, since the power of choice (*Willkühr*) cannot be brought to action without a prior determination of the faculty of desire. Hence there is a distinction",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('Daher distinguirt sich'); translated as it stands, without completion.","'VVillkühr' is an OCR slip for Willkühr.","'es' in 'wird es zugleich Bestimmungs Grund' taken to refer to the sensibility's affecting (the affection), rendered 'it'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-031":["(a) Sensuous pleasure and displeasure (or sensuous feeling). This obtains when the representation produced, or the action itself, has its ground in a faculty of desire determined through pleasure or displeasure. Hence every circumstance external to me, every enjoyment or pain, every advantage or representation of happiness, if it becomes the ground of the action or representation in such a way that the feeling of pleasure or displeasure in the representation precedes the faculty of desire and acts upon it as determining ground, then the pleasure and displeasure, which is at the same time the determining ground of the faculty of desire, is sensuous feeling [*sinn-hohes Gefühl*]. This feeling is always empirical, and determines the faculty of desire in the manner stated. Or else the feeling is",["Restated the first sentence in two. The 'Hence...' sentence is long and irregular in the German; I have kept its sense but smoothed the construction.","'sinn -hohes Gefühl' is an OCR corruption, almost certainly 'sinnliches Gefühl' as in the opening line; I translated it as 'sensuous feeling' with the reading flagged in brackets. 'Lnlust' is silently read as 'Unlust'.","'Begehrungs Vermögen' = faculty of desire; 'Bestimmungs Grund' = determining ground.","The final 'oder' leads into the next passage (b); 'Or else the feeling is' was supplied to make the connection plain and is not an addition of content."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-032":["(b) Intellectual feeling. This obtains when the representation of the law precedes, and the feeling of pleasure and displeasure arises only from the determination effected upon the faculty of desire. Here the determining ground lies in the law and in the consciousness of it. Reason determines the will [*Willkühr*] in accordance with the law, and from this follows the pleasure in my representation or action, since the claim of reason is satisfied by my following its precept, and therefore unfailingly gives birth to an inner pleasure of the soul. This pleasure is rational, and has its source always in the moral determining [ground]",["The passage breaks off in the original ('bricht ab'); I have left it unfinished. 'Bestimmungs' is completed with the bracketed 'ground' only to keep the English intelligible.","OCR slip 'dei Anspruch der ’\\ ernunft' read as 'der Anspruch der Vernunft'.","'Willkühr' left in German in brackets because the choice (will, as distinct from *Wille*) turns on it."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-033":["First of all, it must be maintained (a) against the materialists that the soul can as little be material in and by itself as be a simple part of matter. The soul is rather a simple being (in the negative concept); hence also the soul is immaterial and incorporeal. For 'matter' here means not only what is itself throughout matter, but also what can be merely a part of a matter, and therefore an extended, impenetrable being.",["'Zuvörderst' rendered 'First of all'; 'an und vor sich' as 'in and by itself'.","The parenthesis '(im negativen Begriff)' is kept: simplicity is here understood only negatively, as absence of composition."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-034":["First of all, [however,] the soul cannot be both simple and a part of matter. For as a part of matter (even a simple part) it must occupy a space together with the latter; but space again consists of spaces, and consequently this simple [part] would again have parts. Every part of matter, however, is again matter. Consequently the soul must be either (wholly) material or (by itself) immaterial. But to assume a *materia cogitans* is something impossible; the soul is rather simple, and in no case composite.\n\nThis simplicity rests upon the unity of consciousness in thinking, or upon the unity of the manifold in representation in general. All representations are related to an object by virtue of the determination in the mind through which alone we are in general capable of representing anything to ourselves. The object, however, must be absolutely a unity, with which the consciousness of the manifold is combined. For otherwise the sentence, for example, *a. didicisse b. fideliter c. artes d. mollit f. mores* [etc.], would have to be capable of being thought as a whole in such a way that different powers, a, b, c, d, e, f, etc., would each think a concept for itself, and yet could be conscious in common of the whole sentence. Since this is impossible, no representation of an object can arise unless there is present an absolute unity of the representing subject. And it is impossible to let the consciousness of the representation arise through a bodily divisibility, as if there were different subjects which divided the representation among themselves. For in that case it would be impossible for these parts, each known only to its own subject, to be combined without an intermediary into a whole, that is, into a unity.\n\nIf it be assumed, then, that the *materia cogitans* is an aggregate of substances, the representation attributed to it would also have to contain an aggregate of representations, namely of its parts, separated from one another. One part would then grasp one portion, another part think another, and a unity could not be attained. In order that the representation should nevertheless be brought to a whole, it remains always necessary to assume a uniting subject, which again combines all these parts among themselves and with one another. Thus a composite representation cannot be attributed to the thinking subject, but only a simple representation; that is, it combines the representation into a simple principle, or is simple.",["The opening 'Zuvörderst... aber' repeats the 'Zuvörderst' of the previous passage; I supplied '[however,]' to mark the contrast and divided the long final sentences into several.","The Latin tag is Horace-like (*didicisse fideliter artes emollit mores*, Ovid); the transcript splits it among letters a–f. The 'e' is missing in the sentence (letters run a, b, c, d, f) while the later list reads 'a. b. c. d. e. f.'; I kept the text as it stands.","'dem Einfachen' (this simple) read as 'this simple [part]'; supplied 'part' for sense.","'vel materiell (ganz) oder immateriell (für sich)': 'vel' is a stray Latin 'or', rendered with 'either'.","'Bestimmung im Gemüth' = 'determination in the mind'; 'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Kräfte' = powers.","Some OCR spacing ('seyn,wozu dasBewußtseyn') silently corrected. Typo 'ihm beygelegte' took 'ihm' to refer to the *materia cogitans*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-035":["By this it is indeed proved that the subject can think without its principle being corporeal; the materialists are refuted. But it does not yet follow from this",["The sentence runs on into the next passage ('b) noch nicht'); I translated it as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-036":["(b) that the soul can think without being united with the body. And this is what pneumatology teaches, the possibility of which, however, can never be proved.",["'noch nicht' continues from the preceding passage and is rendered there; 'deßen' is read as referring to pneumatology ('the possibility of which')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-037":["We are incapable of investigating the causality of such a self-subsisting principle, or of determining the effects, and their character, which might arise from it as cause; and we do not know *a priori*, therefore, whether such a thinking principle, separated from the body, exists. We could learn it in no other way than empirically, by observing a soul separated from the body. But in life this is not possible, on account of the union with the body; and apart from this state we perceive nothing of the soul. What the soul will be after death is equally impossible for us to know, on the same ground. Since, therefore, we can obtain through experience no object of representation in this matter, it is also impossible to arrive at pneumatology, or ever to know whether there is any spirit, even a God, in the *universum*.",["'caussalitaet' read as 'Causalität'; 'Ursach' = cause. 'Gegenstand der Vorstellung' = 'object of representation'.","Divided the sentence beginning 'Dies geht nun aber...' into several, keeping the sense."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-038":["We know nothing further than that the soul is an immaterial substance, and that it cannot be known as the predicate of another being, because I cannot think a *compositum* of many substances. For this the unity of the subject is absolutely presupposed; materialism accordingly contradicts itself in the idea, and nothing can be explained from it. But whether the soul is a spirit (*spiritus*), as a thinking being, as the author [Baumgarten] holds in § 742, does not follow from the latter concept. Animals also have souls, which for that reason are not spirits. Spirits are specifically thinking immaterial substances, which can also think without connection with the material. Were the body merely an indispensable *adminiculum* and a condition for the thinking of the soul, then the soul would exist as soul, but not as spirit; for the former, but not the latter, stands in a necessary connection with the body in order to bring about the *actus* of the substance.",["'[Baumgarten]' is supplied; the text says only 'der Autor', i.e. the textbook author of the *Metaphysica*.","'Verbindüng' silently corrected; 'so auch' read as 'die auch' (which also). 'im Fall nur' rendered 'Were the body merely' as a conditional.","The § number '742' stands as printed in the source; I have not checked it against Baumgarten."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-039":["§ 82. If this immaterial being in the body, which we call soul, is now considered in relation to matter, efforts have been made to discover the seat of the soul, or the place where the *sensorium commune* is to be found. That is, [efforts have been made to find] partly the place where the soul receives all impressions of objects, that is, the sensation of them, and partly the place corresponding to this sensation, which can be called the *primum movens* of the animal, that is, that from which the whole body can be set in motion.",["The first sentence ('Im Fall man sich nun... betrachtet, so...') is slightly irregular; I smoothed it and supplied the bracketed words in the second sentence to complete the construction.","'Empfindung' = sensation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-040":["This capacity of the soul is called the *facultas locomotiva*, in distinction from the *facultas cogitandi*. Both powers, namely that of sensing and that of imparting motion, are placed in the *sensorium commune*, from which both are supposed to proceed.",["The Latin ('facultatem locomotivam', 'facultate cogitandi') is in inflected form in the transcript; I give the nominative.","'Fähigkeit' rendered 'capacity', 'Vermögen' 'power', to keep the two words apart."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-041":["Some have taken the *glandula pinealis*, the pineal gland, to be the seat of the *sensorium communē*. For there is only one isolated nerve in the human brain, the pineal gland. It lies in the middle of the brain, where the tips of the nerves running to the brain first meet, and the soul reacts upon it when the body is to be moved. In the whole body there is only one such gland. Since the simplicity of the soul does not permit us to grant it more than one place, this nerve has been taken to be the common seat; this was above all the opinion of Descartes. It is, however, still possible in itself that there may be, in other parts of the body, yet other living principles which are not the soul or the supreme principle of all. Moreover, it has been discovered in recent times that the pineal gland is, in most cases, filled with sand.",["OCR 'zurückge wirkt' read as 'zurückgewirkt'. 'die mehreste Male' rendered 'in most cases'.","I restated the second sentence of the passage as two. In the Latin term I give the nominative form *sensorium commune*; the text has the form as printed in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-042":["Others, Bonnet in particular, have therefore declared the callous body of the brain, that is, the *corpus callosum*, to be the *sensorium commune*, whither sensation is to penetrate, or whence movement is to issue. If, however, we consider the question metaphysically, whether the soul can perceive itself and determine the place of its abode in the body, the answer must be declared impossible.",["'Gehirnschwiele' rendered literally as 'callous body of the brain', glossed by Kant's own *corpus callosum*.","'wo dahin … oder daraus' is clumsy in the German; I smoothed it to 'whither … or whence'. 'Auflösung' is rendered 'answer' in the sense of solution of the question."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-043":["No place anywhere in space can be assigned to an immaterial being, since what is an object in space must be simply matter, if it is not to lack all relations of local presence. To the soul, therefore, we can ascribe only a dynamical relation to the body, a virtuality. Only by virtue of this *nexus dynamicus* does it stand in connection with the body, and produce the changes of the body through, and in accordance with, the sensation excited. How this comes about cannot be determined. For we can never first think the place from which the soul acts upon the body; we must first think the sensation, and then, along with the place, that which immediately produces the sensation.\n\nIn general, what is object of outer sense cannot be thought in any relation to the object of inner sense. The soul is indeed conscious of the objects of outer sense; but just as little as they are present in the soul, so little does their influence upon the soul determine the place where they are produced. It is as impossible to perceive the soul outwardly as it is to perceive the place of another soul. Equally little can the soul itself determine its place immediately in the body, or immediately in the world; for it cannot become aware of itself as an object of outer sense. To do so it would have to determine outer things in their relation to the soul, and would have to perceive its presence as to place, and thus would have to become aware of itself through its outer sense. But to perceive oneself belongs to inner sense; to perceive oneself through place belongs to outer sense. The soul would therefore have to perceive itself, as object of inner sense, at the same time through outer sense; but the two senses have no relation whatever that would be local.\n\nThe soul, then, cannot become conscious of all this, because an outer perception of itself is lacking to it, and it cannot become aware of itself outside itself, or of its relations to itself. Hence the soul cannot determine its place.\n\nThe question now arises how we are to understand the assertion of an actual perception of the soul. The community of soul and body cannot be thought at all, so long as what is *phaenomenon* is taken in both. For how, in regard to pleasure and displeasure, for instance, or in regard to imagination, can anything bodily be made noticeable? If man is to have representations of outer objects, they are not depicted in him as if he were enclosed in space. He does not know the objects in material figure; that is, outer matter does not flow over into the soul. What flows into the soul is something unknown, which is not appearance; and so we obtain a homogeneity in us with things. Herein lies the representation, which is generated in him not by the *phaenomenon* of the body itself, but by the substratum of matter, the *noumenon* in us. The soul separates this from the object. The *noumenon* in the body stands in agreement with that *noumenon* of the soul, and this unity is the determining ground [*Bestimmungsgrund*] of both for representing the object; on this rests the *commercium corporis et animae*.\n\nThe assumed *influxus physicus* must likewise be explained in this way. First, we must think it as real, that is, as meaning that substances external to one another can influence one another through their existence (and so outside space, for within space the influx raises no difficulty). But to think this influx between soul and body materially, and yet in such a way that each is outside the other and each for itself, is something impossible in itself. And if it is assumed ideally, it would be nothing but the *harmonia praestabilita*, and would no longer be influx. It must therefore be thought as an immaterial effect of the *noumenon* of both. It then means nothing more than that something flows into the soul, and no heterogeneity remains which could occasion doubt here, since nothing further can be said of the nature of this action. We can say only that the soul is in connection with the body, and that where the body is, there is the soul; but within the body no place can be assigned to the soul.",["Restated as several sentences and divided into paragraphs at the natural turns of the argument; the German is one continuous block.","'Er erkennt die Objecte nicht in materieller Figur': *erkennen* here rendered 'know'. 'Vor-Stellungen' printed with a hyphen in the source, rendered 'representations'.","'sie sondert dies vom Object ab': the subject (soul) and the antecedent of 'dies' (the *noumenon* in us) are my judgment; the sentence is obscure and possibly corrupt in the transcript.","'Die Seele kann sich also alles dessen nicht bewußt werden … sich, außer sich, und ihre Verhältniße zu sich selbst': the construction is irregular and I smoothed it. 'Wornach denn dies nichts weiter heißt' also smoothed.","'Er muß … gedacht werden' refers to the influx; 'ideal' contrasted with 'real'. 'Bestimmungs Grund' rendered 'determining ground'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-044":["It is further said against this: the brain feels the thinking. It is true that the nerves are the instrument in which the soul, according to its *deportement*, exercises its action through the stimuli excited, and that, conversely, feelings are conveyed by the nerves to the soul. We may assume virtually that the soul is in the brain, but locally this cannot be inferred from it. *Anima est tota in toto corpore et tota in quavis parte*, the ancients said rather. But to assume that it could be whole and undivided and yet at two particular places is not possible. Otherwise it would also be possible that it could be outside myself, and that I should then regard myself as an object outside me, which is impossible.\n\nWe know merely the effect of the reciprocal influence of soul upon body. For example, we feel a pain whose action comes from the finger, and we ultimately refer it to the finger. But how sensibility and locomotivity are connected in their effects, or what the kind of this action is, cannot be determined, any more than the place of the reacting soul can be determined. The soul has no determinate place or local relation to the body; for this would be a mathematical relation, which we absolutely do not comprehend, whereas we can form a concept only of the dynamical relation of the soul.",["'Man sagt ferner dagegen': 'against this' refers to the preceding denial of a locality of the soul in the brain.","OCR 'redueiren' read as 'reduciren' ('refer'). 'sensibilitaet' and 'locomotivitaet' rendered 'sensibility' and 'locomotivity'.","'deportement' kept in the French-derived form of the source; 'Virtualiter'/'localiter' rendered 'virtually'/'locally'.","'oder die Art der Wirckung zu bestimmen' is syntactically loose; I supplied 'what the kind of this action is'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-045":["§ 83. The object *ad b* of rational psychology is what the soul was before the birth of man, or the question *de origine animae*. Here men divide as follows,",["OCR garbled: 'neralich' read as 'nemlich' (namely) and the stray 'h' after 'Geburt' dropped; 'nemlich' is left untranslated as the sentence is broken. The syntax of 'was neralich die Seele … war' is irregular and I restated it as two sentences.","The passage breaks off; 'Der Gegenstand ad b.' refers to a second head of the topics of rational psychology."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-046":["1.) that one lets it arise *per ortum physicum*, either as educt or as product of an older soul, or",["Breaks off; continues the division begun in the preceding passage. 'educt' and 'product' (*educt*/*product* in the German) kept as the technical terms; 'older' = *altern*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-047":["2) That the soul is represented as created by God at birth. This is entitled the *ortus hyperphysicus*. The latter is the system of creations, the system of creation proper, in which the soul is assumed to be a product of the parents, and a creative power is ascribed to the parents. We ought not to take refuge in this view, however, so long as we can manage with the *ortus physicus*.",["The passage opens with '2.)' because it continues an enumeration begun in the preceding passage; not completed here.","'Das letztere' is awkward: it follows a description of creation by God at birth, yet what is then described (soul as product of the parents, creative power ascribed to them) fits rather a creationist-generationist system. I have translated it as it stands rather than resolve the tension; the transcript may be confused or corrupt here.","Restated as several sentences; 'das System der Schöpfung' rendered 'the system of creation proper' to avoid repetition. 'zu dem' (referring to the view) resolved as 'this view'.","Latin *ortus hyperphysicus* / *ortus physicus* kept; the OCR's 'ortum' and 'orto' are grammatical case forms (accusative, ablative) normalised to the nominative.","'Eltern' is read as 'parents'; 'auslangen' = 'manage with'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-048":["The *ortus physicus*, on the other hand, gives us two systems to recognise, namely:",["'zu erkennen geben' rendered 'gives us to recognise' in a smoothed form; literally 'gives to be known'. The sentence breaks off with 'nemlich' and the colon; the enumeration continues in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-049":["a) The soul is assumed to be an *educt*, that is, the human soul, or the principle that animates the animal, was already present in the body before birth (perhaps as a crude substance that was not yet animate), and was then brought to life through the forces of nature. This is the system of *praeexistentia* [pre-existence], since the soul must already have existed beforehand in the body of the parents; or, so far as it exists freely in the body and is transferred from this into the descendants, or if it lay as a germ of the soul in the soul of the parents and, separated from it as a part of it, issues forth from it. This last is the *systema per traducem*, which however also comprehends the former under itself, since the soul always comes from the body of the parents, into which the germs may also have come from the air and so forth through nourishment.",["'educt' kept as Kant's/transcript's term (Latin *eductum*).","'a praeexistentiae' is evidently an OCR or transcript slip; read as 'praeexistentiae', and rendered 'system of *praeexistentia*'.","'bzw.' is not eighteenth-century usage and is probably an editorial or OCR intrusion; I rendered it as 'or'. The clause 'insofern sie frei ... existire' is loosely constructed; I kept its looseness rather than supply a main verb.","The sentence 'Dies ist das System ...' was split at the semicolon; the sense of the second half (the alternative description of preexistence/traduction) is somewhat unclear in the German.","'Keime' = 'germs'; 'Luft usw.' = 'air and so forth'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-050":["(b) Or the soul is taken to be an *educt*, but in such a way that it is produced by the parents through a mixture with other materials, and so has proceeded out of the parents' souls; in that case parents' souls would give birth to children's souls. This latter view is plainly contrary to the simplicity of the soul as substance, since it would here be presupposed that the souls of the parents must be composite, in order to yield up their children's souls as parts.",["The opening 'sie' has no antecedent in this passage; I take it to be the soul (Seele), as the sequel ('aus der Seele der Eltern') and the argument about simplicity require. Doubtful, as the preceding sentence is not given.","'b.)' is kept as a division marker, rendered '(b)'; the preceding member (a) is presumably in the lost context.","*educt* is left in Latin, as in the German (educt, from *educere*): what is drawn out of something already existing, as opposed to *productum* or creation.","Restated with a semicolon; the German 'wo also Eltern Seelen Kinder Seelen gebähren' is a loose relative clause, which I turned into a consequence. 'Eltern Seelen' and 'Kinder Seelen' are rendered as possessives; 'Seelen Substanz' as 'the soul as substance'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-051":["At bottom these systems, in so far as they have regard to the *ortus physicus* [physical origin], are formed and conceived on the analogy of the animal generation of human beings, where likewise the different systems come down to the following:",["OCR 'ortum physicum' is corrected to *ortus physicus*; the form in the source is a case-slip (accusative after 'auf ... Rücksicht nehmen' would in fact be 'ortum', so I treat the form as the transcript's own and render the sense only). The Latin is bracketed in translation.","The passage breaks off at 'darauf hinausgehen'; the enumeration begins in the next passage, so I end with a colon, supplying only the punctuation, not the content.","'geformt, und gedacht': rendered 'formed and conceived'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-052":["1) To the *systema praeformationis*, according to which, either, as Leeuwenhoek assumed in his *theoria involutionis* (the system of encasement), the germ for all future generations was already laid down at the creation, for every species throughout the whole animal and plant kingdom, in its seed, in such a way that one germ lay within another as dead matter, and only single ones were brought to life. Hence the *animalcula spermatica* of animals. Or, as others assume, Providence created the individual germs beforehand, lying free and side by side, as, for example, when they float in the air or are free in the body, and are *educed* from it [the body].",["'Leeuvenhoek' is Leeuwenhoek; spelling silently corrected. Strictly, encasement (emboîtement) was not his theory, but I render the lecture as it stands.","Restated: the long 'daß' clause is kept but 'wäre ... gelegt' is rendered as indicative past for English readability; the 'wie andere annehmen, daß ...' alternative is split off as a new sentence.","'educirt werden': rendered 'educed', keeping the link with *educt* in the previous passage. 'aus diesem' I take to refer to the body (Körper); doubtful.","'z.E.' (zum Exempel) rendered 'for example'. The sentence about 'wenn sie in der Luft schweben' is obscure in the transcript and may be corrupt or abbreviated; I translate it literally without conjecture.","'Vorsehung' rendered 'Providence'; 'Keim' 'germ'; 'Saamen' 'seed'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-001":["The system of *praeexistentia* [pre-existence] of the free [sort]",["Heading. \"liberae\" is Latin and its noun is not given; the sense is doubtful. I supplied \"[sort]\" in brackets only to make an English reading possible. It may be an OCR or transcription slip (perhaps for *seminum*, or *generalis*?), and the reading should be checked against the Academy text.","*systema praeexistentiae* kept in Latin as a technical term; the English gloss is bracketed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-002":["This system has now been fairly generally rejected. The reason is that, in order to preserve a species, an infinite sum of germs would have to be assumed in each individual, and that with the consumption of a single seed-kernel millions would be lost at once; and such a useless sacrifice would not be in keeping with the purpose of nature.",["Restated one long sentence as two, the second beginning at \"and such a useless sacrifice\"; the German's final clause (\"diese ... Aufopferung aber ...\") is made independent.","\"Zweck der Natur\" rendered \"purpose of nature\"; \"Keimen\" as \"germs\" (Keime); \"Saamenkern\" as \"seed-kernel\".","\"veranstaltet seyn würde\" is rendered loosely as \"would not be in keeping\"; the literal sense is \"would be arranged/contrived\" in accordance with nature's purpose."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-003":["2) Hence the system of *epigenesis* is now fairly generally accepted. According to it the parents are the producing cause of generation, and the young animal accordingly arises as a product from the mixing of both sexes. This is more probable, as is already shown by the pairing of related species, for example ass and horse, or black and white human beings, and by the resemblances of the varieties [Abarten] in mules and mulattoes, and so on; and likewise by the hybrid plants produced through related pollen.",["Divided the sentence at \"nach welchem\" and at \"Dies ist wahrscheinlicher\" for clarity.","\"Maulesel\" translated as \"mules\" (strictly the offspring of ass and mare, i.e. hinnies are \"Maultiere\"); \"Abarten\" given as \"varieties\" with the German bracketed.","\"Bastardtpflanzen\" (OCR/original spelling) read as \"hybrid plants\"; \"Blumenstaub\" as \"pollen\". The final clause (\"und so auch ...\") is loosely attached in the German; I took it as another instance of what \"zeigen\" shows."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-004":["If we now try to apply these theories to the origin of a finite [*unendlichen*] soul, it appears that, if the soul is assumed to be an *educt*, the *systema propagationis per traducem vel praeexistentiae* would have to be the correct one. But we should then also have to presuppose that the souls of the children, and of their descendants, were already present in the bodies of the parents. The souls of the parents would in that case be composite, which is impossible. If, on the other hand, the soul is assumed to be a *product*, after the analogy of *epigenesis*, we should have to concede to the parents a generative power of the soul. But then the soul could not previously have been in the parents' bodies as an *accidens*. For the soul is a substance, and as a simple substance it cannot at the same time have existed as an *accidens*. The parents would therefore have had to perform an act of creation, and generation would consist in this. It would thus be a *productio ex nihilo*, which requires a creative power. Now such a power cannot be thought in any being that is itself created. A created being can indeed alter, separate and combine the form and shape of what has been created, but it cannot bring forth anything new. The capacity for that can belong only to the original being [*Urwesen*] of all creatures.",["*unendlichen* is printed (OCR 'unendliehen'); I have rendered it as 'finite' in brackets, since the context (a human soul, created) seems to demand 'endlichen'. The transcript's reading may be a slip by the student or the editors; flagged as doubtful.","OCR 'mäßen' corrected to 'müssen' (müßten); 'Elternseelen', 'Schöpfung' etc. rendered plainly.","The long second sentence is divided into several shorter ones, and 'dann wären aber ...' is rendered as 'in that case', after Kemp Smith's practice of restating cumbrous sentences.","*Educt* and *product* are kept in Latin as Kant's technical terms; *systema propagationis per traducem vel praeexistentiae*, *epigenesis*, *accidens* and *productio ex nihilo* likewise left untranslated.","The final clause 'dazu kann das Vermögen nur allein ...' is smoothed into 'The capacity for that can belong only to ...'; *Vermögen* = capacity."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-005":["One might therefore conclude that, since the soul can be neither *educt* nor *product*, and consequently neither the system *per traducem* nor that of creation [*creationis*] can hold, the soul as a simple being must be preformed [*praeformirt*]. But strictly we have no proper representation [*Vorstellung*] of how matters stand with the origin of the soul.",["'weder systema per traducem noch creationis' is rendered with the Latin retained; 'systema' is understood with *creationis*.","The repetition 'eigentlich ... eigentliche' is rendered 'strictly ... proper'.","'Bewandtniß' rendered 'how matters stand'; the sentence is divided into two."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-006":["One might also maintain, as a hypothesis, that perhaps the noumenon, the *substratum materiae*, which underlies all appearances [*Erscheinungen*] of matter, besides manifesting itself as body, at the same time also produces another appearance, which we call soul; that is, that this *substratum materiae* at the same time takes the place of the soul, without, to be sure, being able to generate a soul.",["*noumenon* and *substratum materiae* kept in Latin/Greek as technical terms.","'Materialien' in 'Erscheinungen der Materialien' rendered 'matter'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-001":["§ 84. The Unity of the Monads.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-002":["Leibniz, and after him Wolff and others, assumed that all matter is an aggregate of monads, and that these are simple substances in themselves. These propositions have already been denied above, on the ground that it is impossible for matter to consist of simple parts; matter is, rather, divisible *in infinitum*, and so remains matter [however far it is divided].",["The German sentence was divided into two in English, in keeping with the practice of restating cumbrous sentences; 'welche Sätze' is rendered 'These propositions'.","'Leibnitz' and 'Wolf' are given in their modern English spellings (Leibniz, Wolff).","The bracketed words 'however far it is divided' are supplied to complete the sense of 'mithin Materie bleibe'.","'Einfache Substanzen' is rendered 'simple substances'; 'an sich' is rendered 'in themselves', taken to qualify 'simple substances'. The construction is loose and the attachment of 'an sich' is a judgment."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-003":["Externally, now, these unities were supposed to have all the relations which pertain to things in space in their composition. They would therefore at once be composites [*composita*], and as such would have contact, motion, and the like, as composite substances.",["The German sentence was divided into two. 'Sollten' is rendered 'were supposed to', as reporting the Leibnizian-Wolffian claim.","'Verhältniße' is rendered 'relations'; 'componirter Substanzen' is rendered 'composite substances'; 'usw.' is rendered 'and the like'.","The sentence is awkward: 'mithin zugleich composita' has no explicit subject, and I supply 'They would' on the understanding that the 'Einheiten' (the monads) are meant."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-004":["Above all, Leibniz ascribed to the monads a *vis repraesentativa*, and to each monad, as its sole *substantiale*, the power of representation [*Vorstellungskraft*]. In itself this was, so far, a correct inference. We are unable to observe the inner [nature] of other things and can observe only ourselves; and in doing so we become aware of nothing except that we have representations. It was therefore correct to conclude that the inner forces of the monads consist in the power of representation and in the consequences of the representation of pleasure, that is, of desire [*Begehrung*]. Substance and force, however, are not synonymous. Substance only has forces, and it by no means follows necessarily that the monad *quoad substantiam* could have no forces other than the power of representation; we simply do not know them. Leibniz next assumed, on account of the *commercium* of things in the world, that all monads stand in connection with one another, and that each monad is conscious of all the remaining monads in the *universum*. He entitled this the *speculum universi*. He laid it down, however, that although all monads live and have a *vis repraesentativa*, some are nevertheless to be regarded as *sopita*, that is, as slumbering. That is, they have either no actual consciousness of their representations at all, or at least no consciousness of the manifold in things; hence they could form no knowledge [*Erkenntniß*], since they would be unable to separate, combine and compare representations. These he set apart as *bruta*. Herr Kant entitles this division *specula bruta*, in contrast to the intelligent mirrors, *rationalia*. In the latter he now assumed degrees, and a progression of *vires repraesentativae*, namely *obscurae*, *clarae*, *distinctae*, and so on through all degrees up to the highest perfection of God. This is a continuation of the *continuum formarum*, which Bonnet in particular compiled [*aggregirte*] *ratione physicorum*. He passed from the minerals to the sponges, fungi and lichens; from these to the plants; from these to the zoophytes, the animals and man; and finally to God, as who comprehended in himself the origin of all things, and from whom emanation was afterwards assumed. In the same way Leibniz assumed that matter contains in itself the stuff for all souls, and so built up everything, step by step, to the degrees of the Godhead.",["The second sentence ('An sich, da wir …') is syntactically tangled; I restated it as several sentences, taking 'An sich' as 'in itself' and supplying 'nature' in brackets after 'inner' (*das innere*).","'Herr Kant nennt…' is the student's third-person reference to Kant; kept as written.","'veribus' read as *viribus* (*vires*), and 'Claris' as *clarae*; the nominative plural is supplied to agree with *vires*. Original reads *obscuris, Claris, distinctis* (ablative).","'als der allen Ursprung der Dinge in sich faßte' is awkward; I read 'der' as a relative/demonstrative and rendered 'as who comprehended…'. 'woher' construed as 'from whom'.","*Begehrung* is given as 'desire'; 'von Lust = Begehrung' is as in the text (equation sign), rendered 'of pleasure, that is, of desire'.","*aggregirte* is uncertain in sense (Bonnet 'compiled/assembled'); the transcript is possibly corrupt."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-005":["Nothing can be said in support of this hypothesis of Leibniz, just as Bonnet, for that matter, has already been very well refuted by Blumenbach.",["The sentence is loosely constructed ('so wenig … als … vielmehr'); I smoothed it, losing a slight awkwardness in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-006":["§ 85. From what has been said so far, then, we know nothing about the origin of the soul. Likewise, even if we assume the life of the soul to be that of an immaterial substance (and not a predicate of some other matter), and to be created (though not through the generative power of man and his soul), it is still impossible for us to learn the state of life, namely whether it could be an *vita animalis vel spiritualis*. In life we know only that the soul, in thinking, is connected with the body; and if we went out of the body with the soul, it would be just as impossible to learn anything of the state of the thinking being that would then exist, or of how it is now constituted.",["The last clause ('oder wie es jetzt beschaffen ist') is obscure; I translated it literally. 'wie es' may refer to the soul apart from the body.","'vita animalis vel spiritualis' kept in Latin; the 'an' before it in English is a smoothing of 'ob es … seyn könne'.","'wir … gingen' rendered as a conditional ('if we went out'), as the German suggests."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-007":["Yet it does seem possible to maintain that a spiritual life, that is, a living principle without connection with the body, may be assumed. For",["Breaks off at 'Denn'; the argument continues in the following passages. 'anehmen' read as 'annehmen'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-008":["(a) All matter is lifeless. This is a proposition which, though it contradicts the animated monads of Leibniz, must nevertheless be accepted, because to be matter is to be composite, whereas to live is to be the cause of the representation of the actuality of action. Representing requires a thinking principle; a *compositum qua tale*, however, absolutely cannot think, because this manifold of representation must be united in the unity of the subject. If, then, a living principle is to be present for thinking, it must be simple.",["'Die Ursache der Vorstellung von der Wircklichkeit der Handlung' is rendered literally; the sense is uncertain.","Division into shorter sentences is mine; 'Vorstellen erfordert…' onward restated in separate sentences."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-009":["(b) From this one can infer that a separation of the living principle in the subject from matter is possible; that this separation so little hinders life that, since matter is lifeless and can therefore only make thinking by the living principle more difficult and hinder it, thinking is rather made easier and promoted by the separation, and an unhindered life is brought about. According to this, then,",["Breaks off in mid-sentence ('Es würde hiernach also'); continued by (c).","The long second clause was kept as a single sentence because its construction is closely bound; a division would have required supplying more words."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-010":["(c) connection with matter would not be the ground of life, nor necessary to it. On the other hand,",["Continues from (b); 'Dagegen' leads into (d)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-011":["(d) it seems indeed to be opposed to this that the body is an *adminiculum* to thinking, as must be assumed on the evidence of experience. But the question is whether it is absolutely necessary that the body remain in connection with the soul in order to be able to think. It is impossible for us to conceive the effect which each separated substance (body and soul) would have. We assume that in their connection the body is as much an *adminiculum* as a hindrance in respect of thinking. Nevertheless it could still be assumed that, notwithstanding this, in matter",["'Es scheint diesem zwar entgegen zu seyn' is rendered 'it seems indeed to be opposed to this'; the 'scheint' stands in the original after the enumeration sign.","Breaks off before (e); the sentence is completed there ('ein überwiegendes Hinderniß liege')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-012":["(e) there lies a preponderant hindrance, which would fall away after the separation, and the soul would then act so much the more freely. Connection with the body, however, need not be necessary to it, because matter, as remarked, is lifeless, and the *adminiculum*, as well as the hindrance which the body produces in regard to the soul, may be contingent. Yet with certainty nothing can be decided on this question either.",["'dürfte … nicht seyn' rendered 'need not be', a judgment of the modal force of *dürfte*.","'ihr' taken to refer to the soul."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-013":["§ 86. As regards [*ad*] c, the third section of *psychologia rationalis*, namely what the soul will be after death, everything rests on the question whether death (the separation of soul and body) will consist in a dissolution of the whole life of man, that is, of soul and body, or whether the life of man can be continued even after separation from the body. In deciding this, the essential difference between the theories deserves consideration:",["'betritt' read as 'betrifft'; 'ad c' refers to an earlier division of rational psychology (not in the passage).","Ends with a colon; the two theories are given in the next passages."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-014":["(a) *spem vitae futurae concernens* [concerning the hope of a future life]",["Latin phrase kept in italics with English in brackets as a supplied gloss."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-015":["(b) *de immortalitate animae* [on the immortality of the soul].",["English gloss supplied in brackets."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-016":["Both presuppose a future life; only immortality involves the assumption that, from the nature of the soul, it is regarded as necessary that man cannot die at all, and hence that a future life after death is also necessary.",["*Immortalitaet* rendered 'immortality' (the German keeps the Latin form)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-017":["Resurrection is still far more distinct from immortality, as regards its source. For it consists in this, that the substance of man (not the connection of the accidents of the body with the appearance [*Erscheinung*], as with the spiritual breath) does indeed remain even after death, but its animation depends solely on divine free choice [*Willkühr*], and so the body is reawakened with the soul only in consequence of God's decree.",["The parenthesis '(nicht die Verbindung der accidenzien … geistige Hauch)' is obscure and possibly corrupt; rendered as literally as possible.","'Willkühr' rendered 'free choice'; 'Beschluß' as 'decree'; 'ihres Beschlußes' taken to refer to divine will (God), so 'God's' is supplied."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-018":["In relation to the *spes vitae futurae* [hope of a future life], immortality is likewise to be distinguished as something that follows in accordance with the order, that is, in accordance with the laws of the nature of the soul. For we assume a future life on moral grounds, for the sake of universal purposiveness, which has its ground either in universal purposes of nature or in divine purposes. It is in accordance with teleology or theology, respectively, that the future life is considered. Now from the purposiveness in nature we can say as little as from accordance with the divine intention that, because there ought to be a future life, it is a law of nature for the soul to be immortal. For this it would be necessary that the nature of the human soul should make it necessary, since immortality is the impossibility of dying. Only in the nature of the soul can the condition lie of being unable to die, if it is to be necessary to assume a future life. On the other hand, the determination of the divine will to a future life can be regarded only as a supernatural cause of immortality. Consequently the distinction between immortality and resurrection also rests on the difference whether either",["'spei' is read as *spei* (dative, for 'spes') as printed; the Latin phrase is kept in italics with the English in brackets.","Restated as several sentences. In the long sentence beginning 'Nun kann man...' the construction is irregular; I resolved it as 'because there ought to be a future life, it does not follow that it is a law of nature'.","'=' rendered 'that is'. 'Bestimmung' rendered 'determination'. The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('entweder'); not completed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-019":["(a) that the life of the soul after death is thought and assumed in accordance with natural causes, that is, in accordance with the constitution of its nature, or from physiological grounds, or",["Breaks off with 'oder'; continued in the next passage. 'Physiologisch' kept as 'physiological' (in Kant's older sense, pertaining to the knowledge of nature)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-020":["(b) that it is assumed from supernatural, that is, hyperphysical causes. In the first case we have immortality, in the latter resurrection. If, on the other hand, we derive it from the purposiveness in nature, or from the moral purpose of the highest being, we cannot infer any necessity of the future life, but only that we have cause, on moral grounds, to expect a future life. This *spes vitae futurae* differs as greatly as possible [*quam maxime differt*] from the *immortalitas animae*.",["'different' in the OCR text is probably an error for *differt*; I translate it as the verb, and the remaining Latin ('immortalitate animae', ablative) is given in its nominative sense for readability.","'Noth wendigkeit' is a broken word, corrected to 'Nothwendigkeit'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-021":["§ 87. If we are to be able to assume a life of the soul after death, this requires",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-022":["1.) Continued duration of its substance after death.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-023":["2.) Continued duration of its person, that is, identity of its personality, that is, that in the state after death it should be conscious of being the very same subject that it was before.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-024":["N.B. Consciousness of oneself is *memoria intellectualis* in the psychological sense (*in sensu psychologico*); [it is] another [thing] in the moral sense (*in sensu morali*), where it consists in the ground of imputation, or of accountability.",["The German is elliptical and probably corrupt ('Ist das Bewußtseyn... anderer in sensu morali'); the bracketed 'it is' and 'thing' are supplied to complete the sense. 'Zurechnung' rendered 'imputation'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-025":["Both must be proved from physiological grounds.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-026":["(a) As to the first point, the proof for it is usually put as follows. The soul is simple, therefore indivisible; or, what is the same, *anima est incorruptibilis*. It is subject to no decay, that is, to no dispersal through dissolution of its parts, and therefore it cannot perish. This is an inference drawn by analogy with the body. Wood, for instance, does decay; yet it is also true that various constituents of the wood remain which are themselves not chemically soluble, but which nevertheless make the division possible. The soul, however, being an incorporeal substance and therefore simple, is on this presupposition not subject to corruption. Mendelssohn (in his *Phaedo*) quite rightly recognised this proof to be insufficient, since there was no necessity in assuming that, because the soul is not *corruptibilis*, it cannot perish; for it might also perish without division, by vanishing. On the other hand, in assuming that its substance is imperishable, he nevertheless inferred too much. His reasoning was this: if the soul were not imperishable, it would exist at one point of time and no longer exist at the next. But between two instants there must absolutely be a time, through which the one can pass over into the other. If, then, the soul is to exist no longer in the next instant, a being and a not-being would have to follow one another without an intermediate time being determined, a time of a not-wholly being and a not-completely not-being. The transition would thus be thought as occurring through a leap. But a simple substance cannot be changed into nothing, or annihilated, through a leap; and therefore, he concluded, the soul is immortal.",["Restated as several sentences, as the method allows. The German 'zu •viel' (OCR) read as 'zu viel'.","'Mithin' variously 'therefore'/'thus'. 'Verwesung' rendered 'decay', 'Zertheilung' 'division'/'dispersal'; *corruptibilis* and *incorruptibilis* kept in Latin. 'Phaedon' rendered as the title *Phaedo*.","In 'wäre sie es nicht' I supplied 'imperishable' ('unvergänglich') for 'es'; the Mendelssohn argument is in indirect speech (subjunctive) and is rendered accordingly."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-027":["N.B. He presupposed that a simple being cannot, like a body, perish through successive diminution of its parts, but must either exist wholly or cease at once to have existed at all.",["'auf hören' (OCR) read as 'aufhören'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-028":["It is correct that there is no time [between two instants] between which there is not again an infinite series of times, and no state between which and another there would not again be a state through which the transition occurs. But from this we cannot declare it impossible that the soul may perish after death. Since the soul is simple, its parts cannot indeed perish through division, but they can perish through an *evanescentia*, that is, a gradual remission of its forces (the opposite of which is intension of the forces). Just as the clearness of a representation can gradually grow dark, so that at last the soul falls asleep over it and consciousness of it is lost little by little, so all the degrees of the forces of the human soul can gradually diminish, and when they have been reduced through all degrees they may at last pass over into nothing. There is here no *saltus* [leap], but the soul obeys the laws of continuity, descending through ever smaller degrees, between which there is always again a time.",["The German says 'nicht vergehen könne' ('cannot perish') after 'unmöglich erklären'; by the sense of the argument I render 'may perish', treating 'nicht' as an error or slip in the transcript.","'Theile der Seele... bey ihr' is awkward (Kant presumably means that the soul, being simple, has no parts to divide); translated as it stands. 'Evanescenz' given as *evanescentia* in the Latin form; the bracketed '[between two instants]' is supplied.","'Kräfte' rendered 'forces' (Kemp Smith would choose 'powers' or 'forces' by context); 'Vorstellung' = representation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-029":["Of a corporeal thing it is indeed rightly said in the cosmological sense (*in sensu cosmologico*) that the substance is persistent while its accidents change. But it is only in corporeal things that the persistence of substance can be observed, for instance in weight, impenetrability, and the like. In the case of a simple being, however, we are unable to know any persistence, for even the I, that is, the self-consciousness of man, vanishes. The principle of persistence can therefore be applied to a body but not to a simple being; and in the case of the soul, where everything is in flux and everything can pass away, nothing can be inferred from it.",["The last sentence is irregular in the German ('des princip' for 'das Princip'; 'daraus etwas schließen' without a negative); I supply 'nothing' on the evident sense of the argument.","'Accidenzien' rendered 'accidents'; 'Ich = Selbstbewußtseyn' rendered 'the I, that is, self-consciousness'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-030":["As to the second point, the identity of the person of the soul, this would be the *memoria intellectualis*. In so far as this is to belong to the soul after death, we cannot at all see the necessity of it. We may well assume the possibility, but cannot prove it, and so nothing can be inferred about it *a priori*. On the contrary, we find psychologically that man forgets what he was before.",["'betritt' read as 'betrifft'; 'intellectuaiis' read as *intellectualis* (OCR)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-031":["To found the state of the soul after death, and its existence in general, upon psychology, since this is taken from the life of man in experience, is therefore not feasible. It is also clear of itself that from the commerce of the soul with the body, from the consciousness of both in their connection, and given the impossibility of an experiment of even a momentary separation with continuance of consciousness, nothing whatever can be known as to what our soul will be after this connection is dissolved. Besides the psychological proof from the nature of the soul, a further proof *a priori*, and indeed a metaphysical one, has been drawn, (b) from the concept of a principle of life in general.",["'psycho -logischen' corrected; 'b.,' stands in the text before 'dem Begriff' and is probably the heading of a second ground of proof: rendered '(b)' and left in the sentence.","'commercio' (Latin, dative/ablative in the German) rendered 'commerce', in the older sense of reciprocal interaction. 'erkennen' = know."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-032":["It is supposed to have been demonstrated that our life (*quoad principium* [as regards its principle]) does not depend upon a connection of the soul with a matter, that it therefore exists without it, and that death must be regarded as a continuation of life. For, it is said, since matter is lifeless, there is no ground for assuming that life could depend upon a connection with it, that is, that matter could be a ground of the possibility of the principle of life. On the contrary, connection with matter may rather create a hindrance to the latter in life, inasmuch as the soul is compelled, besides its own life, to animate matter also, or to produce an animal life.",["The first sentence is in indirect speech ('Es sey dargethan'); I render it with 'is supposed to have been', and keep the indirect form in the following sentence. 'dem letztem' = the principle of life (the soul), judged from the sense.","Breaks off at the end of the passage's argument; 'Grund der Möglichkeit' rendered 'ground of the possibility'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-033":["But we must distinguish the continuance of the principle of life, or of the capacity [Vermögen] to live, from the *actus* of life itself. The former may possibly continue, even more freely and unhindered, after the separation from the body. But to assume the actuality of life itself, we require an experience whether the human being, or his soul, can exercise *actus* of life without connection with the body. Now the experience which we have of our state teaches us, on the contrary, that we cannot think, will, desire, and so forth, otherwise than in connection with the body. The co-operation of the body shows itself visibly in sensation [Empfindung] as a consequence of these actions of life; for example, whenever we expend our powers, in thinking we feel fatigue, and so on. This co-operation therefore seems to betray a condition which underlies the exercise of life. It is accordingly not proved that the soul lives *actu* after death, even if the principle of life continues after death. We see, then, that even metaphysically nothing regarding immortality (*immortalitas*) can be proved from the nature of the soul, and consequently that all physiological arguments on this matter prove nothing.\n\nBesides this, the proof of the immortality of the soul has been sought in the order of ends, or taken from purposiveness [Zweckmäßigkeit]. It is derived either (a) from the ends of nature according to laws of nature; this is the teleological proof; or (b) from the divine end according to moral laws (regarded as divine); this is the theological proof. The teleological proof (NB: it is still the only one that grounds a possibility of a future life and deserves approval from the metaphysical point of view) is therefore taken as follows:",["Restated the long sentence on the experience of our state as several shorter sentences; the German 'indem … z.E.' clause is smoothed into separate statements, with 'Aufopferung der Kräfte' rendered 'expend our powers'.","'actu', 'actus' kept in Latin in italics; 'immortaiitaet' is an OCR slip for *immortalitas*, silently corrected.","'Vermögen' rendered 'capacity' here (capacity to live); 'Empfindung' = sensation.","'Den teleologischen Beweis … nimmt man daher:' breaks off; the continuation is in the next passage. 'Daher' rendered 'therefore'; the sense may be 'accordingly'.","'Zweck' rendered 'end', 'Zweckmäßigkeit' 'purposiveness'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-034":["(a) There is no organic being in nature whose existence can be thought possible otherwise than through, and by virtue of, an end to be effected by it. With this we must absolutely and at once connect the idea of a being which has created the organic creature for some end. One may take into consideration, for example, the circulation of the sap in trees, shrubs and plants, and the connection of the parts of flowers with a view to the manner of their fertilization, and so forth.",["Restated the first sentence in a smoother form; 'Man nehme z.E. auf … Rücksicht' rendered 'One may take into consideration, for example'.","'Umtrieb der Säfte' rendered 'circulation of the sap'; 'Stauden' as 'shrubs' is a choice (perennial plants is also possible)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-035":["(b) There are in nature no superfluous dispositions [Anlagen]. In the case of minerals, purposiveness is indeed not yet (breaks off)",["The text breaks off in the transcript ('bricht ab' is the editors' remark); translated as it stands and not completed.","'Anlagen' rendered 'dispositions'; 'capacities' is possible."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-036":["[…] to make possible the communication of a proportionate happiness [Glückseligkeit] to the whole human race, and to bring the well-being of men into agreement with their well-conduct. How God brings this about, however, we do not know; we adopt this presupposition only because, on the one hand, moral well-conduct is made a duty for the human race, while it lies beyond its powers to procure happiness for itself. It is indeed already evident, according to laws of nature, that observance of the moral laws in our conduct brings contentment with ourselves, as well as the approval of others; but of itself it does not afford satisfaction in respect of what is sensibly good.\n\nFrom this one sees very easily how greatly the assumption of the divine unconditioned decrees, or of the *decretum divinum absolutum*, is opposed to all concepts. This is an *actus voluntatis consequentis Dei*. By virtue of it God, through the antecedent will (*per voluntatem antecedentem*), that is, in the case where the impulsive cause was general and common to all subjects of the human race, wished all men to be saved; but through the consequent will (*per voluntatem consequentem*) he determined, at the creation of the world, one class for blessedness and the other for damnation, and accordingly granted to the former the means of grace which it needed, but withheld them from the latter altogether. Hence, since man is by nature evil, some are absolutely to expect blessedness, while others are, equally absolutely, to be subjected to damnation. This is the doctrine of predestination (*praedestinatio*). From it the hypothetical *decretum divinum* is entirely distinguished in this respect, that according to the latter blessedness or damnation is predetermined by God only after the subject has made himself worthy or unworthy of it through his moral conduct.",["The passage begins mid-sentence; the opening infinitive phrase has no governing verb in what is given, so '[…]' marks the gap and nothing is supplied.","The sentence 'Es ist dies … Seligkeit' was divided into several sentences; the text is awkward and partly garbled by OCR.","'unbedingtenRathschlüße' read as 'unbedingten Rathschlüße'; 'decreti divini absolut i' read as *decreti divini absoluti*; rendered with the nominative *decretum divinum absolutum*.","'casu causa impulsiva erat generalis atqui omnibus subjectis generis humani communis' is probably garbled (perhaps 'quo casu'); I translate 'in the case where…' tentatively and keep the Latin sense as it stands.","'Verdammmß' read as 'Verdammnis'; 'unterscheidt' as 'unterscheidet'; 'der erstem/letztem' as 'der ersteren/letzteren'; 'Seligkeit' = blessedness, to distinguish it from 'Glückseligkeit' = happiness.","'Wohlbefinden' = well-being, 'Wohlverhalten' = well-conduct (a literal choice, to keep the contrast)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-037":["[…]finitum — 20th Feb. 95.",["A fragment: probably the end of a word (e.g. *in*finitum, or similar) followed by a date, presumably the transcriber's note; the initial letters are not in the passage, so nothing is supplied. '95' is presumably 1795."]]};
